(technical language has been avoided so far as the requirements of) Tj T*
(scientific accuracy will permit, the student is not encouraged) Tj T*
(to shirk the use of necessary botanical terms, out of a mere) Tj T*
(superstitious fear of words because they happen to be a little new or) Tj T*
(unfamiliar. Such a practice not only leads to careless and inaccurate) Tj T*
(modes of expression, but tends to foster a slovenly habit of mind,) Tj T*
(and in the long run causes the waste of more time and labor in the) Tj T*
(search after roundabout, and often misleading, substitutes, than it) Tj T*
(would require to master the proper use of a few new words and phrases.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the choice of materials for experiment and illustration, the) Tj T*
(endeavor has been to call for such only as are familiar and easily) Tj T*
(obtained. The specimens for flower dissection have been selected) Tj T*
(mainly from common cultivated kinds, because their wide distribution) Tj T*
(makes them easy to obtain everywhere, while in cities and large) Tj T*
(towns they are practically the only specimens available. Another) Tj T*
(important consideration has been the desire to spare our native) Tj T*
(wild flowers, or at least not to hasten the extinction with which) Tj T*
(they are threatened by the ravages of Sunday excursionists and) Tj T*
(summer tourists, to whose unthinking, but none the less destructive,) Tj T*
(incursions, the automobile has laid open the most secret haunts) Tj T*
(of nature. The influence of the public school teacher, and more) Tj T*
(especially the teacher of botany, is the most potent factor from) Tj T*
(which we can hope for aid in putting a stop to the relentless) Tj T*
(persecution that has practically exterminated many of our choicest) Tj T*
(wild plants and is fast reducing the civilized world to a depressing) Tj T*
(monotony of weediness and artificiality. Except for purely systematic) Tj T*
(and anatomical work, flowers can be studied to better purpose in) Tj T*
(their living, active state than as dead subjects for dissection;) Tj T*
(and the best way to show our interest in them, or to get the most) Tj T*
(rational enjoyment out of them, is not, as a general thing, to) Tj T*
(cut their heads off and throw them away to wither and die by the) Tj T*
(roadside. The teacher, by instilling into the minds of the rising) Tj T*
(generation a reverence for plant life, may do a great deal to aid) Tj T*
(in the conservation of one of our chief national assets for the) Tj T*
(gratification of the higher esthetic instincts. The fruits and) Tj T*
(flowers of cultivation do not stand in the same need of protection,) Tj T*
(since they are produced solely with a view to the use and pleasure of) Tj T*
(man, and their propagation is provided for to meet all his demands.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To avoid too frequent interruptions of the subject matter, the) Tj T*
(experiments are grouped together at the beginning or end of the) Tj T*
(sections to which they belong, according as they are intended to) Tj T*
(explain what is coming, or to illustrate what has gone before. A few) Tj T*
(exceptions are made in cases where the experiment is such an integral) Tj T*
(part of the subject that it would be meaningless if separated from) Tj T*
(the context. Under no circumstances should those capable of being) Tj T*
(performed in the schoolroom be omitted, as much of the information) Tj T*
(which the book is intended to give is conveyed by their means. For) Tj T*
(this reason, and also because the aim of the book is to present the) Tj T*
(science from a practical rather than from an academic point of view,) Tj T*
(the experiments outlined are for the most part of a simple, practical) Tj T*
ET
endstream
