(  MATERIAL.?In addition to the four food tests described in Exps.) Tj T*
(  1-6, there should be provided some raw starch, a solution of grape) Tj T*
(  sugar, the white of a hard-boiled egg, and any fatty substance,) Tj T*
(  such as lard or oil. For Exps. 8 and 9, a little diastase solution) Tj T*
(  will be necessary. ?Taka? diastase, made from rice acted upon by a) Tj T*
(  fungus, can be obtained for a trifle at almost any drug store.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  LIVING MATERIAL.?Grains of corn and wheat, and seeds of some kind) Tj T*
(  of bean, the larger the better. The ?horse bean? \(_Vicia faba_\),) Tj T*
(  if it can be obtained, makes an excellent object for study, as the) Tj T*
(  cells are so large that they can be seen with the naked eye. For) Tj T*
(  showing the presence of proteins \(aleurone grains\) and oily matter,) Tj T*
(  use thin cross sections through the kernel of a castor bean or a) Tj T*
(  Brazil nut. Specimens for the study of the individual cell will be) Tj T*
(  found in the hairs growing on squash seedlings, in the epidermis of) Tj T*
(  one of the inner coats of an onion, in the roots of oat or radish) Tj T*
(  seedlings, or in the section of a young corn root.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  A compound microscope will be required for this study.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
