(=1. The economic importance of seeds.=?As a source of food to both) Tj T*
(man and the lower animals, the importance of seeds can hardly be) Tj T*
(overrated. All the flour, meal, rice, hominy, and other breadstuffs) Tj T*
(sold in the market come from them, to say nothing of the fleece from) Tj T*
(the cotton seed that clothes the greater part of the world, besides) Tj T*
(furnishing a substitute for lard and an important food for cattle.) Tj T*
(The oils and fats stored in nuts are also to be taken into account,) Tj T*
(the peanut alone yielding the greater part of the so-called olive) Tj T*
(oil of commerce. Since the value of our farm crops depends largely) Tj T*
(upon the kind and quantity of these substances furnished by them, it) Tj T*
(is worth our while, as a matter of economic as well as scientific) Tj T*
(interest, to learn something about the nature of the different foods) Tj T*
(contained in plants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 1-3.?The world?s three most important food) Tj T*
(grains \(magnified\): 1, section of a rice grain; _a_, cuticle; _b_,) Tj T*
(aleurone, or protein layer; _c_, starch cells; _d_, germ; 2, section) Tj T*
(of a wheat grain; _k_, germ; _s_, starch; _a_, gluten; _t_, _t_,) Tj T*
(_t_, layers of the seed coat; 3, section of a grain of corn; _c_,) Tj T*
(husk; _e_, aleurone layer containing proteins; _eg_, yellowish,) Tj T*
(horny endosperm, containing proteins and starch; _ew_, lighter) Tj T*
(starchy endosperm: the darker part below is rich in oil and proteins,) Tj T*
(and contains the _embryo_, consisting of the absorbing organ, or) Tj T*
(_cotyledon_, _sc_; the rudimentary bud, _s_; and the root, _w_. \(1,) Tj T*
(from Circular 77, La. Exp. Station; 2, from Francé; 3, from Sachs.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=2. Why food is stored in seeds.=?The one purpose for which plants) Tj T*
(produce their seed is to give rise to a new generation and so carry) Tj T*
(on the life of the species. The seed is the nursery, so to speak, in) Tj T*
(which the germ destined to produce a new plant is sheltered until) Tj T*
(it is ready to begin an independent existence. But the young plant,) Tj T*
(like the young animal, is incapable of providing for itself at first,) Tj T*
(and would die unless it received nourishment from the mother plant) Tj T*
(until it has formed roots and leaves so that it can manufacture food) Tj T*
(for itself. Plants in general require very much the same food that) Tj T*
(animals do, and they have the power, which animals have not, of) Tj T*
(manufacturing it out of the crude materials contained in the soil) Tj T*
(water and in the air. Such of these foods as are not needed for) Tj T*
(immediate consumption, they store up to serve as a provision for the) Tj T*
(young shoot when the seed begins to germinate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 4-7.?Sections of corn grains showing different) Tj T*
(qualities of food contents: 4, 5, small germ and large proportion) Tj T*
(of horny part, showing high protein; 6, 7, large germ and smaller) Tj T*
(proportion of horny part, showing high oil content.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=3. Food substances contained in seeds.=?There are four principal) Tj T*
(classes of food stored in seeds: _sugars_, _starches_, _oils_, and) Tj T*
(_proteins_. The first are held in solution and can be detected, if) Tj T*
(in sufficient quantity, by the taste. The most important varieties) Tj T*
(of this group are cane and grape sugar, the latter occurring most) Tj T*
(abundantly in fruits, the former in roots and stems. Oil usually) Tj T*
