(occurs in the form of globules. It is very abundant in some seeds,) Tj T*
(_e.g._ flax, castor bean, and Brazil nut. In the corn grain it is) Tj T*
(found in the part constituting the germ, or embryo \(Figs. 6, 7\).) Tj T*
(Starches and proteins occur in the form of small granules, which have) Tj T*
(specific shapes in different plants \(Figs. 8, 9\). Those containing) Tj T*
(proteins are called _aleurone_ grains, and are, as a rule, smaller) Tj T*
(than the starch grains with which they are intermixed in the bean) Tj T*
(and some other seeds. In wheat, corn, rice, and most grains they) Tj T*
(form a layer just inside the husk, as shown in Fig. 10. This is the) Tj T*
(reason why polished rice and finely bolted flour are less nutritious) Tj T*
(than the darker kinds, from which this valuable food substance has) Tj T*
(not been removed. The two most familiar kinds of proteins are the) Tj T*
(_albumins_, of which the white of an egg is a well-known example, and) Tj T*
(the _glutins_, which give to the dough of wheat flour and oatmeal) Tj T*
(their peculiar gummy or ?glutinous? structure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 8-9.?Different forms of starch grains: 8, rice;) Tj T*
(9, wheat.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=4. Organic foods.=?These four substances, starch, sugar, fats, and) Tj T*
(proteins, with some others of less frequent occurrence, are called) Tj T*
(_organic foods_, because they are produced, in a state of nature,) Tj T*
(only through the action of organized living bodies, or, more strictly) Tj T*
(speaking, of living vegetable bodies.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 10.?Transverse section near the outside of a) Tj T*
(wheat grain: _e_, the husk; _a_, cells containing protein granules;) Tj T*
(_s_, starch cells \(_after_ Tschirch\).]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=5. Our dependence upon plants.=?While the animal organism can digest) Tj T*
(and assimilate these substances after they have been formed by) Tj T*
(plants, it has no power to manufacture them for itself, and, so far) Tj T*
(as we know at present, is wholly dependent upon the vegetable world) Tj T*
(for these necessaries of life. In one sense the whole animal kingdom) Tj T*
(may be said to be parasitic on plants. The wolf that eats a lamb is) Tj T*
(getting his food indirectly from the grains and grasses consumed by) Tj T*
(its victim, and the lion that devours the wolf that ate the lamb is) Tj T*
(only one step further removed from a vegetable diet.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=6. The vegetable cell.=?If you will break open a well-soaked horse) Tj T*
(bean and examine the contents with a lens, you will see that they) Tj T*
(are composed of small oval or roundish granules packed together) Tj T*
(like stones in a piece of masonry. These little bodies, called) Tj T*
(_cells_, are the ultimate units out of which all animal and vegetable) Tj T*
(structures are built up, as a wall is built of bricks and stones.) Tj T*
(They differ very much from bricks and stones, however, in that they) Tj T*
(are, or have been, living structures with their periods of growth,) Tj T*
(activity, decline, and death, just like other living matter, as) Tj T*
(will be seen by and by, when we come to look more particularly into) Tj T*
(their life history. They consist usually of an inclosing membrane) Tj T*
(which contains a living substance called _protoplasm_. This is the) Tj T*
(essential part of the cell, and, so far as we know at present, the) Tj T*
