(=7. Microscopic examination of a cell.=?Place under a high power) Tj T*
(of the microscope a portion of fresh skin from one of the inside) Tj T*
(scales of an onion, or a piece of the root tip of a very young corn) Tj T*
(or oat seedling, and fix your attention on one of the individual) Tj T*
(cells. Notice \(1\) the cell wall or inclosing membrane, _w_ \(Fig. 11\);) Tj T*
(\(2\) the protoplasm, _p_, which may be recognized by its granular) Tj T*
(appearance; \(3\) the _nucleus_, _n_; and \(4\) the cell sap, _s_. In) Tj T*
(very young cells the protoplasm will be seen to fill most of the) Tj T*
(interior; but in mature ones, like the large one on the right of the) Tj T*
(figure, it forms a thin lining around the wall, with the nucleus) Tj T*
(on one side, while the cell sap, composed of various substances in) Tj T*
(solution, occupies the central portion. Though there is generally) Tj T*
(an inclosing wall, this is not essential, its office being to give) Tj T*
(strength and mechanical support by holding the contents together, as) Tj T*
(an India-rubber bag holds water. It is the turgidity of the cell,) Tj T*
(when distended with liquid, that gives firmness to herbaceous plants) Tj T*
(and the tender parts of woody ones. This may be illustrated by) Tj T*
(observing the difference between a rubber bag when quite full and) Tj T*
(when only half full of water, or a football when partially and when) Tj T*
(fully inflated. In its simplest form, however, the cell is a mere) Tj T*
(particle of protoplasm, which has one part, constituting the nucleus,) Tj T*
(a little more dense in appearance than the rest, but this kind is not) Tj T*
(common in vegetable structures.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=8. How food substances get into the cells.=?As there are no openings) Tj T*
(in the cell walls, the only way substances can get into a cell or) Tj T*
(out of it is by soaking through the inclosing membrane, as will be) Tj T*
(explained in a later chapter. Since starch, oil, and proteins, the) Tj T*
(most important foods stored in seeds, are none of them soluble in the) Tj T*
(cell sap, it is clear that they could not have got into the cells in) Tj T*
(their present state, but must have undergone some change by which) Tj T*
(they were rendered capable of passing through the cell wall.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=9. Digestion.=?The process by which this change is brought about) Tj T*
(is known as _digestion_, from its similarity to the same function) Tj T*
(in animals. Not only are foods, in the state in which we find them) Tj T*
(stored in the seed, incapable of passing through the cell wall,) Tj T*
(but the protoplasm, the living part of the cell, has no power to) Tj T*
