(During the process of digestion the different kinds of food are) Tj T*
(acted upon and made soluble by certain chemical ferments, which are) Tj T*
(secreted in plants for the purpose. The digestion of starch, the most) Tj T*
(abundant of plant foods, is effected by diastase, a common ferment) Tj T*
(obtained from germinating grains of barley, wheat, corn, rice, etc.) Tj T*
(By the presence of diastase starch is converted into grape sugar,) Tj T*
(a substance which is readily soluble in water, and which can be) Tj T*
(diffused easily through the tissues of the plant to any part where it) Tj T*
(is needed. In this way food travels from the leaf, where it is made,) Tj T*
(to the seed, where the sugar is generally reconverted into starch and) Tj T*
(stored up for future use, though sometimes, as in the sugar corn and) Tj T*
(sugar pea, it remains in part unchanged. The kernels of this kind of) Tj T*
(corn can be distinguished readily from those of the ordinary starch) Tj T*
(corn, after maturity, by their wrinkled appearance, owing to their) Tj T*
(greater loss of water in drying.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
