(          IV. SEED DISPERSAL) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Fruits and seeds of any kind that show adaptations for) Tj T*
(  dispersal. Some common examples are: \(1\) Wind: ash, elm, maple,) Tj T*
(  ailanthus, milkweed, clematis, sycamore, linden, dandelion,) Tj T*
(  thistle, hawkweed. \(2\) Water: pecan, filbert, cranberry, lotus,) Tj T*
(  hickory nut, coconut?obtain one with the husk on, if possible. \(3\)) Tj T*
(  Animal agency \(involuntary\): cocklebur, tickseed, beggar-ticks,) Tj T*
(  burdock; \(voluntary\) almost all kinds of edible fruits, especially) Tj T*
(  the bright-colored ones?wild plums, cherries, haws, dogwood,) Tj T*
(  persimmons, etc. \(4\) Explosive and self-planting: witch-hazel,) Tj T*
(  wood sorrel, violet, crane?s-bill, wild vetch, peanut, medick,) Tj T*
(  stork?s-bill \(Erodium\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 17. TO SHOW HOW SEEDS ARE DISPERSED BY WIND.?Take a) Tj T*
(  number of winged and plumed fruits and seeds, such as those of the) Tj T*
(  maple, ash, ailanthus, dandelion, clematis, milkweed, and trumpet) Tj T*
(  creeper; stand on a chair or table in a place where there is a) Tj T*
(  draft of air and let them all go. Which travel the farther, the) Tj T*
(  winged or the plumed kinds? Which sort is better fitted to aërial) Tj T*
(  transportation?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 18. DISPERSAL BY WATER.?Place in a bucket of water a) Tj T*
(  hazelnut, an acorn, an orange, a cranberry, a pecan, a hickory nut,) Tj T*
(  a fresh apple, and a coconut with the husk on. Which are the best) Tj T*
(  floaters? Cut open or break open the good swimmers, compare with) Tj T*
(  the non-floaters, and see to what peculiarity of structure their) Tj T*
(  floating qualities are due. In what situations do the cranberry and) Tj T*
(  the coconut grow? Can you see any advantage to a plant so situated) Tj T*
(  in producing fruits that float easily?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 19. DISPERSAL BY EXPLOSIVE CAPSULES.?Moisten slightly) Tj T*
(  some mature but unopened capsules of witch hazel, wood sorrel,) Tj T*
(  rabbit pea, or violet, and leave in a warm, dry place for fifteen) Tj T*
(  to forty-five minutes. What happens when the pods begin to dry?) Tj T*
(  Measure the distance to which the different kinds of seeds have) Tj T*
(  been ejected. Which were thrown farthest? What was the object of) Tj T*
(  the movement? What caused the explosion?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 20. THE USE OF ADHESIVE FRUITS.?Scatter broadcast) Tj T*
(  a handful of hooked or prickly seeds or fruits?cocklebur,) Tj T*
(  tickseed, beggar-ticks, bur grass, etc. Are they suited for wind) Tj T*
(  transportation? Drop one of them on your sleeve, or on the coat) Tj T*
(  of a fellow student; will it stay there? What would be the effect) Tj T*
(  if it became attached to the fur of a roaming animal? Is this a) Tj T*
(  successful mode of dissemination?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
