(and bony, and either too small to be destroyed by chewing, and) Tj T*
(thus capable of passing uninjured through the digestive system of) Tj T*
(an animal; or, if too large to be swallowed whole, compelling the) Tj T*
(animal, by their hardness or disagreeable flavor, to reject them.) Tj T*
(In cases where the seeds themselves are edible and attractive, the) Tj T*
(fruits are usually armed during the growing season with protective) Tj T*
(coverings, like the bur of the chestnut and the astringent hulls) Tj T*
(of the hickory nut and walnut. The acidity or other disagreeable) Tj T*
(qualities of most unripe fruits serves a similar purpose, while their) Tj T*
(green color, by making them inconspicuous among the foliage leaves,) Tj T*
(tends still further to insure them against molestation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 40-42.?Adhesive fruits: 40, fruit of) Tj T*
(hound?s-tongue; 41, akene of bur marigold; 42, fruit of bur grass) Tj T*
(\(cenchrus\).]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=22. Voluntary agency.=?The cultivated fruits and grains owe their) Tj T*
(distribution and survival almost entirely to the voluntary agency) Tj T*
(of man. Dispersal by this means, whether intentional or accidental,) Tj T*
(is purely artificial, and except in the case of a few annuals like) Tj T*
(horseweed, bitterweed, ragweed, goosefoot, and other field pests that) Tj T*
(have adjusted their season of growth and flowering to the conditions) Tj T*
(of cultivation, is not correlated with any special modification of) Tj T*
(the plants for self-propagation. On the contrary, many of the most) Tj T*
(widely distributed weeds of cultivation, such as the oxeye daisy, the) Tj T*
(rib grass, mayweed and bitterweed, possess very imperfect natural) Tj T*
(means of dispersal, and are largely dependent for their propagation) Tj T*
(on the involuntary agency of man.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=23. Use of the fruit in dispersal.=?It will be seen from the) Tj T*
(foregoing observations that the fruit plays a very important part in) Tj T*
(the work of dispersal, most of the adaptations for this purpose being) Tj T*
(connected with it. In cases where a number of seeds are contained in) Tj T*
(a large pod that could not conveniently be blown about by the breeze,) Tj T*
(adaptations for wind dispersal are attached to the individual seeds,) Tj T*
(as in the willow, milkweed, trumpet creeper, and paulonia; but as a) Tj T*
(general thing, adaptations of the seed are for protection, the work) Tj T*
(of dispersal being provided for by the fruit. In the case of the) Tj T*
(large class of plants known as ?tumbleweeds,? the whole plant body is) Tj T*
(fitted to assist in the work of transportation. Such plants generally) Tj T*
(grow in light soils and either have very light root systems, or are) Tj T*
(easily broken from their anchorage and left to drift about on the) Tj T*
(ground. The spreading, bushy tops become very light after fruiting,) Tj T*
(so as to be easily blown about by the wind, dropping their seeds) Tj T*
(as they go, until they finally get stranded in ditches and fence) Tj T*
(corners, where they often accumulate in great numbers during the) Tj T*
(autumn and winter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 43.?A fruiting plant of winged pigweed) Tj T*
(\(_Cycloloma_\), showing the bunchy top and weak anchorage of a typical) Tj T*
(tumbleweed.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
