() Tj T*
(=24. The advantages of dispersal.=?Seed cannot germinate unless) Tj T*
(they are placed in a suitable location as to soil, moisture, and) Tj T*
(temperature. In order to increase the chances of securing these) Tj T*
(conditions, it is clearly to the advantage of a species that its) Tj T*
(seeds should be dispersed as widely as possible, both that the) Tj T*
(seedlings may have plenty of room, and that they may not have to draw) Tj T*
(their nourishment from soil already exhausted by their parents. The) Tj T*
(farmer recognizes this principle in the rotation of crops, because) Tj T*
(he knows that successive growths of the same plant will soon exhaust) Tj T*
(the soil of the substances required for its nutrition, while they may) Tj T*
(leave it richer in nourishment for a different crop.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 45.?Self-planting pod of peanut.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=25. Self-planting seeds.=?Dispersal is not the only problem the) Tj T*
(seed has to meet. The majority of seeds cannot germinate well on) Tj T*
(top of the ground, and must depend on various agencies for getting) Tj T*
(under the soil. Some of them do this for themselves. The seeds of the) Tj T*
(stork?s-bill, popularly known as ?filarees,? have a sharp-pointed) Tj T*
(base and an auger-shaped appendage at the apex, ending in a) Tj T*
(projecting arm \(the ?clock? of the filaree\) by which it is blown) Tj T*
(about by the wind with a whirling motion till it strikes a soft) Tj T*
(spot, when it begins at once to bore its way into the ground. The) Tj T*
(common peanut is another example. The blossoms are borne under the) Tj T*
(leaves, near the base of the stem, and as soon as the seeds begin to) Tj T*
(form, the flower stalks lengthen several inches, carrying the young) Tj T*
(pods down to the ground, where they bore into the soil and ripen) Tj T*
(their seeds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
