(substances containing carbon goes on. Heat and moisture are evolved) Tj T*
(at the same time, and if oxidation is very active, as in Exps. 21 and) Tj T*
(22, light also. When the process takes place very slowly, no light is) Tj T*
(evolved, and so little heat as to be imperceptible without special) Tj T*
(observation. Hence, oxidation may go on around us and even in our own) Tj T*
(bodies without our being conscious of the fact.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Carbon dioxide is of prime importance to the well-being of plants. It) Tj T*
(furnishes the material from which the greater part of their organic) Tj T*
(food is derived, as will be seen when we take up the study of the) Tj T*
(leaf and its work. To animals, on the contrary, its presence is so) Tj T*
(injurious that if the proportion of it in the air we breathe ever) Tj T*
(rises much above 1 part to 1000, the ill effects become painfully) Tj T*
(sensible. It is not, however, as was formerly supposed, a poison,) Tj T*
(the harm it does being to decrease the proportion of oxygen in the) Tj T*
(atmosphere so that animals cannot get enough of it to breathe, and) Tj T*
(die of suffocation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=30. Respiration in plants and in animals.=?It was shown in Exp.) Tj T*
(24 that respiration in animals is accompanied by the products of) Tj T*
(oxidation; hence we conclude that respiration is a form of oxidation.) Tj T*
(And since these same products are given off by plants \(Exp. 25\), the) Tj T*
(inference is clear that the same process goes on in them. But in) Tj T*
(plants the life functions are so much more sluggish than in animals) Tj T*
(that it is only in their most active state, during germination and) Tj T*
(flowering, that evidence of it is to be looked for.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=31. Respiration and energy.=?In plants, as in animals, respiration) Tj T*
(is the expression or measure of energy. Sleeping animals breathe) Tj T*
(more slowly than waking ones, snakes and tortoises more slowly than) Tj T*
(hares and hawks. The more we exert ourselves and the more vital) Tj T*
(force we expend, the harder we breathe; hence, respiration is more) Tj T*
(active in children than in older persons and in working people than) Tj T*
(in those at rest. It is the same with plants; respiration is most) Tj T*
(perceptible in germinating seeds and young leaves, in buds and) Tj T*
(flowers, where active work is going on. Hence, in this condition) Tj T*
(they consume proportionately larger quantities of oxygen and) Tj T*
(liberate correspondingly larger quantities of carbon dioxide, with) Tj T*
(a proportionate increase of heat. In some of the arums,?calla lily,) Tj T*
(Jack-in-the-pulpit, colocasia, etc.,?and in large heads of compositæ,) Tj T*
(like the sunflower, where a great number of small flowers are brought) Tj T*
(together within the same protecting envelope, the rise of temperature) Tj T*
(is sometimes so marked that it may be perceived by placing a flower) Tj T*
(cluster against the cheek.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
