(  OF AIR CAUSED BY IT, THAT INTERFERED WITH GERMINATION IN THE LAST) Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT??To answer this question experimentally is not easy,) Tj T*
(  since it is difficult to obtain a complete vacuum without special) Tj T*
(  appliances. The simplest way is to fill with mercury a glass tube) Tj T*
(  30 inches long, closed at one end, and invert it over a small) Tj T*
(  vessel?a teacup, or an egg cup will answer?containing mercury) Tj T*
(  enough to cover the bottom to a depth of two or three centimeters) Tj T*
(  \(see Appendix, Weights and Measures, for English equivalents.\)) Tj T*
(  The tube must be supported in such a way that its lower end will) Tj T*
(  dip into the mercury without touching the bottom of the vessel.) Tj T*
(  With a pair of forceps insert under the mouth of the tube two or) Tj T*
(  three seeds that have been well soaked in water deprived of air) Tj T*
(  by previous boiling. Being lighter than mercury, they will float) Tj T*
(  to the top, where there is a complete absence of air while other) Tj T*
(  conditions favorable to germination are present. Before releasing,) Tj T*
(  they should be well shaken under the mercury to free them from air) Tj T*
(  bubbles, and if the coats are loose fitting so that they can be) Tj T*
(  removed without injury to the parts inclosed in them, they should) Tj T*
(  be slipped off in order to get rid of any imprisoned air they) Tj T*
(  may contain. Additional moisture may be supplied, if necessary,) Tj T*
(  by injecting, by means of a medicine dropper inserted under the) Tj T*
(  mouth of the tube, a drop or two of water that has been previously) Tj T*
(  boiled. Keep in a warm, even temperature, under conditions) Tj T*
(  favorable to germination, and compare the behavior of the seeds) Tj T*
(  with those placed in the different vessels in Exp. 26.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  If appliances for this experiment are lacking, a rough) Tj T*
(  approximation can be made by using the seeds of aquatic plants,) Tj T*
