(than when dry. This, in the opinion of the most recent investigators,) Tj T*
(is because the water in the spaces outside the cells freezes first) Tj T*
(and as moisture is gradually withdrawn from the inside to take its) Tj T*
(place, the soluble salts which may be present in the cell sap become) Tj T*
(more concentrated, and by their chemical action on the contained) Tj T*
(proteins cause them to be precipitated, or ?salted out,? as we see) Tj T*
(sugar or salt precipitated from solutions of those substances when) Tj T*
(water is withdrawn by evaporation. In this way, it is believed, the) Tj T*
(fundamental protoplasm of the cell may be so disorganized that death) Tj T*
(ensues if the freezing is continued long enough, since the protein) Tj T*
(precipitates become ?denatured? and cannot be reabsorbed if kept) Tj T*
(in a solid state too long. The length of time necessary to produce) Tj T*
(death from this cause is, of course, different in different plants,) Tj T*
(according to the kind of salts dissolved in the sap and the nature of) Tj T*
(the proteins acted on by them. The proteins in the sap of Begonia,) Tj T*
(or Pelargonium, plants which are very sensitive to cold, yield a) Tj T*
(denatured precipitate at, or a little below the freezing point of) Tj T*
(water, while those of winter rye withstand a temperature of -15° C.,) Tj T*
(and of pine needles, -40° C.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Mechanical injury through rupture of parts by freezing is not apt) Tj T*
(to cause serious damage except in cases of sudden and violent cold) Tj T*
(at a time when the tissues are gorged with sap, as not infrequently) Tj T*
(happens during the abrupt changes of temperature which sometimes) Tj T*
(occur in spring after the trees have put forth their leaves. In an) Tj T*
(extreme case of this kind, the writer has seen the trunk of an oak) Tj T*
(a foot or more in diameter split in deep seams from the effects of) Tj T*
(freezing.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=34. The length of time during which seeds may retain their) Tj T*
(vitality.=?No direct experiment can be made to test this point,) Tj T*
(since it would require months, or even years, covering in some) Tj T*
(instances more than the lifetime of a generation. It has been stated) Tj T*
(on good authority that seeds of the water chinquapin \(Nelumbo\) have) Tj T*
(germinated after more than a hundred years, and moss spores preserved) Tj T*
(in herbariums, after fifty. But the records in such cases are not) Tj T*
(always trustworthy, and there is absolutely no foundation for the) Tj T*
(statements sometimes made about the germination of wheat grains found) Tj T*
(preserved with mummies over two thousand years old. If kept perfectly) Tj T*
(dry, however, seed may sometimes be preserved for months, or even) Tj T*
(years. Peas have been known to sprout after ten years, red clover) Tj T*
(after twelve, and tobacco after twenty. Ordinarily, however, the) Tj T*
(vitality of seeds diminishes with age, and in making experiments it) Tj T*
(is best to select fresh ones. Those used for comparison should also,) Tj T*
(as far as possible, be of the same size and weight.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=35. Effect of precocious germination.=?It has been found by) Tj T*
(experiment that plants raised from immature seed, when they will) Tj T*
(germinate at all \(Exp. 32\), yield earlier and larger crops than the) Tj T*
(same kinds from mature seed. Early tomatoes and some other vegetables) Tj T*
(are produced in this way. The majority of seeds, however, require) Tj T*
(a period of rest before beginning their life work. Those that are) Tj T*
