(          III. DEVELOPMENT OF THE SEEDLING) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Seedlings of various kinds in different stages of growth.) Tj T*
(  It is recommended that the same species be used that were studied) Tj T*
(  in Section III, Chapter I, or such equivalents as may have been) Tj T*
(  substituted for them. Enough should be provided to give each pupil) Tj T*
(  three or four specimens in different stages of development. Seeds,) Tj T*
(  even of the same kind, develop at such different rates that it) Tj T*
(  will probably not be necessary to make more than two plantings of) Tj T*
(  each sort, from 2 to 5 days apart. Soaked seeds of corn and wheat) Tj T*
(  will germinate in from 3 to 7 days, according to the temperature;) Tj T*
(  oats in 1 to 4; beans in 4 to 6; squash and castor beans in from) Tj T*
(  8 to 10. Very obdurate ones may be hastened by clipping. Keep the) Tj T*
(  germinators in an even temperature, at about 70° to 80° F.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Pine is a very difficult seed to germinate, requiring usually) Tj T*
(  from 18 to 21 days. By soaking the mast for twenty-four hours and) Tj T*
(  planting in damp sand or sawdust kept at an even temperature of 23°) Tj T*
(  C. or about 75° F., specimens may be obtained.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
