(=37. The root.=?Examine the lower end of the hypocotyl and find where) Tj T*
(the roots originate; would you say that they are an outgrowth from) Tj T*
(the stem, or the stem from the root? Observe that the root of the) Tj T*
(corn does not continue to grow in a single main axis like that of) Tj T*
(the castor bean, but that numerous adventitious and secondary roots) Tj T*
(spring from various points near the base of the hypocotyl and spread) Tj T*
(out in every direction, thus giving rise to the fibrous roots of) Tj T*
(grains and grasses.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=38. Root hairs.=?Notice the grains of sand or sawdust that cling to) Tj T*
(the rootlets of plants grown in a bedding of that kind. Examine with) Tj T*
(a lens and see if you can account for their presence. Lay the root) Tj T*
(in water on a bit of glass, hold up to the light and look for root) Tj T*
(hairs; on what part are they most abundant?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 56.?Seedling of wheat, with root hairs.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The hairs are the chief agents in absorbing moisture from the) Tj T*
(soil. They do not last very long, but are constantly dying and) Tj T*
(being renewed in the younger and tenderer parts of the root. These) Tj T*
(are usually broken away in tearing the roots from the soil, so) Tj T*
(that it is not easy to detect the hairs except in seedlings, even) Tj T*
(with a microscope. In oat, maple, and radish seedlings they are) Tj T*
(very abundant and clearly visible to the naked eye. The amount of) Tj T*
(absorbing surface on a root is greatly increased by their presence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 57.?Diagrammatic section of a root tip: _a_,) Tj T*
(cortex; _b_, central cylinder in which the conducting vessels are) Tj T*
(situated; _c_, root cap; _g_, growing point.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=39. The root cap.=?Look at the tip of the root through your lens) Tj T*
(and notice the soft, transparent crescent or horseshoe-shaped mass) Tj T*
(in which it terminates. This is the root cap and serves to protect) Tj T*
(the tender parts behind it as the roots burrow their way through the) Tj T*
(soil. Being soft and yielding, it is not so likely to be injured by) Tj T*
(the hard substances with which it comes in contact as would be the) Tj T*
(more compact tissue of the roots. It is composed of loose cells out) Tj T*
(of which the solid root substance is being formed; the growing point) Tj T*
(of the root, _g_, is at the extremity of the tip just behind the) Tj T*
(cap, _c_ \(Fig. 57\). The cap is very apparent in a seedling of corn,) Tj T*
(and can easily be seen with the naked eye, especially if a thin) Tj T*
(longitudinal section is made. It is also well seen in the water roots) Tj T*
(of the common duckweed \(_Lemna_\), and on those developed by a cutting) Tj T*
(of the wandering Jew, when placed in water. Are there any hairs on) Tj T*
(the root cap? Can you account for their absence?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
