(=48. What growth is.=?With the seedling begins the growth of the) Tj T*
(plant. Most people understand by this word mere increase in size; but) Tj T*
(growth is something more than this. It involves a change of form,) Tj T*
(usually, but not necessarily, accompanied by increase in bulk. Mere) Tj T*
(mechanical change is not growth, as when we bend or stretch an organ) Tj T*
(by force, though if it can be kept in the altered position till such) Tj T*
(position becomes permanent, or as we say in common speech, ?till it) Tj T*
(grows that way,? the change may become growth. To constitute true) Tj T*
(growth, the change of form must be permanent, and brought about, or) Tj T*
(maintained, by forces within the plant itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=49. Conditions of growth.=?The internal conditions depend upon) Tj T*
(the organization of the plant. The essential external conditions) Tj T*
(are the same as those required for germination: food material,) Tj T*
(water, oxygen, and a sufficient degree of warmth. It may be greatly) Tj T*
(influenced by other circumstances, such as light, gravitation,) Tj T*
(pressure, and \(probably\) electricity; but the four first named are) Tj T*
(the essential conditions without which no growth is possible.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=50. Cycle of growth.=?When an organ becomes rigid and its) Tj T*
(form fixed, there is no further growth, but only nutrition and) Tj T*
(repair,?processes which must not be confounded with it. Every plant) Tj T*
(and part of a plant has its period of beginning, maximum, decline,) Tj T*
(and cessation of growth. The cycle may extend over a few hours, as in) Tj T*
