(=56. Osmosis.=?The passage of liquids or of solids in solution) Tj T*
(through membranes is known as _osmosis_. Our experiments have) Tj T*
(shown that the principles governing the osmotic movement are: \(1\)) Tj T*
(the passage of water from the thinner liquid toward the denser) Tj T*
(takes place more rapidly than in the opposite direction; \(2\) the) Tj T*
(rapidity of the transfer depends on the difference in density; \(3\)) Tj T*
(crystallizable substances in solution, like sugar and salt, osmose) Tj T*
(readily; \(4\) albuminous or gelatinous substances, such as the white) Tj T*
(of an egg, osmose so slowly that the cell wall may be regarded as) Tj T*
(practically impermeable to them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=57. Osmosis a form of diffusion.=?Osmosis is related to diffusion) Tj T*
(as a part to the whole. In other words, it is a name given to the) Tj T*
(process when it takes place through a membrane, whether solid, as the) Tj T*
(outer wall of the cell, or semi-fluid, as the inner wall of living) Tj T*
(protoplasm. Diffusion may therefore take place without osmosis,) Tj T*
(that is, in the absence of a membrane, as, for example, when we) Tj T*
(sweeten our tea or coffee by allowing sugar to diffuse through it.) Tj T*
(Many membranes offer little resistance to the osmotic movement of) Tj T*
(crystallizable substances. Such membranes are said to be _permeable_.) Tj T*
