(Membranes which are not permeable to the dissolved solids, are called) Tj T*
(_semi-permeable_, since they allow the diffusion of water but not of) Tj T*
(the substances in solution. Living protoplasm is of this class. It is) Tj T*
(only very slightly permeable to many substances toward which, when) Tj T*
(dead, it acts as a permeable membrane.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=58. Absorption in living and dead cells.=?There is one great) Tj T*
(difference between the action of the artificial cell used in the) Tj T*
(foregoing experiments and that of the cells of which a living body) Tj T*
(is built up. The living cell always has at least two membranes. One) Tj T*
(of these, the cell wall, is readily permeable, while the other, the) Tj T*
(protoplasm, is semi-permeable?that is, substances in solution usually) Tj T*
(diffuse more or less slowly, while water diffuses rapidly. Hence) Tj T*
(in the living cell the protoplasm exercises a power of absorption) Tj T*
(independent of the cell wall, sometimes rejecting substances admitted) Tj T*
(by the latter, sometimes retaining others to which it is permeable,) Tj T*
(as shown in Exp. 40. In the boiled beet the protoplasm had been) Tj T*
(killed and the red coloring matter passed through it unhindered,) Tj T*
(while in the living one it was held back by the protoplasmic lining,) Tj T*
(which is thus seen to control the absorptive properties of the cell.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=59. Plasmolysis.=?Cells can be killed or injured in other ways) Tj T*
(than by heat; for example, by cold, by poisons, by starvation, and) Tj T*
(by overfeeding through the use of too much fertilizer or too rich) Tj T*
(a one. In this last case, the soil water becomes impregnated with) Tj T*
(soluble matter from the manure, which may render it denser than the) Tj T*
(sap in the roots. When this happens, it will cause the osmotic flow) Tj T*
(to set outward and thus deplete the cell of its water; whence we have) Tj T*
(the paradox that a cell, or even a whole plant, may be starved by) Tj T*
(overfeeding. This action of osmosis in withdrawing the contents from) Tj T*
(a cell is termed _plasmolysis_, and you can easily understand how) Tj T*
(very important a knowledge of the principles governing it is to the) Tj T*
(farmer in determining the application of fertilizers to his crops.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dead cells, although powerless to carry on the life processes of a) Tj T*
(plant, have nevertheless important uses in serving the purposes of) Tj T*
(mechanical support and also to some extent in assisting in the work) Tj T*
(of absorption, though their function here is a purely mechanical one.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
