(=62. Essential constituents.=?The composition of the ash of any) Tj T*
(particular plant will depend upon two things: the absorbent capacity) Tj T*
(of the plant itself and the nature of the substances contained in the) Tj T*
(soil in which it grows. But chemical analysis has shown that however) Tj T*
(the ashes may vary, they always contain some proportion of the) Tj T*
(following substances: potassium \(potash\), calcium \(lime\), magnesium,) Tj T*
(phosphorus, and \(in green plants\) iron. These elements occur in all) Tj T*
(plants, and if any one of them is absent, growth becomes abnormal if) Tj T*
(not impossible.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The part of the dried substances that was burned away after expelling) Tj T*
(the water consists, in all plants, mainly of carbon, hydrogen,) Tj T*
(oxygen, nitrogen, and sulphur, in varying proportions. These five) Tj T*
(rank first in importance among the essential elements of vegetable) Tj T*
(life, and without them the plant cell itself, the physiological unit) Tj T*
(of vegetable structure, could not exist. They compose the greater) Tj T*
(part of the substance of every plant, carbon alone usually forming) Tj T*
(about one half the dry weight. Other substances may be present in) Tj T*
(varying proportions, but the two groups named above are found in all) Tj T*
(plants without exception, and so we may conclude that \(with the) Tj T*
(possible addition of chlorine\) they form the indispensable elements) Tj T*
(of plant food. Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur, and) Tj T*
(phosphorus compose the structure of which the plant is built. The) Tj T*
(other four ingredients do not enter into the substance as component) Tj T*
(parts, but aid in the chemical processes by which the life functions) Tj T*
(of the plant are carried on, and are none the less essential elements) Tj T*
(of its food. Figure 74 shows the difference between a plant grown in) Tj T*
(a solution where all the food elements are present, and others in) Tj T*
(which some of them are lacking.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 75.?Roots of soy bean bearing tubercle-forming) Tj T*
(bacteria.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=63. How plants obtain their food material.=?Plants obtain their) Tj T*
(supply of the various mineral salts from solutions in the soil) Tj T*
(water which they absorb through their roots. With a few doubtful) Tj T*
(exceptions, they cannot assimilate their food unless it is in a) Tj T*
(liquid or gaseous form. Of the gases, carbon dioxide, oxygen, and) Tj T*
(hydrogen can be freely absorbed from the air, or from water with) Tj T*
(various substances in solution, but most plants are so constituted) Tj T*
(that they cannot absorb free nitrogen from the air; they can take it) Tj T*
(only in the form of compounds from nitrates dissolved in the soil,) Tj T*
(and hence the importance of ammonia and other nitrogenous compounds) Tj T*
(in artificial fertilizers. Some of the pea family, however, bear) Tj T*
(on their roots little tubers formed by minute organisms called) Tj T*
(bacteria, which have the power of extracting nitrogen directly from) Tj T*
(the free air mingled with the soil; and hence the soil in which these) Tj T*
(tuber-bearing legumes decay is enriched with nitrogen in a form ready) Tj T*
(for use.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
