(  grams, or 1 ounce, to a pint. Try the same experiment with weak and) Tj T*
(  strong solutions of any conveniently obtainable liquid fertilizer.) Tj T*
(  After 45 minutes or an hour examine the roots under a lens and note) Tj T*
(  the change that has taken place. What has gone out of them? What) Tj T*
(  caused the loss of the contained sap?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 52. TO TEST THE WEIGHT OF SOILS.?Thoroughly dry and) Tj T*
(  powder a pint each of sand and clay, measure accurately, and) Tj T*
(  balance against each other in a pair of scales. Which weighs more,) Tj T*
(  bulk for bulk, a ?light? soil, or a ?heavy? one? \(77.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 53. TO TEST THE CAPACITY OF SOILS FOR ABSORBING AND) Tj T*
(  RETAINING MOISTURE.?Arrange, as shown in Fig. 82, a number of) Tj T*
(  long-necked bottles from which the bottom has been removed. This) Tj T*
(  can be done by making a small indentation with a file at the point) Tj T*
(  desired and leading the break round the circumference with the end) Tj T*
(  of a glowing wire or a red-hot poker. The crack will follow the) Tj T*
(  heated object with sufficient regularity to answer the purpose.) Tj T*
(  Tie a piece of thin cloth over the mouth of each bottle and invert) Tj T*
(  with the necks extending an inch or two into empty tumblers placed) Tj T*
(  beneath. Fill all to the same height with soils of different) Tj T*
(  kinds?sand, clay, gravel, loam, vegetable mold, etc.?and pour over) Tj T*
(  each the same quantity of water from above. Watch the rate at which) Tj T*
(  the liquid filters through into the tumblers. Which loses its) Tj T*
(  moisture soonest? Which retains it longest?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 82.?Apparatus for testing the capacity of soils) Tj T*
(to take in and retain moisture.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Next leave the soils in the bottles dry, fill the tumblers up to) Tj T*
(  the necks of the bottles, and watch the rate at which the water) Tj T*
(  rises in the different ones. The power of soils to absorb moisture) Tj T*
(  is called _capillarity_. Which of your samples shows the highest) Tj T*
(  capillarity? Which the lowest? Do you observe any relation between) Tj T*
(  the capillarity of a soil and its power of retention?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=68. Roots as holdfasts.=?One use of ordinary roots is to serve as) Tj T*
(props and stays for anchoring plants to the soil. Tall herbs and) Tj T*
(shrubs, and vegetation generally that is exposed to much stress of) Tj T*
(weather, are apt to have large, strong roots. Even plants of the same) Tj T*
(species will develop systems of very different strength according as) Tj T*
(they grow in sheltered or exposed places.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 83.?Dandelion: _a_, common form, grown in plains) Tj T*
(region at low altitude; _b_, alpine form.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
