(=69. Root pull.=?Roots are not mere passive holdfasts, but exert) Tj T*
(an active downward pull upon the stem. Notice the rooting end of a) Tj T*
(strawberry or raspberry shoot and observe how the stem appears to) Tj T*
(be drawn into the ground at the rooting point. In the leaf rosettes) Tj T*
(of herbs growing flat on the ground or in the crevices of walls and) Tj T*
(pavements, the strong depression observable at the center is due to) Tj T*
(root pull. \(Fig. 84.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 84.?Raspberry stolon showing root pull.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=70. Storage of food.=?Another office of roots is to store up food) Tj T*
(for the use of the plant. This is done chiefly in the tissues of) Tj T*
(fleshy roots and tubers, and gives to them their great economic) Tj T*
(value. Next to grains and cereals, roots probably furnish a larger) Tj T*
(portion of food to the human race than any other crop. In addition to) Tj T*
(this they are also the source of valuable drugs, condiments, and dyes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=71. Absorption and conveyance of sap.=?But the most important) Tj T*
(function of roots is that of absorption. By their action the soil) Tj T*
(water and the minerals contained in it are drawn up into the plant) Tj T*
(body and made available for conversion by the leaves into organic) Tj T*
(foods, as will be explained in another chapter. From the nature) Tj T*
(of their function, most roots have naturally a strong affinity) Tj T*
(for water, and its presence or absence has a marked influence on) Tj T*
(their direction of growth, being often sufficient to overcome that) Tj T*
(of geotropism \(Exp. 47\). There are many trees and shrubs, notably) Tj T*
(willow, sweet bay, red birch, and the like, that grow best on the) Tj T*
(banks of streams and ponds, where their roots can have direct access) Tj T*
(to water. Excess of moisture, however, is injurious to most land) Tj T*
(plants by preventing the roots from getting sufficient air for) Tj T*
(respiration.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=72. The conditions of absorption.=?The sap in the root cells is) Tj T*
(normally denser than the water in the soil, so there is a continuous) Tj T*
(flow from the latter to the former. But if, for any reason, the) Tj T*
(density of the liquids should be reversed, the flow would set in the) Tj T*
(opposite direction, and if continued long enough, the strength of the) Tj T*
(plant would be literally ?sapped? by the exhaustion of its tissues,) Tj T*
(so that it would die. What is this process of cell exhaustion called?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=73. The use of acid secretions to the root.=?It was shown in Exp.) Tj T*
(50 that carbon dioxide and probably other substances occur in the) Tj T*
(immediate vicinity of roots. Carbon dioxide is an active agent in) Tj T*
(dissolving the various mineral matters contained in the soil, and as) Tj T*
(these last can be absorbed only in a liquid or a gaseous state \(63\),) Tj T*
(the advantage to the root as an absorbent organ, of being able to) Tj T*
(secrete such active solvents, is obvious.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 85.?A natural root etching, found on a piece of) Tj T*
(slate.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=74. Relation of roots to the soil.=?In order to perform their work) Tj T*
