(of absorption, roots must have access to a suitable soil. To produce) Tj T*
(the best results a soil must contain \(1\) all the essential mineral) Tj T*
(constituents \(62\); \(2\) moisture for dissolving these materials; and) Tj T*
(\(3\) air enough to supply the oxygen which is necessary to the life) Tj T*
(processes of all green plants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=75. Composition of soils.=?Sand, clay, and humus, or vegetable) Tj T*
(mold, with the various substances dissolved in them, constitute the) Tj T*
(basis of cultivated soils. A mixture of sand, clay, and humus is) Tj T*
(called loam. When the proportion of humus is very large and well) Tj T*
(decomposed, the mixture is called _muck_. Pure sand contains but) Tj T*
(little nourishing matter and is too porous to retain water well. Pure) Tj T*
(clay is too compact to be easily permeable to either air or water.) Tj T*
(Most soils are composed of a mixture of the two with vegetable mold) Tj T*
(in varying proportions, giving a sandy loam, or a clay loam, as the) Tj T*
(case may be.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=76. Tillage.=?The advantages of tillage are: \(_a_\) that by breaking) Tj T*
(up the hard lumps it renders the soil more permeable to air and water) Tj T*
(and more easily penetrable by the roots in their search for food;) Tj T*
(\(_b_\) the covering of loose, friable earth left by the plow and the) Tj T*
(harrow acts as a mulch, and by shading the soil below, prevents) Tj T*
(too rapid a loss of water by evaporation. Where the essential food) Tj T*
(ingredients are present, good tillage counts for more in making a) Tj T*
(crop than the original quality of the soil.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=77. Light and heavy soils.=?These terms are used by farmers not) Tj T*
(in relation to the weight of soils, but in reference to the ease) Tj T*
(or difficulty with which they are worked. Light soils contain a) Tj T*
(preponderance of sand; heavy ones, of clay.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
