(          V. DIFFERENT FORMS OF ROOTS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Examples of taproots: bean, pea, cotton, maple seedlings,) Tj T*
(  or any kind of very young woody root. Fibrous: any kind of grass) Tj T*
(  or grain. Fleshy: parsnip, turnip, carrot, dahlia, sweet potato.) Tj T*
(  Water: duckweed, pondweed, or a cutting of wandering Jew grown) Tj T*
(  in water. Parasitic: mistletoe, dodder, beech drops. Aërial and) Tj T*
(  adventitious: the aërial roots of old scuppernong vines, climbing) Tj T*
(  roots of ivy and trumpet vine, prop roots from the lower nodes of) Tj T*
(  cornstalks and sugar cane.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=78. Basis of distinction.=?Roots vary in form and external structure) Tj T*
(according to their origin, function, and surroundings. In reference) Tj T*
(to the first, they are classed as primary or secondary; in regard to) Tj T*
(the second, as dry or fleshy; while as to surroundings, they may be) Tj T*
(adapted to either the soil, water, air, or the parasitic habit. Soil) Tj T*
(roots are the normal form. According to their mode of growth they are) Tj T*
(either fibrous or axial.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: PLATE 3.?Aërial roots of a Mexican ?strangling? fig,) Tj T*
(enveloping the trunk of a palm \(_From_ ?Rep?t. Mo. Bot. Garden?\).]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=79. Taproots.=?These are the common form of the axial type. Compare) Tj T*
(the root of any young hardwood cion a year or two old with one) Tj T*
(of a mature stalk of corn or other grain, and with the roots of) Tj T*
(seedlings of the same species. Notice the difference in their mode) Tj T*
(of growth. In the first kind a single stout prolongation called a) Tj T*
(taproot proceeds from the lower end of the hypocotyl and continues) Tj T*
(the axis of growth straight downward, unless turned aside by some) Tj T*
(external influence. A taproot may be either simple, as in the turnip,) Tj T*
(radish, and dandelion, or branched, as in most shrubs and trees. In) Tj T*
(the latter case the main axis is called the primary root, and the) Tj T*
(branches are secondary ones.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 86.?Branched taproot of maple.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
