(=80. Fibrous and fascicled roots.=?Where the main axis fails) Tj T*
(to develop, as in the corn and grasses generally, a number of) Tj T*
(independent branches take its place, forming what are known as) Tj T*
(fibrous roots. Both fibrous and taproots may be either hard or) Tj T*
(fleshy. The turnip and carrot are examples of fleshy taproots, the) Tj T*
(dahlia and rhubarb of fascicled roots. The function of both is the) Tj T*
(storage of nourishment. The sweet potato is an example of a tuberous) Tj T*
(root.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=81. Practical importance of this distinction.=?The difference) Tj T*
(between axial and fibrous roots has important bearings in) Tj T*
(agriculture. The first kind, which are characteristic of most) Tj T*
(dicotyls, strike deep and draw their nourishment from the lower) Tj T*
(strata of the soil, while the fibrous and fascicled, or radial kinds,) Tj T*
(as we may call them for want of a better name, spread out near the) Tj T*
(surface and are more dependent on external conditions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 87.?Fibrous root.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=82. Roots that grow above ground.=?The kinds of roots that have) Tj T*
(just been considered are all subterranean, and bring the plant) Tj T*
(into relation with the earth, whether for the purpose of absorbing) Tj T*
(nourishment, or of mechanical support, or, as in the majority of) Tj T*
(cases, for both. Many plants, however, do not get their mineral) Tj T*
(nutrients directly from the soil, and these give rise to various) Tj T*
(forms suited to other conditions of alimentation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=83. Adventitious roots.=?This name applies to any kinds of roots) Tj T*
(that occur on stems, or in other unusual positions. They may be) Tj T*
(considered as intermediate between the two classes named in 81; for) Tj T*
(while their starting point is above ground, they generally end by) Tj T*
(fixing themselves in the soil, where they often function as normal) Tj T*
(roots. Familiar examples are the roots that put out from the lower) Tj T*
(nodes of corn and sugar cane stalks, and serve both to supply) Tj T*
(additional moisture and to anchor the plant more firmly to the soil.) Tj T*
(Most plants will develop adventitious roots if covered with earth, or) Tj T*
(even if merely kept in contact with the ground. The gardener takes) Tj T*
(advantage of this capacity when he propagates by cuttings and layers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=84. Water roots.=?These are generally white and threadlike and more) Tj T*
(tender and succulent than ordinary soil roots, because they have less) Tj T*
(work to do. Floating and immersed plants, such as bladderwort and) Tj T*
(hornwort \(_Ceratophyllum_\) have no need of absorbent roots, since the) Tj T*
(greater part of their surface is in contact with water and can absorb) Tj T*
(directly what is needed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Land plants will often develop water roots and thrive for a time if) Tj T*
(the liquid holds in solution a sufficient quantity of air and mineral) Tj T*
(nutrients. Place a cutting of wandering Jew in a glass of clear) Tj T*
(water, and in from four to six days it will develop beautiful water) Tj T*
(roots in which both hairs and cap are clearly visible to the naked) Tj T*
(eye.) Tj T*
