(=85. Haustoria=, from a Latin word meaning to drain, or exhaust,) Tj T*
(is a name given to the roots of parasitic plants, or such as live) Tj T*
(by attaching themselves to some other living organism, from which) Tj T*
(they draw their nourishment ready made. Their roots are adapted) Tj T*
(to penetrating the substance of the _host_, as their victim is) Tj T*
(called, and absorbing the sap from it. Dodder and mistletoe are the) Tj T*
(best-known examples of plant parasites, though the latter is only) Tj T*
(partially parasitic, as it merely takes up the sap from the host and) Tj T*
(manufactures its own food by means of its green leaves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 88.?Beech root: _A_, grown in unsterilized wood) Tj T*
(humus: _p_, strands of fungal hyphæ, associated at _a_, with humus;) Tj T*
(_B_, grown in wood humus freed from fungus by sterilization?it is not) Tj T*
(provided with fungal hyphæ, and has root hairs, _h_. \(_A_ and _B_) Tj T*
(both several times magnified.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=86. Saprophytes.=?Akin to parasites are saprophytes, which live) Tj T*
(on dead and decaying vegetable matter. They are only partially) Tj T*
(parasitic and do not bear the haustoria of true parasites. Many of) Tj T*
(them, of which the Indian pipe \(_Monotropa_\) and coral root are) Tj T*
(familiar examples, obtain their nourishment in part, at least, by) Tj T*
(association with certain saprophytic fungi, which enmesh their roots) Tj T*
(in a growth of threadlike fibers that take the place of root hairs) Tj T*
(and absorb organic food from the rich humus in which these plants) Tj T*
(grow. Such growths are called _mycorrhiza_, meaning ?fungal roots.?) Tj T*
(Similar associations are formed by some of the higher plants also.) Tj T*
(The rootlets of the common beech and of certain of the pine family,) Tj T*
(for instance, are often enveloped in a network of fungus fibers,) Tj T*
(and in this case root hairs are developed very poorly, or not at) Tj T*
(all. Besides greatly increasing the absorbent surface by their) Tj T*
(ramification through the soil, the mycorrhizal threads may possibly) Tj T*
(benefit the plant in other ways also, as, for instance, by bringing) Tj T*
(about chemical changes that might aid in the work of nutrition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 89.?An air plant \(_Tillandsia_\), growing on the) Tj T*
(underside of a bough.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 90.?A single strand of _Tillandsia usneoides_, a) Tj T*
(rootless epiphyte belonging to the pineapple family; better known as) Tj T*
(the ?Spanish moss? that drapes the boughs of trees so conspicuously) Tj T*
(in the warm parts of America. Two-thirds natural size. \(Photographed) Tj T*
(by C. F. O?Keefe.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=87. Epiphytes, or air plants.=?In the proper meaning of the word) Tj T*
(these are not parasitic, but use their host merely as a mechanical) Tj T*
(support to bring them into better light relations. The name, however,) Tj T*
(is loosely applied to all plants that find a lodgment on the trunks) Tj T*
(and branches of trees, whether parasites or true epiphytes that draw) Tj T*
(no nourishment from the host. Not infrequently the latter is killed) Tj T*
(by them through suffocation, overweighting, or the constriction of) Tj T*
(the stems by close clinging twiners.) Tj T*
ET
