() Tj T*
(=88. Aërial roots= are such as have no connection at all with the) Tj T*
(soil or with any host plant, except as they may lodge upon the trunks) Tj T*
(and branches of trees for a support. In other than purely epiphytic) Tj T*
(plants, which get all their nourishment from the air, they are) Tj T*
(generally subsidiary to soil roots, like the long dangling cords that) Tj T*
(hang from some species of old grapevines; or they subserve other) Tj T*
(purposes altogether than absorbing nourishment, as the climbing roots) Tj T*
(of the trumpet vine and poison ivy. A very remarkable development) Tj T*
(of aërial roots takes place in the ?strangling fig? of Mexico and) Tj T*
(Florida, which begins life as a small epiphyte, from seeds dropped by) Tj T*
(birds on the boughs or trunks of trees. When it gets well started,) Tj T*
(the young plant sends down enormous aërial roots, which find their) Tj T*
(way to the ground, and in time so completely envelop the host that it) Tj T*
(is literally strangled to death \(Plate 3, p. 73\). When this support) Tj T*
(is removed, the sheathing roots take its place and become to all) Tj T*
(intents and purposes the stem of the fig tree, which now leads an) Tj T*
(independent life.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 91.?Root system of a tobacco plant.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=89. The root system.=?The entire mass of roots belonging to a) Tj T*
(plant, with all its ramifications and subdivisions, composes a root) Tj T*
(system. The extent of root expansion is in general about equal to) Tj T*
(that of the crown, thus bringing the new and active parts under the) Tj T*
(drip of the boughs where the moisture is most abundant. Some plants) Tj T*
(have root systems out of all seeming proportion to their size. A) Tj T*
(catalpa seedling six months old showed, by actual measurement,) Tj T*
(250 feet of root growth, and it is estimated that the roots of a) Tj T*
(thrifty cornstalk, if laid end to end, would extend a mile. In the) Tj T*
(development of the root system, a great deal depends upon external) Tj T*
(conditions. In a poor, dry soil, the roots have to travel farther in) Tj T*
(search of a livelihood, and so a larger system has to be developed) Tj T*
(than in a more favorable location.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
