(  EXPERIMENT 54. TO SHOW THE MOVEMENTS OF TWINING STEMS.?Raise a) Tj T*
(  young hop or bean seedling in the schoolroom and allow it to grow) Tj T*
(  about two decimeters?8 to 10 inches?in length before providing) Tj T*
(  it with a support. Does the stem form any coils? Bring it in) Tj T*
(  contact with a suitable upright support and watch for a day or) Tj T*
(  two. What happens? Notice whether it starts to coil from right to) Tj T*
(  left or from left to right and see if you can coax it to turn in) Tj T*
(  the opposite direction. When it has reached the end of its stake,) Tj T*
(  allow it to grow about five centimeters \(two inches, approximately\)) Tj T*
(  beyond, and watch the revolution of the tip. Cut a hole through the) Tj T*
(  center of a piece of cardboard about 14 centimeters \(five to six) Tj T*
(  inches\) in diameter, slip it over the loose end of the stem, and) Tj T*
(  fasten it to the stake in a horizontal position, with a pin. Note) Tj T*
(  the position of the stem tip at regular intervals and mark on the) Tj T*
(  cardboard; how long does it take to complete a revolution? Does it) Tj T*
(  continue to coil, or to coil as readily, after leaving its stake as) Tj T*
(  before? What would you infer from this as to the effect of contact) Tj T*
(  in stimulating it to coil?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Find out by experiment if it can climb well by means of a glass or) Tj T*
(  other smooth rod; by a fine wire; a broomstick; a large, smooth) Tj T*
(  post. See whether it does better on a horizontal or an upright) Tj T*
(  support.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 55. TO ILLUSTRATE THE COILING OF STEMS.?Run a gathering) Tj T*
(  thread in one side of a narrow strip of muslin and notice how the) Tj T*
(  ruffle thus drawn will curl into a spiral when allowed to dangle) Tj T*
(  from the needle. Can you think of any cause that might act on a) Tj T*
(  stem in the same way? Suppose, for instance, that one side should) Tj T*
(  grow faster than the other; what would be the effect? \(54.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Split the stem of a fresh dandelion, or other herbaceous scape,) Tj T*
(  longitudinally, and immerse it in a pan of fresh water for a few) Tj T*
(  minutes. Notice how the two halves curve outward, or even coil up) Tj T*
(  like the strip of muslin. This is due to the tension caused by the) Tj T*
(  more rapid absorption of the thinner walled cells of the internal) Tj T*
(  tissues. These, when relieved of the resistance of the thicker) Tj T*
(  walled outer tissues, swell on their free side, but are held back) Tj T*
(  on the other by the non-absorbent outer parts, as one side of the) Tj T*
(  muslin ruffle was held by the gathering thread.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 56. TO FIND OUT WHETHER THE DIRECTION OF STEM GROWTH IS) Tj T*
(  INFLUENCED BY LIGHT.?Place two rapidly growing young pea, bean,) Tj T*
(  sunflower, or squash plants, each with several well-developed) Tj T*
(  leaves, in a room or box with a light exposure on one side only.) Tj T*
(  After two or three days, notice the position of the stems in regard) Tj T*
(  to the light. Does either one show a more decided inclination) Tj T*
(  toward it than the other?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 57. IS THE LIGHT RELATION OF THE STEM INFLUENCED BY THE) Tj T*
(  LEAVES??Cut the leaves from one of the plants used in Exp. 56,) Tj T*
