(=90. Classification.=?Stems are classed according to \(1\) duration, as) Tj T*
(annuals, biennials, and perennials; \(2\) with reference to hardness) Tj T*
(or softness of structure, as herbaceous and woody; \(3\) in regard to) Tj T*
(position and direction of growth, as erect, prostrate, climbing,) Tj T*
(inclined, declined, underground, etc.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=91. Annuals= complete their life cycle in a single season and then) Tj T*
(die down as soon as they have perfected their seed. Many of our most) Tj T*
(troublesome weeds belong to this class and might be exterminated) Tj T*
(by the simple expedient of mowing them down before their time of) Tj T*
(flowering.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=92. Biennials=, as the name implies, live for two years. Their) Tj T*
(energy during the first season is spent chiefly in laying by a store) Tj T*
(of nourishment, usually in the tissues of fleshy roots \(70\). By this) Tj T*
(means they get a good start in the second season and mature their) Tj T*
(seeds early. Many of our common garden vegetables, such as turnips,) Tj T*
(carrots, parsnips, and cabbage, belong to this class. Where is the) Tj T*
(nourishment stored in the cabbage?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 93.?A biennial plant, mullein, in winter) Tj T*
(condition with stem reduced to little more than a disk supporting) Tj T*
(a rosette of leaves. Notice how close they cling to the earth, and) Tj T*
(compare them with their fruiting condition a few months later as) Tj T*
(shown in Fig. 237.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=93. Perennials= are plants that live on indefinitely, like most of) Tj T*
(our forest trees and woody-stemmed shrubs. Woody stems are usually) Tj T*
(perennial and may live for hundreds and even thousands of years, as) Tj T*
(those of the giant sequoias of California, and the famous chestnut of) Tj T*
(Mt. Etna.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=94. Herbaceous stems= are more or less succulent and die down after) Tj T*
(fruiting. They are usually annuals, though some kinds, like the) Tj T*
(garden geraniums and the common St.-John?s-wort, show a tendency) Tj T*
(to become woody, especially at the base, and live on from year to) Tj T*
(year. Others, such as the hawkweed and dahlia, die down above ground) Tj T*
(in winter, but are enabled to keep their underground parts alive) Tj T*
(indefinitely, through the nourishment stored in them, and are thus) Tj T*
(perennial below ground and annual above. Woody-stemmed annuals, such) Tj T*
(as the cotton and castor oil plant, are not, properly speaking,) Tj T*
(herbs. In the tropical countries to which they belong they are) Tj T*
