(perennial shrubs, or even small trees, but on being transplanted to) Tj T*
(colder regions have been compelled to take on the annual habit as an) Tj T*
(adaptation to climate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 94.?Orange hawkweed with runners.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 95.?Prostrate stem of Lycopodium with assurgent) Tj T*
(branches.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 96.?Diagram of stem growth: _ps_, surface of the) Tj T*
(ground; _e_, erect position; _d_, declined; _a_, assurgent; _p_,) Tj T*
(prostrate; _u_, vertical direction underground.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=95. Direction and habit of growth.=?As to manner of growth, there) Tj T*
(are many forms, from the upright boles of the beech and pine to the) Tj T*
(trailing, prostrate, and creeping stems of which we have examples) Tj T*
(in the running periwinkle, the prostrate spurge and the creeping) Tj T*
(partridge berry \(_Mitchella repens_\), respectively. Trailing and) Tj T*
(prostrate stems are very apt to become creepers by the development of) Tj T*
(adventitious roots at their nodes wherever they come in contact with) Tj T*
(the soil. The rooting stems of dewberries, the runners and stolons of) Tj T*
(strawberries and currants, are familiar examples.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Between the extremes of prostrate and upright, stems may be inclined) Tj T*
(or bent in various degrees. As shown in Fig. 96, there are two modes) Tj T*
(of inclination: _assurgent_, _a_, from the prostrate, _p_, toward) Tj T*
(the upright, _e_; and _declined_, _d_, from the upright toward the) Tj T*
(prostrate. Below the surface, _ps_, occur only underground stems. Is) Tj T*
(the prostrate habit an advantageous one for light exposure? Can you) Tj T*
(think of any compensating advantages a plant might derive from it;) Tj T*
(for example, in regard to warmth and moisture?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 97.?Twining stems: _A_, hop twining with the sun;) Tj T*
(_B_, convolvulus twining against the sun.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=96. Climbing stems.=?These are such as lift themselves from the) Tj T*
(ground and attain the advantages of the upright position by clinging) Tj T*
(to supports of various kinds?usually, in a state of nature, the) Tj T*
(stems and boughs of other plants. The means of climbing may be:) Tj T*
(\(1\) by merely leaning upon or propping themselves up by the aid of) Tj T*
(the supporting object?examples, the rose, wistaria, star jessamine) Tj T*
(\(_Jasminum officinalis_\); \(2\) by coiling their main axes spirally) Tj T*
(around the support?hop, bean, morning-glory; \(3\) by means of) Tj T*
(adventitious roots?poison ivy, common English ivy, trumpet vine) Tj T*
(\(_Tecoma radicans_\); \(4\) by organs specially developed for the) Tj T*
(purpose, called tendrils?gourd, cucumber, grape, passion flower.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 98.?Leaf of common pea, showing upper leaflets) Tj T*
(reduced to tendrils.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=97. Tendrils.=?The part assigned to do the work of climbing may be) Tj T*
(a secondary branch, a flower stem, a leafstalk, a leaf, a leaflet,) Tj T*
