(or a group of leaflets \(Fig. 98\). Tendrils behave in general very) Tj T*
(much like twining stems, except that they are more sensitive and) Tj T*
(respond more quickly to any cause that may influence their movement.) Tj T*
(While young, their tips revolve just as do the tips of twining stems,) Tj T*
(until they meet with an object round which they can coil. When this) Tj T*
(happens, not only the part in contact with the object coils, but) Tj T*
(the free part between it and the main axis will usually respond by) Tj T*
(twisting itself into a helix \(Fig. 99\). As the distance between the) Tj T*
(base and tip of the tendril is shortened by coiling, the body of) Tj T*
(the plant is drawn upward proportionally. It will be observed that) Tj T*
(the helix is interrupted at one or more points, above and below) Tj T*
(which the coils turn in opposite directions. This is because the) Tj T*
(tendril is attached at both ends and cannot adjust itself to the) Tj T*
(opposite strains of torsion. Twist with your fingers a piece of tape) Tj T*
(so attached, and you will see that on one side of your hand it turns) Tj T*
(from right to left and on the other from left to right.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=98. The cause of twining.=?Botanists are not fully agreed on this) Tj T*
(point. The explanation most generally accepted at present is that) Tj T*
(the twining of stems is due to the combined action of lateral and) Tj T*
(negative geotropism \(51\). The first causes one side to grow more) Tj T*
(rapidly than the other, thus forming a succession of coils, while) Tj T*
(the second, by stimulating the upward growth of the axis, stretches) Tj T*
(it into a spiral, and in this way draws it more tightly round the) Tj T*
(support. For this reason twining stems do best on an upright support.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 99.?Stems of a passion flower transformed into) Tj T*
(tendrils. \(_After_ GRAY.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In tendrils, the twining is thought to be due not to gravity, but to) Tj T*
(contact with a solid body, which, by inducing unequal development) Tj T*
(on opposite sides of the tendril, causes it to turn about an) Tj T*
(available object. The coiling of the free part of the twining) Tj T*
(organ is in response to the stimulus transmitted from the part in) Tj T*
(contact?_stimulus_, in this sense, denoting the influence of any) Tj T*
(external agent that calls forth a responsive adjustment on the part) Tj T*
(of the plant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 100.?Showing the economy of labor and building) Tj T*
(material effected by the climbing habit. Notice how the grapevine) Tj T*
(coils like an anaconda around the tree boles, and overtops their) Tj T*
(tallest branches. Compare the diameter of the vine with that of the) Tj T*
(trees.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=99. The object of the various habits of stem growth.=?To bring) Tj T*
(the growing parts of the plant into the best possible relations) Tj T*
(with light and air is one of the special functions of the stem, and) Tj T*
(the various habits of growth described in this section have been) Tj T*
(developed with reference to this function. In the case of prostrate) Tj T*
(and underground stems other factors may intervene; can you name some) Tj T*
(of the causes that might influence the position of the stem in such) Tj T*
(cases?) Tj T*
