(          II. MODIFICATIONS OF THE STEM) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?A shoot of asparagus; thorny branches of locust, plum, or) Tj T*
(  haw; a cactus plant; bulbs of lily and hyacinth or onion; tubers of) Tj T*
(  potato; rootstocks of iris, fern, or violet. If fresh specimens are) Tj T*
(  not accessible, dried rootstocks of the sweet flag and Florentine) Tj T*
(  iris may be obtained at the drug stores under the names of calamus) Tj T*
(  and ?orris? root.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=100. How to recognize modified parts.=?Stems, like roots, are often) Tj T*
(modified to serve other than their normal purpose, and in adapting) Tj T*
(themselves to these new functions they sometimes undergo such changes) Tj T*
(of form and structure that it would be impossible to recognize their) Tj T*
(true nature from appearances alone. The safest tests in such cases) Tj T*
(are: \(1\) by a comparison of the parts of the modified structure with) Tj T*
(those of known organs of the same kind; and \(2\) by observing its) Tj T*
(position in reference to other parts. For instance, we know that) Tj T*
(the stem is the part of the plant which normally bears leaves and) Tj T*
(flowers, and if either of these, or if the small scales which often) Tj T*
(take the place of leaves, are found growing on any plant structure,) Tj T*
(we may usually take for granted that it is a stem. Then, again, as) Tj T*
(will be shown in the next chapter, buds and branches naturally appear) Tj T*
(only at the nodes, in or near the _axil_, or inner angle made by a) Tj T*
(leaf with the stem. Hence, if you see any growth springing from such) Tj T*
(a position, you may generally conclude it to be a stem.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 101.?Stem-leaves \(cladophylls\) of a ruscus,) Tj T*
(bearing flowers.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=101. Stems as foliage.=?The connection between stem and leaf is so) Tj T*
(intimate that we need not be surprised to find a frequent interchange) Tj T*
(of function between them, the leaf, or some part of it, doing the) Tj T*
(work of the stem \(Fig. 98\), the stem more often taking upon itself) Tj T*
(the office of the leaf. A common example is the garden asparagus.) Tj T*
(Examine one of the young shoots sold in the market, and notice that) Tj T*
(it bears a number of small scales in place of leaves. On an older) Tj T*
