(=109. Economic value of stems.=?We probably get a greater variety) Tj T*
(of economic products from the stem than from any other part of the) Tj T*
(plant. Consider the vast amount of food stored in underground stems) Tj T*
(like the potato; the resins, gums, and sugar found in the sap of) Tj T*
(plants like the sugar cane, the pine, and India-rubber trees; the) Tj T*
(medicines, dyes, and extracts obtained from the tissues; the valuable) Tj T*
(fibers, such as flax, jute, and hemp, furnished by the bast; the wood) Tj T*
(pulp for making paper; and the timber for building and furnishing) Tj T*
(our houses that we get from the woody trunks of trees. When we think) Tj T*
(of all these things, it seems hardly possible to overestimate the) Tj T*
(importance of this part of the vegetable kingdom to man, or to exert) Tj T*
(ourselves too strenuously to regulate and prevent the destruction of) Tj T*
(these invaluable natural resources.) Tj T*
