() Tj T*
(=113. Growth of monocotyl stems.=?After tracing the course of the) Tj T*
(leaf veins at the nodes of the cornstalk, you will have no difficulty) Tj T*
(in identifying these veins as part of the vascular system. In jointed) Tj T*
(stems like those of the corn and sugar cane and other grasses, their) Tj T*
(intercalation between the vascular bundles of the stem takes place,) Tj T*
(as we have seen, at the nodes, forming the hard rings known as) Tj T*
(joints; but in other monocotyls the fibers entering the stem from the) Tj T*
(leaves usually tend first downward, toward the interior \(Fig. 114\),) Tj T*
(then bend outward, toward the surface, where they become entwined) Tj T*
(with others and form the tough, inseparable cortex that gives to) Tj T*
(palmetto and bamboo stems their great strength. Generally, monocotyl) Tj T*
(stems do not increase in diameter after a certain point, and as) Tj T*
(they can contain only a limited number of vascular fibers, they are) Tj T*
(incapable of supporting an extended system of leaves and branches.) Tj T*
(Hence plants of this class, with a few exceptions, like smilax and) Tj T*
(asparagus, are characterized by simple, columnar stems and a limited) Tj T*
(spread of leaves. Such plant forms are admirably adapted by their) Tj T*
(structure to the purposes of mechanical support. It is a well-known) Tj T*
(law of mechanics that a hollow cylinder is a great deal stronger than) Tj T*
(the same mass would be in solid form, as may easily be tested by the) Tj T*
(simple experiment of breaking in your fingers a cedar pencil and a) Tj T*
(joint of cane or a stem of smilax of the same weight. In stems that) Tj T*
(may be technically classed as solid in structure, like the corn and) Tj T*
(palmetto, the interior is so light compared with the hard epidermis) Tj T*
(that the result is practically a hollow cylinder.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: PLATE 4.?Forest of bamboo, showing the tall, straight,) Tj T*
(branchless habit of monocotyl stems.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=114. Minute study of a monocotyl stem.=?Place under the microscope) Tj T*
(a very thin transverse section of a cornstalk. The little dots that) Tj T*
(looked like the cut ends of threads to the naked eye will now appear) Tj T*
(as the complex group of cells shown in Fig. 115. The same parts are) Tj T*
(shown longitudinally in Fig. 116. As seen in cross section, their) Tj T*
(arrangement suggests a grotesque resemblance to the face of an old) Tj T*
(woman wearing a pair of enormous spectacles and surrounded by a cap) Tj T*
(frill of netting with very wide meshes. These are parenchyma cells,) Tj T*
(_f_, _f_, Fig. 115, and constitute the greater portion of the living) Tj T*
(tissues.) Tj T*
