(The two large openings, _m_, _m?_, that represent the spectacles, are) Tj T*
(ducts for carrying water _up_ the stem. They are called pitted ducts) Tj T*
(on account of the bordered pits which cover their outer surface. The) Tj T*
(two smaller openings between and slightly below the pitted ducts are) Tj T*
(also vessels for carrying liquids up the stem. The lower one, _a_,) Tj T*
(is called the annular _tracheid_ because its tube is strengthened) Tj T*
(by rings on the inside. The upper, smaller one, _sp_, is known as) Tj T*
(the spiral tracheid, because its walls are reinforced by spiral) Tj T*
(thickenings. Can you think what is the use of these strengthening) Tj T*
(contrivances in the walls of conducting cells? \(Suggestion: What is) Tj T*
(the use of the spiral wire on a garden hose?\) The large, irregular) Tj T*
(opening below the ducts is an air space. What is its object? Why has) Tj T*
(it no surrounding wall?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Next look above the ducts for a group of rhomboidal or hexagonal) Tj T*
(cells, _v_, _v_, with smaller ones, _s_, between them. The larger of) Tj T*
(these are _sieve tubes_, the smaller ones, _companion cells_. The) Tj T*
(sieve tubes carry sap _down_ the stem after it has been made into) Tj T*
(food by the leaves. They get their name from the sievelike openings) Tj T*
(between the connecting walls of the cells which form them?as if a row) Tj T*
(of pepper boxes with perforations at both top and bottom were placed) Tj T*
(end to end, so as to form a long tube divided into compartments) Tj T*
(by perforated walls. Can you give a reason why the cells of ducts) Tj T*
(that carry elaborated nutriment should have a more open line of) Tj T*
(communication than those carrying crude sap? [56 \(2\).] Which one of) Tj T*
(the organic food substances was shown by Exp. 39 to be unable, or) Tj T*
(nearly so, to pass through the cell wall by osmosis? [56 \(4\).] The) Tj T*
(conducting cells are surrounded by a mass of strengthening fibers) Tj T*
(separating them from the parenchyma, _f_, and constituting with) Tj T*
(them a _fibrovascular bundle_. The larger vessels, _m_, _m?_, _a_,) Tj T*
(and _sp_, compose the _xylem_, the harder, more woody part of the) Tj T*
(bundle, and the smaller ones, _v_, _s_, the _phloëm_, or softer part.) Tj T*
(Notice also that there is no parenchyma in contact with the xylem) Tj T*
(and phloëm in the fibrovascular bundles of a monocotyl, to supply) Tj T*
(material for new growth, but they are entirely surrounded by a sheath) Tj T*
(of strengthening tissue, whence such bundles are said to be _closed_,) Tj T*
(and are incapable of further growth by the addition of new cells.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
