(The corresponding parts in the two sections are indicated by the) Tj T*
(same letters. Notice the cortex, _R_, on the outside and the pith,) Tj T*
(_M_, on the inside; between these, the cambium, _C_, the _xylem_,) Tj T*
(or woody tissue, included between the radiating lines _X_, and the) Tj T*
(newer tissues composing the _phloëm_ between the lines _P_. The) Tj T*
(cambium and pith, which includes the medullary rays so conspicuous) Tj T*
(in perennial stems, are composed of live parenchyma cells, from) Tj T*
(which alone growth can take place; they are the active part of the) Tj T*
(stem. The xylem contains the large vessels, _t_ and _s_, that convey) Tj T*
(water _up_ the stem, together with the wood fibers, _h_. These are) Tj T*
(the permanent tissues. After completing their growth the cells of) Tj T*
(the xylem gradually lose their protoplasm, and all vitality ceases.) Tj T*
(Even the cell sap disappears, and sometimes the walls of the ducts) Tj T*
(are disintegrated, leaving a mere air space like that shown at _l_) Tj T*
(in Figs. 115 and 116. The dead cells and tissues, however, are by) Tj T*
(no means useless. They constitute the heartwood that is so valuable) Tj T*
(for timber, and serve an important purpose as a mechanical support) Tj T*
(for the stem. The phloëm contains on its outer face a mass of hard) Tj T*
(fibers, _b_, called bast, and toward the interior, the sieve tubes,) Tj T*
(_sb_, with a number of smaller vessels that convey _down_ the stem) Tj T*
(the sap containing the food made in the leaves. It is separated from) Tj T*
(the cortex by the bundle sheath, _e_, and on its other side, from) Tj T*
(the exterior face of the xylem by the cambium, _C_. In this position) Tj T*
(the growing cambium adds new cells to the inner side of the phloëm,) Tj T*
(and to the outer side of the xylem, so that the former grows on its) Tj T*
(inner face and the latter on its outer. In perennial plants, as new) Tj T*
(rings are added to the xylem from season to season, the older ones) Tj T*
(die and are changed into heartwood, which thus gradually increases in) Tj T*
(thickness till in some of the giant redwoods and eucalypti, it may) Tj T*
(attain a diameter of thirty-five or forty feet. In the phloëm, on) Tj T*
(the other hand, as new cells are added from within, the older ones) Tj T*
(are gradually changed into hard bast, _b_, then into bark, and are) Tj T*
(finally sloughed off and fall to the ground. It is this free line of) Tj T*
(communication with the active cambium that enables dicotyl stems to) Tj T*
(grow on indefinitely, the sheath, _e_, being formed on the exterior) Tj T*
(face of the bundles only, leaving the other free, whence they are) Tj T*
(said to be _open_.) Tj T*
