(=120. The cambium layer.=?This is not always easily distinguishable) Tj T*
(with a hand lens, but is conspicuous in the stems of sassafras,) Tj T*
(slippery elm, and aristolochia. If some of these cannot be obtained,) Tj T*
(the presence of the cambium can be recognized by observing the) Tj T*
(tendency of most stems to ?bleed,? when cut, between the wood and) Tj T*
(bark. The reason for this is because the cambium is the active part) Tj T*
(of the stem, in which growth is taking place, and consequently it is) Tj T*
(most abundantly supplied with sap. In spring, especially, it becomes) Tj T*
(so full of sap that if a rod of hickory or elder is pounded, the) Tj T*
(pulpy cambium is broken up and the bark may be slipped off whole from) Tj T*
(the wood.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=121. Medullary rays.=?Observe the whitish, silvery lines that) Tj T*
(radiate in every direction from the center, like the spokes of a) Tj T*
(wheel from the hub. These are the medullary rays, and consist of) Tj T*
(threads of pith that serve as lines of communication between the) Tj T*
(?central cylinder? and the growing cambium layer. In old stems the) Tj T*
(central pith frequently disappears and its office is filled by the) Tj T*
(medullary rays, which become quite conspicuous.) Tj T*
