(=123. The rings= into which the woody cylinder is divided mark the) Tj T*
(yearly additions to the growth of the stem, which increases by the) Tj T*
(constant accession of new material to the outside of the permanent) Tj T*
(tissues \(116\). The cambium constantly advances outward, beginning) Tj T*
(every spring a new season?s growth, and leaving behind the ring of) Tj T*
(ducts and woody fibers made the year before. As the work of the plant) Tj T*
(is most active and its growth most vigorous in spring, the largest) Tj T*
(ducts are formed then, the tissue becoming closer and finer as the) Tj T*
(season advances, thus causing the division into annual rings that is) Tj T*
(so characteristic of woody dicotyl stems. Each new stratum of growth) Tj T*
(is made up of the fibrovascular bundles that supply the leaves and) Tj T*
(buds and branches of the season. In this way we see that the increase) Tj T*
(of dicotyl trunks and branches is approximately in an elongated cone) Tj T*
(\(Fig. 127\), the number of rings gradually diminishing toward the top) Tj T*
(till at the terminal bud of each bough it is reduced to a single one,) Tj T*
(as in the stems of annuals.) Tj T*
