(=126. Direction of the current.=?These experiments show that the) Tj T*
(upward movement of crude sap toward the leaves is mainly through the) Tj T*
(ducts in the woody portion of the stem, while the downward flow of) Tj T*
(elaborated sap from the leaves takes place chiefly through the soft) Tj T*
(bast and certain other vessels of the cortical layer. The action of) Tj T*
(the leaves in giving off part of the water absorbed, as shown in Exp.) Tj T*
(59, probably has also an important influence on the course of sap) Tj T*
(movement. If loss of water takes place in any organ through growth or) Tj T*
(other cause, the osmotic flow of the thinner sap from the roots will) Tj T*
(set in that direction.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=127. Ringing fruit trees.=?The course of the sap explains why) Tj T*
(farmers sometimes hasten the ripening of fruit by the practice of) Tj T*
(_ringing_. As the food material cannot pass below the denuded ring,) Tj T*
(the parts above become gorged, and a process of forcing takes place.) Tj T*
(The practice, however, is not to be commended, except in rare cases,) Tj T*
(as it generally leads to the death of the ringed stem. The portion) Tj T*
(below the ring can receive no nourishment from above, and will) Tj T*
(gradually be so starved that it cannot even act as a carrier of crude) Tj T*
(sap to the leaves, and so the whole bough will perish.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 131.?Diagram showing general movement of sap.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=128. Sap movement not circulation.=?It must not be supposed that) Tj T*
(this flow of sap in plants is analogous to the circulation of the) Tj T*
(blood in animals, though frequently spoken of in popular language) Tj T*
(as the ?circulation of the sap.? There is no central organ like the) Tj T*
(heart to regulate its flow, and the water taken up by the roots does) Tj T*
(not make a continual circuit of the plant body as the blood does of) Tj T*
(ours, but is dispersed by a process of general diffusion, partly into) Tj T*
(the air through the leaves and partly through the plant body as food,) Tj T*
(wherever it is needed. Figure 131 gives a good general idea of the) Tj T*
(movement of sap in trees, the arrows indicating the direction of the) Tj T*
(movement of the different substances.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
