(=139. Forestry has for its object=: \(1\) the preservation and) Tj T*
(cultivation of existing forests; \(2\) the planting of new ones, or) Tj T*
(the reforestation of tracts from which the timber has been destroyed.) Tj T*
(Forests may be either _pure_, that is, composed mainly of one kind of) Tj T*
(tree, as a pine or a fir wood; or _mixed_, being made up of a variety) Tj T*
(of different growths, as are most of our common hardwood forests.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 146.?Oyster fungus on linden.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=140. Enemies of the forest.=-The first step in the preservation of) Tj T*
(our forests is to know the dangers to be guarded against. The chief) Tj T*
(of these are: \(1\) fires; \(2\) the ignorance or recklessness of man in) Tj T*
(cutting for commercial purposes; \(3\) fungi; \(4\) injurious insects;) Tj T*
(\(5\) sheep, hogs, and other animals that eat the seeds and the young,) Tj T*
(tender growth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=141. How to protect the forests.=?The annual destruction of forests) Tj T*
(by fires probably exceeds that from all other causes combined. The) Tj T*
(only effectual safeguard against this danger is watchfulness on the) Tj T*
(part of _everybody_. We can each one of us help in this work by at) Tj T*
(least being careful ourselves never to kindle a fire in the woods) Tj T*
(without taking every precaution against its spreading. A single) Tj T*
(match, or the glowing stump of a cigar, carelessly thrown among dry) Tj T*
(leaves or grass, may start a conflagration that will destroy millions) Tj T*
(of dollars? worth of standing timber.) Tj T*
