(=154. Differences in the branching of trees.=?We are now prepared) Tj T*
(to understand something about the causes of that endless variety in) Tj T*
(the spread of bough and sweep of woody spray that makes the winter) Tj T*
(woods so beautiful. Where the terminal bud is undisputed monarch) Tj T*
(of the bough, as in the pine and fir, or where it is so strong and) Tj T*
(vigorous as to overpower its weaker brethren and keep the lead, as) Tj T*
(in the magnolia, tulip tree, and holly, we have excurrent growth. In) Tj T*
(plants like the oak and apple, where all the buds have a more nearly) Tj T*
(equal chance, the lateral branches show more vigor, and the result is) Tj T*
(either deliquescent growth, or a mixture of the two kinds. In the elm) Tj T*
(and beech, where the usurping pseudo-terminal bud keeps the mastery,) Tj T*
(but does not completely overpower its fellows, we find the long,) Tj T*
(sweeping, delicate spray characteristic of those species. Examine a) Tj T*
(sprig of elm, and notice further that the flower buds are all down) Tj T*
(near the base of the stem, while the leaf buds are near the tip. The) Tj T*
(chief development of the season?s growth is thus thrown toward the) Tj T*
(end of the branch, giving rise to that fine, feathery spray which) Tj T*
(makes the elm an even more beautiful object in winter than in summer) Tj T*
(\(Fig. 158\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An examination of the twigs of other trees will bring out the various) Tj T*
(peculiarities that affect their mode of branching. The angle, for) Tj T*
(instance, which a twig makes with its bough has a great effect in) Tj T*
(shaping the contour of the tree. Compare in this respect the elm and) Tj T*
(hackberry; the tulip tree and willow; ash and hickory. As a general) Tj T*
