(=159. Inflorescence= is a term used to denote the position and) Tj T*
(arrangement of flowers on the stem. It is merely a mode of branching,) Tj T*
(and follows the same laws that govern the branching of ordinary stems.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The stalk that bears a flower is called the _peduncle_. In a cluster) Tj T*
(the main axis is the common peduncle, and the separate flower stalks) Tj T*
(are _pedicels_. A simple leafless flower stalk that rises directly) Tj T*
(from the ground, like those of the dandelion and daffodil, is called) Tj T*
(a _scape_ \(Fig. 165\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 165.?Solitary terminal flower of a lily.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 166.?Indeterminate inflorescence of moneywort.) Tj T*
(\(_After_ GRAY.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=160. Two kinds of inflorescence.=?The growth of flower stems,) Tj T*
(like that of leaf stems, is of two principal kinds, definite and) Tj T*
(indefinite, or, as it is frequently expressed, determinate and) Tj T*
(indeterminate. The simplest kind of each is the solitary, a single) Tj T*
(flower either terminating the main axis, as the tulip, daffodil,) Tj T*
(trillium, magnolia, etc., or springing singly from the axils, as the) Tj T*
(running periwinkle, moneywort, and cotton.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 167.?Raceme of milk vetch \(_Astragalus_\).]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG, 168.?Catkins of aspen.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=161. Indeterminate inflorescence= is always axillary, since the) Tj T*
(production of a terminal flower would stop further growth in that) Tj T*
(direction and thus terminate the development of the axis. The) Tj T*
(_raceme_ is the typical flower cluster of the indefinite sort. In) Tj T*
(such an arrangement the oldest flowers are at the lower nodes,) Tj T*
(new ones appearing only as the axis lengthens and produces new) Tj T*
(internodes. The little scale or _bract_ usually found at the base of) Tj T*
(the pedicel in flower clusters of this sort is a reduced leaf, and) Tj T*
(the fact that the flower stalk springs from the axil shows it to be) Tj T*
(of the essential nature of a branch. When the flowers are sessile) Tj T*
(and crowded on the axis in various degrees, the cluster produced may) Tj T*
(be a _spike_, as seen in the plantain, knotweed, etc., or a _head_,) Tj T*
(like that of the clover, buttonwood, and sycamore. The _catkins_ that) Tj T*
