(form the characteristic inflorescence of most of our forest trees) Tj T*
(are merely pendant spikes. The _corymb_ is a modification of the) Tj T*
(raceme in which the lower pedicels are elongated so as to place their) Tj T*
(flowers on a level with those of the upper nodes, making a convex, or) Tj T*
(more or less flat-topped cluster, as in the wallflower and hawthorn.) Tj T*
(The _umbel_ differs from the corymb in having the pedicels with their) Tj T*
(bracts all gathered at the top of the peduncle, from which they) Tj T*
(spread in every direction like the rays of an umbrella, as the name) Tj T*
(implies. This is the prevalent type of flower cluster in the parsley) Tj T*
(family, which takes its botanical name, _Umbelliferæ_, from its) Tj T*
(characteristic form of inflorescence. The pedicels of an umbel are) Tj T*
(called _rays_, and the circle of bracts at the base of the cluster is) Tj T*
(an _involucre_.) Tj T*
