(=162. Determinate, or cymose, inflorescence.=?In the _cyme_, the) Tj T*
(typical cluster of the determinate kind, the older blossoms in the) Tj T*
(center, being terminal, stop the axis of growth in that direction and) Tj T*
(force the stem, in continuing its growth, to send out side branches) Tj T*
(from the axils of the topmost leaves, in a manner precisely similar) Tj T*
(to the two-forked branching of stems like the horse-chestnut and) Tj T*
(jimson weed. When the older peduncles are lengthened as described) Tj T*
(in 161, a flat-topped cyme is produced, which is distinguished) Tj T*
(from the corymb by its order of flowering, the oldest blossoms) Tj T*
(being at the center, while in the corymb they appear in the reverse) Tj T*
(order. A peculiar form of cyme is found in the scorpioid or coiled) Tj T*
(inflorescence of the pink-root \(_Spigelia_\), heliotrope, comfrey,) Tj T*
(etc. Its structure will be made clear by an inspection of Figs.) Tj T*
(174-176.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 173.?Scorpioid cyme.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=163. The nature of flower stems.=?A comparison of the types of) Tj T*
(inflorescence with the modes of branching in ordinary stems \(144,) Tj T*
(152, 153\) will show a strict correspondence between them. Both bear) Tj T*
(leaves and buds, and the individual flowers of a cluster usually) Tj T*
(spring from the axils of leaves or from bracts, which are merely) Tj T*
(reduced leaves. What, then, is the essential nature of flower stems?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
