(          I. THE TYPICAL LEAF AND ITS PARTS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Leaves of different kinds showing the various modes of) Tj T*
(  attachment, shapes, texture, etc. For stipules, leaves on very) Tj T*
(  young twigs should be selected, as these bodies often fall away) Tj T*
(  soon after the leaves expand. The rose, Japan quince, willow,) Tj T*
(  strawberry, pea, pansy, and young leaves of beech, apple, elm,) Tj T*
(  tulip tree, India rubber tree, magnolia, knotweed, furnish good) Tj T*
(  examples of stipules. For the different orders of leaf arrangement,) Tj T*
(  lilac, maple, spurge, trillium, cleavers \(Galium\) show the opposite) Tj T*
(  and whorled kinds. Elm, basswood, grasses; alder, birch, sedges;) Tj T*
(  peach, apple, cherry, show respectively for each group the three) Tj T*
(  principal orders of alternate arrangement.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=165. Parts of the leaf.=?Examine a young, healthy leaf of apple,) Tj T*
(quince, or elm, as it stands upon the stem, and notice that it) Tj T*
(consists of three parts: a broad expansion called the _blade_; a) Tj T*
(leaf stalk or _petiole_ that attaches it to the stem; and two little) Tj T*
(leaflike or bristle-like bodies at the base, known as _stipules_.) Tj T*
(Make a sketch of any leaf provided with all these parts, and label) Tj T*
(them, respectively, blade, petiole, and stipules. These three parts) Tj T*
(make up a perfect or typical leaf, but as a matter of fact, one or) Tj T*
(more of them is usually wanting.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
