() Tj T*
(=166. Stipules.=?The office of stipules, when present, is generally) Tj T*
(to subserve in some way the purposes of protection. In many cases,) Tj T*
(as in the fig, elm, beech, oak, magnolia, etc., they appear only as) Tj T*
(protective scales that cover the bud during winter, and fall away as) Tj T*
(soon as the leaf expands. When _persistent_, that is, enduring, they) Tj T*
(take various forms according to the purposes they serve. But under) Tj T*
(whatever guise they occur, their true nature may be recognized by) Tj T*
(their position on each side of the base of the petiole, and not in) Tj T*
(the _axil_, or angle formed by the leaf with the stem. \(149.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 179.?Adnate stipules of clover. FIG. 180.?Leaves) Tj T*
(of smilax, showing stipular tendrils. FIG. 181.?Leafy stipules of) Tj T*
(Japan quince.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=167. Leaf attachment.=?The normal use of the petiole is to secure a) Tj T*
(better light exposure for the leaves, but, like other parts, it is) Tj T*
(subject to modifications, and is often wanting altogether. In this) Tj T*
(case the leaf is said to be _sessile_, that is, _seated_, on the) Tj T*
(stem, and the leaf bases are designated by various terms descriptive) Tj T*
(of their mode of attachment. The meaning of these terms, when not) Tj T*
(self-explanatory, can best be learned by a comparison of living) Tj T*
(specimens with Figs. 184-187.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=168. Arrangement of leaves on the stem.=?The mode of attachment is) Tj T*
(something quite distinct from the mode of leaf arrangement on the) Tj T*
(stem, or _phyllotaxy_, as it is termed by botanists. It was seen in) Tj T*
(148 that this takes place in two different ways, the alternate and) Tj T*
(opposite. These two kinds of arrangement represent the principal) Tj T*
(forms of leaf disposition on the stem, the different varieties of) Tj T*
(each depending on the manner in which the leaves are distributed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 182-187.?Petioles, and leaf attachment: 182,) Tj T*
(petioles of jasmine nightshade \(_Solanum jasminoides_\) acting as) Tj T*
(tendrils; 183, acacia, showing petiole transformed to leaf blade;) Tj T*
(184, sessile leaves of epilobium; 185, clasping leaf of lactuca; 186,) Tj T*
(perfoliate leaves of uvularia; 187, peltate leaf of tropæolum. \(182) Tj T*
(and 186 _after_ GRAY.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Where three or more occur at a node, as in the trillium and cleavers) Tj T*
(\(_Galium_\), they constitute a whorl, which is only a variant of the) Tj T*
(opposite arrangement. There is no limit to the number of leaves that) Tj T*
(may be in a whorl except the space around the stem to accommodate) Tj T*
(them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The phyllotaxy of alternate leaves is more complicated. The) Tj T*
(different forms are characterized by the angular distance between the) Tj T*
(points of leaf insertion around the stem. In the elm, basswood, and) Tj T*
(most grasses, they are distributed in two rows or ranks on opposite) Tj T*
