(          II. THE VEINING AND LOBING OF LEAVES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Leaves of any monocotyl and dicotyl will show the) Tj T*
(  difference between parallel and net-veining. To illustrate the) Tj T*
(  palmate and pinnate kinds, the leaves of grasses and arums may be) Tj T*
(  used for monocotyls, and for dicotyls, those of ivy, maple, grape,) Tj T*
(  elm, peach, cherry, etc.; for division, examine lobed and compound) Tj T*
(  leaves of as many kinds as are attainable. A specimen showing each) Tj T*
(  kind of veining should be placed in coloring fluid a short time) Tj T*
(  before the lesson begins. The leafstalks of celery and plantain) Tj T*
(  are excellent for showing the relation between the leaf veins and) Tj T*
(  vascular system of the plant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=171. Parallel and net veining.=?Compare a leaf of the wandering) Tj T*
(Jew, lily, or any kind of grass, with one of grape, ivy, or willow.) Tj T*
(Hold each up to the light, and note the veins or little threads of) Tj T*
(woody substance that run through it. Make a drawing of each so as to) Tj T*
(show plainly the direction and manner of veining. Write under the) Tj T*
(first, _parallel-veined_, and under the second, _net-veined_. This) Tj T*
(distinction of leaves into parallel and net-veined corresponds with) Tj T*
(the two great classes into which seed-bearing plants are divided,) Tj T*
(monocotyls, as a general thing, being characterized by the first) Tj T*
