(=172. Pinnate and palmate veining.=?Next, compare a leaf of the) Tj T*
(canna, calla lily, or any kind of arum, with one of the elm, peach,) Tj T*
(cherry, etc. What resemblances do you notice between the two? What) Tj T*
(differences? Which is parallel-veined and which is net-veined? Make) Tj T*
(a drawing of each, and compare with the first two. Notice that in) Tj T*
(leaves of this kind, the petiole is continued in a large central) Tj T*
(vein, called the _midrib_, from which the secondary veins branch off) Tj T*
(on either side like the pinnæ of a feather; whence such leaves are) Tj T*
(said to be _pinnately_, or _feather_ veined, as in Figs. 206, 207. In) Tj T*
(the cotton, maple, ivy, etc., on the other hand, the petiole breaks) Tj T*
(up at the base of the leaf \(Fig. 208\) into a number of primary) Tj T*
(veins or ribs, which radiate in all directions like the fingers from) Tj T*
(the palm of the hand; hence, such a leaf is said to be _palmately_) Tj T*
(veined. Net-veined leaves?the plantain \(Fig. 209\), wild smilax,) Tj T*
(beech, dogwood?are sometimes ribbed in a way that might lead an) Tj T*
(inexperienced observer to confound them with parallel-veined ones,) Tj T*
(but the reticulations between the ribs show that they belong to the) Tj T*
(net-veined class.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 209.?Ribbed leaf of plantain.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=173. Veins as a mechanical support.=?Hold up a stiff, firm leaf) Tj T*
(of any kind, like the magnolia, holly, or India rubber, to the) Tj T*
(light, having first scraped away a little of the under surface, and) Tj T*
(examine it with a lens. Compare it with one of softer texture, like) Tj T*
(the peach, maple, or clover. In which are the veins the closer and) Tj T*
(stronger? Which is the more easily torn and wilted? Tear a blade of) Tj T*
(grass longitudinally and then cross-wise; in which direction does it) Tj T*
(give way the more readily? Tear apart gently a leaf of maple, or ivy,) Tj T*
(and one of elm or other pinnately veined plant; in which direction) Tj T*
(does each give way with least resistance? What would you judge from) Tj T*
(these facts as to the mechanical use of the veins?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=174. Effect upon shape.=?By comparing a number of leaves of each) Tj T*
(kind it will be seen that the feather-veined ones tend to assume) Tj T*
(elongated outlines \(Figs. 197, 207\); the palmate-veined ones, broad) Tj T*
(and rounded forms \(Figs. 195, 208\). Notice also that the straight,) Tj T*
(unbroken venation of parallel-veined leaves is generally accompanied) Tj T*
(by smooth, unbroken margins, while the irregular, open meshes of) Tj T*
(net-veined leaves are favorable to breaks and indentations.) Tj T*
