(=175. Veins as water carriers.=?Examine a leaf from a stem that has) Tj T*
(stood in red ink for an hour or two. Do you see evidence that it has) Tj T*
(absorbed any of the liquid? Cut across the blade and examine with a) Tj T*
(lens. What course has the absorbed liquid followed? What use does) Tj T*
(this indicate for the veins, besides the one already noted? Observe) Tj T*
(the point of insertion on the stem, and examine the scar with a lens:) Tj T*
(do you see any evidence of a connection between the leaf veins and) Tj T*
(the fibrovascular bundles of the stem? \(111, 125, 126.\) Notice where) Tj T*
(and how the veins end. Are they of the same size all the way, or do) Tj T*
(they grow smaller toward the tip? Are they separate and distinct, or) Tj T*
(are they connected throughout their ramifications, like the veins and) Tj T*
(arteries of the human body? How do you know? Do you see any of the) Tj T*
(coloring fluid in the small reticulations between the veins? How did) Tj T*
(it get there?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=176. The nature and office of veins.=?We learn from 173 and 175 that) Tj T*
(the veining serves two important purposes in the economy of the leaf:) Tj T*
(first, as a skeleton or framework, to support the expanded blade;) Tj T*
(and second, as a system of water pipes, for conveying the sap out) Tj T*
(of which its food is manufactured. In other words the veins are a) Tj T*
(continuation of the fibrovascular bundles into the leaves, by means) Tj T*
(of which the latter are put in communication with the body of the) Tj T*
(plant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=177. The relation between veining and lobing.=?Compare the outline) Tj T*
(of a leaf of maple or ivy with one of oak or chrysanthemum. Do) Tj T*
(you perceive any correspondence between the manner of lobing or) Tj T*
(indentation of their margins, and the direction of the veins? \(Figs.) Tj T*
(210, 211.\) To what class would you refer each one?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The lobes themselves may be variously cut, as in the fennel and rose) Tj T*
(geranium, thus giving rise to twice-cleft, thrice-cleft \(Fig. 212\),) Tj T*
(four-cleft, or even still more intricately divided blades.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=178. Compound leaves.=?Compare with the specimens just examined) Tj T*
(a leaf of horse-chestnut, clover, or Virginia creeper, and one) Tj T*
(of rose, black locust, or vetch. Notice that each of these last is) Tj T*
(made up of entirely separate divisions or leaflets, thus forming a) Tj T*
(_compound leaf_. Notice also that the two kinds of compound leaves) Tj T*
(correspond to the two kinds of veining and lobing, so that we) Tj T*
(have palmately and pinnately compound ones. In pinnate leaves the) Tj T*
(continuation of the common petiole along which the leaflets are) Tj T*
(ranged is called the _rhachis_.) Tj T*
