(          III. TRANSPIRATION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Leafy twigs of actively growing young plants. Sunflower,) Tj T*
(  corn, peach, grape, calla, and arums in general transpire rapidly;) Tj T*
(  thick-leaved evergreens and hairy or rough species, like mullein) Tj T*
(  and horehound more slowly. For Exp. 63, small-leaved, large-leaved,) Tj T*
(  and thick-leaved kinds will be needed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  APPLIANCES.?Glass jars and bottles with air-tight stoppers; a) Tj T*
(  little vaseline, oil, gardener?s wax, thread, cardboard, and a pair) Tj T*
(  of scales.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 62. TO SHOW WHY LEAVES WITHER.?Dry two self-sealing jars) Tj T*
(  thoroughly, by holding them over a stove or a lighted lamp for a) Tj T*
(  short time to prevent ?sweating.? Place in one a freshly cut leafy) Tj T*
(  sprig of any kind, leaving the other empty. Seal both jars and set) Tj T*
(  them in the shade. Place beside them, but without covering of any) Tj T*
(  kind, a twig similar to the one in the jar. Both twigs should have) Tj T*
(  been cut at the same time, and their cut ends covered with wax or) Tj T*
(  vaseline, to prevent access of air. Look at intervals to see if) Tj T*
(  there is any moisture deposited on the inside of either jar. If) Tj T*
(  there is none, set them both in a refrigerator or cover with a wet) Tj T*
(  cloth and allow to cool for half an hour, and then examine again.) Tj T*
(  In which jar is there a greater deposit of dew? How do you account) Tj T*
(  for it? Take the twig out of the jar and compare its leaves with) Tj T*
(  those of the one left outside; which have withered the more, and) Tj T*
(  why?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 63. TO MEASURE THE RATE AT WHICH WATER IS GIVEN OFF BY) Tj T*
(  LEAVES OF DIFFERENT KINDS.?Fill three glass vessels of the same) Tj T*
(  size with water and cover with oil to prevent evaporation. Insert) Tj T*
(  into one the end of a healthy twig of peach or cherry; into the) Tj T*
(  second a twig of catalpa, grape, or any large-leaved plant, and) Tj T*
(  into the third, one of magnolia, holly, or other thick-leaved) Tj T*
(  evergreen, letting the stems of all reach well down into the water.) Tj T*
(  Care must be taken to select twigs of approximately the same size) Tj T*
(  and age, since the absorbent properties of very young stems are) Tj T*
(  more injured by cutting and exposure than those of older ones. All) Tj T*
(  specimens should be cut under water as directed in Exp. 58. Weigh) Tj T*
(  all three vessels, and at the end of twenty-four hours, weigh) Tj T*
(  again, taking note of the quantity of liquid that has disappeared) Tj T*
(  from each glass. This will represent approximately the amount) Tj T*
(  absorbed by the leaves from the twigs to replace that given off.) Tj T*
(  Which twig has lost most? Which least? Note the condition of the) Tj T*
(  leaves on the different twigs; have they all absorbed water about) Tj T*
(  as rapidly as they have lost it? How do you know this? Pluck the) Tj T*
(  leaves from each twig, one by one, lay them on a flat surface) Tj T*
(  that has been previously measured off, into square inches or) Tj T*
(  centimeters, and thus form a rough estimate of the area covered by) Tj T*
