(  may be equally exposed. The leaves must be all of the same species,) Tj T*
(  and as nearly as possible of the same age, size, and vigor, and) Tj T*
(  care must be taken that none of the vaseline is rubbed off in) Tj T*
(  handling. Examine at intervals of a few hours. Which of the leaves) Tj T*
(  withers soonest? Which keeps fresh longest? From what part would) Tj T*
(  you conclude, judging by this experiment, that the water escapes) Tj T*
(  most rapidly?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=179. Transpiration, nutrition, and growth.=?We learn from the) Tj T*
(foregoing, and from Exps. 58 and 59, that plants give off moisture) Tj T*
(very much as animals do by perspiration. The two processes must not) Tj T*
(be classed together, however, for they are physiologically different.) Tj T*
(The action, in plants, is called _transpiration_. It is usually) Tj T*
(assumed that a large amount of water must pass through the plant) Tj T*
(in order to bring to it the necessary supply of food material; but) Tj T*
(since the entrance of mineral salts is brought about by osmosis,) Tj T*
(conditioned by the living cells of the root; and since osmosis) Tj T*
(of salts may take place in a direction opposite to that of the) Tj T*
(greater movement of water, it follows that the entrance of salts is) Tj T*
(independent of transpiration.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Inasmuch, however, as a certain amount of water is necessary to bring) Tj T*
(the living cells into a condition of turgor \(7\) so that they may) Tj T*
(grow, it follows that there is a relation between transpiration and) Tj T*
(growth. If transpiration exceeds absorption for any length of time,) Tj T*
(the tissues will be depleted of their moisture, as is shown by the) Tj T*
(wilting of crops in dry, hot weather; and if the unequal movement) Tj T*
(continues long enough, the plant will die. Hence, a knowledge of the) Tj T*
(laws governing this important function is necessary to all who are) Tj T*
(interested in cultivating agricultural products.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 217.?A ?weeping tree,? showing the effect where) Tj T*
(absorption exceeds transpiration. Notice the position of the tree) Tj T*
(near the water where the roots have unlimited moisture. \(_After_) Tj T*
(FRANCÉ.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=180. Magnitude of the work of transpiration.=?Few people have any) Tj T*
(idea of the enormous quantities of water given off by leaves. It) Tj T*
(has been calculated that a healthy oak may have as many as 700,000) Tj T*
(leaves, and that 111,225 kilograms of water?equal to about 244,700) Tj T*
(pounds?may pass from its surface in the five active months from June) Tj T*
