(As the water given off by transpiration is in the form of vapor,) Tj T*
(it must draw from the plant the amount of heat necessary for its) Tj T*
(vaporization, and thus has the effect of making the leaves and the) Tj T*
(air in contact with them cooler than the surrounding medium. At the) Tj T*
(same time the coolness and moisture of the air tend to check the) Tj T*
(loss by evaporation from the surface soil. It is partly to this) Tj T*
(cause, and not alone to their shade, that the coolness of forests is) Tj T*
(due. Measurements at various weather bureau stations in the United) Tj T*
(States show that in summer the temperature of oak woods is 4° C.) Tj T*
(lower during the day than in the open, and as much higher at night.) Tj T*
(In a beech wood in Germany the difference between the forest and the) Tj T*
(general temperature amounted to as much as 7° C.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
