() Tj T*
(=181. Stomata.=?It was shown in Exp. 64 that the water of) Tj T*
(transpiration escapes most rapidly, as a general thing, from the) Tj T*
(under surface of leaves. To find out why this is so, a careful study) Tj T*
(of the epidermis will be necessary. For this purpose procure, if) Tj T*
(possible, the leaf of a white garden lily \(_Lilium album_\), wandering) Tj T*
(Jew, Spanish bayonet \(_Yucca aloifolia_\), anemone, narcissus, iris,) Tj T*
(or canna. The first-named is preferable, as the transpiration pores) Tj T*
(can be seen on it with the naked eye. Examine the under surface) Tj T*
(with a hand lens, and you will see that it is covered with small) Tj T*
(eye-shaped dots like those shown in Figs. 218 and 219. Strip off) Tj T*
(a portion of the epidermis, hold it up to the light on a piece of) Tj T*
(moistened glass, and they can be seen quite clearly with a lens.) Tj T*
(These dots are the pores through which the water vapor escapes in) Tj T*
(transpiration, and through which air finds its way into the tissues) Tj T*
(of the leaf. They are called _stomata_ \(sing., _stoma_\), from a Greek) Tj T*
(word meaning ?a mouth.? Look for stomata on the upper epidermis; do) Tj T*
(you find any, and if so, are there as many as on the under surface?) Tj T*
(Do you see any relation between this fact and the results obtained) Tj T*
(from Exp. 64? Can you see any good reasons why the stomata should be) Tj T*
(placed on the under side in preference to the upper? Are they as much) Tj T*
(exposed to excessive light and heat, or as liable to be choked by) Tj T*
(dust, rain, and dew here as on the upper side?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
