([Illustration: FIG. 221.?Under epidermis of an oat leaf, showing) Tj T*
(stomata.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=183. Minute study of a leaf epidermis.=?Place a bit of the lower) Tj T*
(epidermis of a leaf under the microscope, and examine with a high) Tj T*
(power. It will appear, if a monocotyl, to be composed of long, flat,) Tj T*
(rectangular spaces \(Fig. 221\); if the leaf of a dicotyl is used,) Tj T*
(they will be more or less irregular \(Fig. 220\), with the outlines) Tj T*
(fitting into each other like the tiling of a floor or the blocks of a) Tj T*
(Chinese puzzle. These spaces are the cells of the epidermis, and the) Tj T*
(lines are the cell walls. Can you find any of the cell contents? The) Tj T*
(cell sap is not often visible; do you see the nuclei? Can you give) Tj T*
(a reason why the epidermal cells are so thin and flat? Between some) Tj T*
(of the cells you will see two kidney-shaped bodies placed with their) Tj T*
(concave sides together so as to leave a lenticular opening between) Tj T*
(them. This is a _stoma_, and the kidney-shaped bodies \(Figs. 218,) Tj T*
(219\) are _guard cells_. They are given this name because they open) Tj T*
(or close the mouth of the stoma. If you will imagine a toy balloon) Tj T*
(made in the form of a hollow ring, like the tire of a bicycle, you) Tj T*
(can easily see, from Figs. 218, 219, that when the ring is strongly) Tj T*
(inflated, it will expand, and in enlarging its own circumference,) Tj T*
(will at the same time increase the diameter of the opening in the) Tj T*
(center. When the expansive force is removed, it collapses, thus) Tj T*
(closing, or greatly reducing, the aperture.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 222.?Outline of a stoma of hellebore in vertical) Tj T*
(section. The darker lines show the shape assumed by the guard cells) Tj T*
(when the stoma is open; the lighter lines, when the stoma is closed.) Tj T*
(The cavities of the guard cells with the stoma closed are shaded, and) Tj T*
(are distinctly smaller than when the stoma is open.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the same way the guard cells, when there is abundance of water in) Tj T*
(them, expand, thus opening the stoma so that the water vapor passes) Tj T*
(out more readily. But when there is a dearth of moisture, or when,) Tj T*
(by reason of chemical action in the soil, the roots fail to supply) Tj T*
(it, the leaves wilt, the guard cells, losing their water, collapse,) Tj T*
(closing the pore, and transpiration is thus prevented or greatly) Tj T*
(retarded. \(Fig. 222.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
