(  aquatic plant; bean or tropæolum, or other green leaves gathered) Tj T*
(  from plants growing in the sunshine; a healthy potted plant; a) Tj T*
(  small, fresh cutting.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  APPLIANCES.?A shallow dish of water and two glass tumblers or) Tj T*
(  wide-mouthed jars; a bent glass or rubber tube; a piece of black) Tj T*
(  cloth or paper; a half pint of alcohol; iodine solution; a glass) Tj T*
(  funnel or a long-necked bottle from which the bottom has been) Tj T*
(  removed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 65. IS THERE ANY RELATION BETWEEN SUNLIGHT AND THE GREEN) Tj T*
(  COLOR OF LEAVES??Place a seedling of oats, or other rapidly growing) Tj T*
(  shoot, in the dark for a few days, and note its loss of color.) Tj T*
(  Leave it in the dark indefinitely, and it will lose all color and) Tj T*
(  die. Hence we may conclude that there is some intimate connection) Tj T*
(  between the action of light and the green coloring matter of leaves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 66. DO LEAVES GIVE OFF ANYTHING ELSE BESIDES) Tj T*
(  WATER??Submerge a green water plant, with the cut end uppermost,) Tj T*
(  in a glass vessel full of water, and invert over it a glass) Tj T*
(  funnel, or a long-necked bottle from which the bottom has been) Tj T*
(  removed as directed in Exp. 53. Expel the air from the neck of the) Tj T*
(  funnel?or bottle?by submerging and corking under water so as to) Tj T*
(  make it air-tight. Place in the sunlight and notice the bubbles) Tj T*
(  that begin to rise from the cut end of the plant. When they have) Tj T*
(  partly filled the neck of the funnel, remove the stopper and thrust) Tj T*
(  in a glowing splinter. If it bursts into flame, or glows more) Tj T*
(  brightly, what is the gas that was given off? \(Exp. 22.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  As oxygen is not a product of respiration, some other process must) Tj T*
(  be at work here, during which oxygen is set free, and some other) Tj T*
(  substance used up. \(Exps. 24 and 25.\)) Tj T*
