() Tj T*
(=185. Influence of plants on the atmosphere.=?These experiments) Tj T*
(show that leaves cannot do their work without light and air. The) Tj T*
(particular element of the atmosphere used by them in the process of) Tj T*
(food making is carbon dioxide. Their action in absorbing this gas) Tj T*
(and giving off oxygen tends to counterbalance the opposite action of) Tj T*
(respiration, decomposition, and combustion of all kinds, by which the) Tj T*
(proportion of it in the atmosphere tends to be constantly increased.) Tj T*
(In this way they help to regulate the quantity of it present and have) Tj T*
(a beneficial effect in ridding the air of one source of impurity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=186. Photosynthesis.=?In our examination of the internal structure) Tj T*
(of the leaf, the chlorophyll bodies \(184\) were found to contain small) Tj T*
(granules of starch which the chlorophyll, under the stimulus of) Tj T*
(light, had elaborated as a nutriment for the plant tissues. Hence,) Tj T*
(the leaf may be regarded as a factory in which vegetable food,) Tj T*
(mainly starch, is manufactured out of the water brought up from the) Tj T*
(soil, and the carbon dioxide derived through the stomata from the) Tj T*
(atmosphere. In this process carbon dioxide \(CO_{2}\) is combined) Tj T*
(with water \(H_{2}O\) in such proportions that part of the oxygen is) Tj T*
(returned to the surrounding air. This is a fundamental food-forming) Tj T*
(process characteristic of green plants, and can take place only in) Tj T*
(the light. For this reason it has been named _Photosynthesis_, a word) Tj T*
(which means ?building up by means of light,? just as _photography_) Tj T*
(means ?drawing or engraving by means of light.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In carrying on the operation of photosynthesis, sunshine is the) Tj T*
(power, the chlorophyll bodies the working machinery, carbon dioxide) Tj T*
(and water the raw materials, and starch or oil the finished product,) Tj T*
(while oxygen and the water of transpiration represent the waste or) Tj T*
(by-products.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=187. How the new combination is effected.=?It may seem strange that) Tj T*
(a gas and a liquid should combine to make something so different) Tj T*
(from either as starch, but their chemical constituents are the same) Tj T*
(in different proportions. Water is made up of 2 parts hydrogen and) Tj T*
(1 part oxygen; carbon dioxide, of 1 part carbon and 2 parts oxygen,) Tj T*
(while starch contains carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen, in the ratios of) Tj T*
(6, 10, and 5, respectively. Hence, by taking sufficient quantities) Tj T*
(of water and carbon dioxide and combining them in the proper) Tj T*
(proportions, the leaf factory can turn them into starch. If we use) Tj T*
(the letters C, H, and O, to represent Carbon, Hydrogen, and Oxygen,) Tj T*
(respectively, the new combination of materials can be expressed by an) Tj T*
(equation; thus:?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  _water_    _carbon dioxide_      _starch_            _by-products_) Tj T*
(  5\(H_{2}O\)  + 6\(CO_{2}\)       = \(C_{6}H_{10}O_{5}\) +  6\(O_{2}\) = 12\(O\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
