() Tj T*
(Simple as the operation appears, the chemist has not, as yet,) Tj T*
(been able to imitate it. He can analyze starch into its original) Tj T*
(constituents, but while he has the ingredients at hand in abundance,) Tj T*
(and knows the exact proportions of their combination, it is beyond) Tj T*
(his power, in the present state of our knowledge, to put them) Tj T*
(together. Hence, both man and the lower animals are dependent on) Tj T*
(plants for this most important food element. The so-called factories) Tj T*
(that supply the starch of commerce do not _make_ starch any more than) Tj T*
(the miller makes wheat, but merely separate and render available for) Tj T*
(use that already elaborated by plants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=188. Proteins.=?Foods of this class are mainly instrumental in) Tj T*
(furnishing material for the growth and repair of the tissues out) Tj T*
(of which the bodies of both plants and animals are built up. They) Tj T*
(embrace a great variety of substances, but their chemical nature) Tj T*
(is very complex and very imperfectly understood. Nitrogen is an) Tj T*
(important element in their composition, whence they are commonly) Tj T*
(distinguished as ?nitrogenous foods.? Besides nitrogen, there are) Tj T*
(present carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, and sulphur, and traces of the) Tj T*
(mineral salts absorbed from the soil are found in varying quantities) Tj T*
(in the ash of different proteins. The percentages in which these) Tj T*
(ingredients are combined and the processes concerned in their) Tj T*
(formation are at present a matter of pure hypothesis. Botanists) Tj T*
(are not agreed even as to whether they are made in the leaf or in) Tj T*
(some other part or parts of the plant, though the weight of opinion) Tj T*
(inclines to the view that their construction takes place in the leaf.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=189. The activities of leaves.=?As there are only 4 parts of CO_{2}) Tj T*
(to every 10,000 parts of ordinary free air, it has been estimated) Tj T*
(that in order to supply the leaf factory with the raw material it) Tj T*
(needs, an active leaf surface of one square meter?a little over) Tj T*
(one square yard?uses up, during every hour of sunshine, the CO_{2}) Tj T*
(contained in 1000 liters \(1000 quarts, approximately\) of air. Suppose) Tj T*
(an oak tree to bear 500,000 leaves, each having a surface of 16 sq.) Tj T*
(cm., or 4 sq. in., and working 12 hours a day for 6 months in the) Tj T*
(year; you will then have some idea of the enormous quantity of air) Tj T*
(that passes each season through its leaf system. Add to this the) Tj T*
(almost incredible volume of water transpired in the same time \(180\),) Tj T*
(and we may well stand amazed at the tremendous activities of these) Tj T*
(silent workers that we are in the habit of regarding as mere passive) Tj T*
(elements in the general landscape.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
