(          VI. THE LEAF AN ORGAN OF RESPIRATION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?A number of vigorous, freshly cut green leaves; a liter) Tj T*
(  or two \(one or two quarts\) of expanding flower or leaf buds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  APPLIANCES.?Some wide-mouthed jars of one or two liters? capacity;) Tj T*
(  two small open vials of limewater.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 72. DO LEAVES GIVE OFF CARBON DIOXIDE??Cover the) Tj T*
(  bottoms of two wide-mouthed jars with water about two centimeters) Tj T*
(  \(1 inch\) deep. Place in one a number of healthy green leaves with) Tj T*
(  their stalks in the water, and insert among them a small open) Tj T*
(  vial containing limewater. In the other jar place only a vial of) Tj T*
(  limewater in the clear water at the bottom, this last being merely) Tj T*
(  to make the conditions in both vessels the same. Seal both tight) Tj T*
(  and keep together in the dark for about 48 hours, and then examine.) Tj T*
(  In which jar does the limewater indicate the greater accumulation) Tj T*
(  of CO_{2}? \(It may show a slight milkiness in the other vessel) Tj T*
(  due to gas derived from the inclosed air and water.\) From this) Tj T*
(  experiment, what process would you conclude has been going on among) Tj T*
(  the leaves in jar No. 1? \(Exp. 25.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
