(and carbon dioxide are given off by leaf buds.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 73. IS THE EXHALATION OF CARBON DIOXIDE ACCOMPANIED) Tj T*
(  BY ANY OTHER CONCOMITANT OF RESPIRATION??In Exps. 24, 25, it was) Tj T*
(  shown that respiration is accompanied by heat; hence, if the) Tj T*
(  production of carbon dioxide by the leaf is due to this cause, it) Tj T*
(  should be attended by the evolution of heat. To find out whether) Tj T*
(  this is the case, partly fill a glass jar of two liters? capacity) Tj T*
(  with unfolding leaf buds arranged in layers alternating with damp) Tj T*
(  cotton batting or blotting paper \(Fig. 228\); close the jar tightly) Tj T*
(  and leave from 12 to 24 hours in the dark to prevent the action) Tj T*
(  of photosynthesis. Then insert a thermometer and note the rise in) Tj T*
(  temperature. If a lighted taper is plunged in, it will quickly be) Tj T*
(  extinguished, showing that respiration has been going on.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=191. Respiration in leaves.=?We see from experiments like the) Tj T*
(foregoing that the leaf, besides carrying on the functions of) Tj T*
(digestion, photosynthesis, and transpiration, is also an active agent) Tj T*
(in the work of respiration. In this function oxygen is used up and) Tj T*
(carbon dioxide given off, just as in the respiration of animals; but) Tj T*
(the process is so slow in plants that it is much more difficult to) Tj T*
(detect than the contrary action in photosynthesis, and is, in fact,) Tj T*
(not perceptible at all while the latter is going on, though it does) Tj T*
(not cease even then.) Tj T*
