(But while the leaf is the principal organ of respiration, the process) Tj T*
(is carried on in other parts of the plant as well, else it could not) Tj T*
(survive during the leafless months of winter. It _appears_ to be most) Tj T*
(active at night, but this is only because it is not obscured then,) Tj T*
(as during the day, by the more active function of photosynthesis.) Tj T*
(Indeed, it was for a long time supposed that plants ?breathed? only) Tj T*
(at night, and it was thought to be unwholesome to keep them in a) Tj T*
(bedroom. It is now known, however, that respiration goes on at all) Tj T*
(times and in all living parts of the plant, but the quantity of) Tj T*
(oxygen taken in is so small from a hygienic point of view that it may) Tj T*
(be disregarded.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=192. Distinctions between respiration and photosynthesis.=?While) Tj T*
(these two functions are contrasting and antipodal, so to speak, in) Tj T*
(their action, they are mutually complementary and interdependent, the) Tj T*
(one manufacturing food and the other using it up, or rather marking) Tj T*
(the activity of those life processes by which it is used up. The) Tj T*
(difference between them will be made clear by a comparison of the two) Tj T*
(processes as summarized in the following statement:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(           PHOTOSYNTHESIS                        RESPIRATION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Goes on only in sunlight and in      Goes on at all times and in all) Tj T*
(  the green parts of plants.           parts of the plant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Produces starch and sugar.           Releases energy \(heat and working) Tj T*
(                                       power\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Gives off, as by-product, oxygen.    Gives off, as by-products, CO?) Tj T*
(                                       and water.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  A constructive process, in which     A destructive, or consumptive) Tj T*
(  energy is used up to make food.      process, in which food is used up) Tj T*
(                                       in expending energy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=193. Metabolism.=?The total of all the life processes of plants,) Tj T*
(including growth, waste, repair, etc., is summed up under the general) Tj T*
(term _metabolism_. It is a _constructive_ or building-up process when) Tj T*
(it results in the making of new tissues out of food material absorbed) Tj T*
(from the earth and air, and the consequent increase of the plant in) Tj T*
(size or numbers. But, as in the case of animals, so with plants, not) Tj T*
(all the food provided is converted into new tissue, part being used) Tj T*
(as a source of energy, and part decomposed and excreted as waste.) Tj T*
(In this sense, metabolism is said to be _destructive_. The waste in) Tj T*
(healthy growing plants is always, of course, less than the gain,) Tj T*
(and a portion of the food material is laid by as a reserve store.) Tj T*
(For this reason, photosynthesis, being a constructive process, is) Tj T*
(usually more energetic than respiration, which is the measure of the) Tj T*
(destructive change of materials that attends all life processes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
