(=200. Night and day adjustments.=?These are movements in response to) Tj T*
(changes in the degree of illumination and temperature, as evidenced) Tj T*
(by the fact that they become feeble and soon cease altogether if) Tj T*
(the plant is kept a sufficient time under uniform conditions as) Tj T*
(to these two factors. \(Exp. 74.\) They are called ?nyctitropic? or) Tj T*
(sleep movements, because they are most obvious in certain plants) Tj T*
(that undergo periodic adjustments to the alternations of day and) Tj T*
(night suggestive of an imaginary likeness to the sleep of animals.) Tj T*
(Examples are most frequently met with among members of the pea) Tj T*
(family \(_Leguminosæ_\), the spurges \(_Euphorbiaceæ_\), and the sorrel) Tj T*
(\(_Oxalis_\) family. They are found among other species also, and) Tj T*
(indeed are much more general than is usually supposed, most plants) Tj T*
(showing signs of them if carefully tested. A simple way of doing) Tj T*
(this is by attaching bristles about two inches long to the tips of) Tj T*
(two leaves on opposite sides of the stem, as in Figs. 240, 241,) Tj T*
(and comparing the divergence of the bristles during the day and at) Tj T*
(nightfall. In this way a change of position in the leaves, too) Tj T*
(slight to attract attention otherwise, will be made apparent. The) Tj T*
(positions assumed vary in different plants, and even in the parts of) Tj T*
(the same compound leaf; in the kidney bean, for instance, the common) Tj T*
(petiole turns up at night, while the individual leaflets turn down.) Tj T*
(One of the common pigweeds \(_Amaranthus Palmeri_, Figs. 242-244\) is) Tj T*
(heliotropic in the day time and nyctitropic at night.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 242-244.?Showing the movements of _Amaranthus) Tj T*
(Palmeri_: 242, 243, position at sunrise and sunset \(heliotropic\);) Tj T*
(244, night position \(nyctitropic\) half an hour after sunset. \(_From_) Tj T*
(photographs by Prof. F. E. Lloyd.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The very striking nyctitropic adjustments of the wild senna \(_Cassia) Tj T*
(tora_\) photographed by Professor Francis E. Lloyd of the Alabama) Tj T*
(Polytechnic Institute \(Figs. 245-250\), though obviously influenced by) Tj T*
(the sun, are not directed toward it as in those of truly heliotropic) Tj T*
(plants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
