(=201. Irritability= is a general term applied to the power in plants) Tj T*
(of receiving and responding by spontaneous movements to impressions) Tj T*
(from without. In its widest acceptation, irritability includes,) Tj T*
(besides the various forms of adjustment described in this section and) Tj T*
(the next, all movements due to geotropism, those of roots seeking) Tj T*
(air and moisture, the revolution of twining stems and tendrils,) Tj T*
(the circulation of protoplasm in the cell?any movement, in short,) Tj T*
(that is made in response to an impression from the environment is a) Tj T*
(manifestation of irritability. It may be of various degrees, but is) Tj T*
(possessed to some extent by every living vegetable organism.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The term is usually applied, however, more especially to those) Tj T*
(obvious and pronounced responses made by plants to their) Tj T*
(surroundings, as exemplified in the cases just given. Still more) Tj T*
(marked instances are to be found in the movements of the tentacles) Tj T*
(of insectivorous plants, and the sensitive leaflets of the mimosa) Tj T*
(that close at the slightest touch. The tendrils of the passion flower) Tj T*
(are said to appreciate and respond to a pressure that cannot be) Tj T*
(distinguished even by the human tongue, and many plants will detect) Tj T*
(and respond to the ultra-violet rays of light, which are entirely) Tj T*
(invisible to man.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This faculty of irritability among plants corresponds, in an) Tj T*
(imperfect, rudimentary way, to what we recognize in animals as) Tj T*
(nervous excitability. By this it is not meant to imply that the two) Tj T*
(things are identical in their ultimate manifestations, though we) Tj T*
(may regard them as fundamentally the same in that they are both to) Tj T*
(be referred to the property inherent in protoplasm of responding to) Tj T*
(stimuli. There is no indication, however, that irritability in the) Tj T*
(vegetable kingdom is accompanied by anything like consciousness or) Tj T*
(volition, or that plants possess any power of initiative. While the) Tj T*
(movements in response to stimuli are in many cases eminently adapted) Tj T*
(to a purpose, we have no evidence of a controlling power behind them.) Tj T*
(The movement comes automatically in response to the stimulus, whether) Tj T*
(the effect at the moment be advantageous or the reverse.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=202. Adjustments in relation to moisture.=?These adjustments may) Tj T*
(be?\(1\) To guard against excess of moisture; _e.g._ glands for) Tj T*
(excreting water and salts; scales, wax, down, etc., on the surface of) Tj T*
(leaves. These may serve also for protection against cold, insects,) Tj T*
(excess of light and heat. \(2\) For the conservation of moisture;) Tj T*
(_e.g._ the revolute leaf margins of grasses and sand plants growing) Tj T*
(along the seashore; the fleshy leaves of stonecrops and purselanes;) Tj T*
(the hard epidermis of yuccas and aloes; the scales, scurf, and down,) Tj T*
(by which the moisture absorbed from the soil by plants growing in) Tj T*
(dry and barren places is prevented from escaping too rapidly through) Tj T*
(the stomata; the leaf cups and holders sometimes formed by winged) Tj T*
(petioles and clasping leaf bases for retaining dew or rain water.) Tj T*
(\(3\) For leaf drainage, or the conduction of moisture, by means of) Tj T*
(grooves, channels, and taper-pointed leaves, which act as natural) Tj T*
(gutters and drain pipes.) Tj T*
ET
