(=203. The fall of the leaf.=?This is, in effect, an adjustment to) Tj T*
(change of temperature, but that it is not directly due to cold is) Tj T*
(shown by Exp. 75, and also by the fact that leaves in the tropics) Tj T*
(and those of evergreens, while they do not fall at stated periods) Tj T*
(like the bulk of the foliage in the temperate zones, are cut off just) Tj T*
(the same and replaced by new ones, whenever, for any reason, they) Tj T*
(are unable to perform their function. In cold climates they fall) Tj T*
(at the approach of winter, not because the frost loosens them, but) Tj T*
(because the roots are not able to absorb enough moisture to supply) Tj T*
(them with material for making food. The needles and the scale-leaves) Tj T*
(characteristic of evergreens in cold regions are enabled to persist) Tj T*
(indefinitely by reason of their contracted surface. This prevents the) Tj T*
(dissipation of moisture and affords no lodging for the accumulations) Tj T*
(of sleet and snow that would otherwise cumber and perhaps break the) Tj T*
(boughs with their weight. Trees and shrubs that shed their leaves) Tj T*
(in winter are said to be _deciduous_, from a Latin word meaning ?to) Tj T*
(fall.? Can you mention some advantages of the deciduous habit to a) Tj T*
(plant with broad, expanded leaves, growing in a cold climate?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The mechanical means by which the leaf fall is accomplished is) Tj T*
(through the growth of a corky layer of loose cells that forms at) Tj T*
(the base of the petiole and cuts it away from the stem, leaving a) Tj T*
(smooth, clean scar. Tear some fresh young leaves from a growing) Tj T*
(twig and compare the scars with those on a winter bough. Do you see) Tj T*
(any difference? This corky layer can be made to form in some plants) Tj T*
(artificially, by depriving them of working material. \(Exp. 75.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=204. The protection of wintergreen leaves.=?A great many, perhaps) Tj T*
(the majority of broad-leaved evergreens, bear no obvious protection) Tj T*
(against cold, while a large proportion, such as chickweed, violet,) Tj T*
(fumitory, groundsel \(_Senecio_\), and dead nettle \(_Lamium_\), would) Tj T*
(seem peculiarly unfitted, by their delicate structure, to withstand) Tj T*
(it. But recent investigations by the Swedish botanist, Lidforss,) Tj T*
(have shown that all wintergreen leaves, with the exception of those) Tj T*
(on submerged water plants, which are sufficiently protected by the) Tj T*
(medium in which they live, lose their starch in winter and contain) Tj T*
(instead an increased percentage of sugar. The same is true of other) Tj T*
(vegetable structures also, where starch is present, such as roots,) Tj T*
(stems, tubers, and winter fruits?nuts, haws, persimmons, and the) Tj T*
(like, which, as every schoolboy knows, become perceptibly sweeter) Tj T*
