() Tj T*
(The presence of certain substances, of which sugar is the most) Tj T*
(frequent, enables plants to withstand a greater degree of cold) Tj T*
(than they could otherwise endure \(Exp. 76\). This effect, as shown) Tj T*
(by Lidforss?s experiments, is due to the action of sugar in) Tj T*
(counteracting, or retarding, the ?salting out? of proteins by cold,) Tj T*
(as explained in 33.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As sugar is readily reconverted into starch by exposure to a) Tj T*
(moderately high temperature for even a few days, we may find here) Tj T*
(an explanation of the fact that plants which have survived the) Tj T*
(prolonged cold of winter are often killed by a single sharp night) Tj T*
(frost following a few warm days in early spring, before the tender) Tj T*
(new growth has appeared. The plant suffers, not from the direct) Tj T*
(effects of cold, but from the warmth preceding it, which stimulated) Tj T*
(the transformation into starch of the sugar that would have prevented) Tj T*
(the loss of proteins. On the same principle we may account for the) Tj T*
(puzzling fact that the sunny southern side of trees and shrubs) Tj T*
(usually suffers more from the effects of sudden frost than the shaded) Tj T*
(and colder northern face.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In apparent conflict with this reasoning is the fact that sugar) Tj T*
(cane and the sugar beet are peculiarly susceptible to cold. This,) Tj T*
(however, does not invalidate the premises established by Lidforss?s) Tj T*
(researches, but merely emphasizes the need of further investigation,) Tj T*
(which may either reconcile all the facts, or modify their) Tj T*
(interpretation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=205. The colors of autumn leaves.=?These are due to the breaking) Tj T*
(up and disappearance of the chlorophyll when the leaf factory has) Tj T*
(to ?shut down? for want of raw material to work with \(203\). It is) Tj T*
(closely connected with the appearance of frost, since the same) Tj T*
(changes of temperature which produce frost cause the cessation of sap) Tj T*
(flow that brings about the disorganization of the chlorophyll and the) Tj T*
(formation of various pigments derived from it. Besides these, leaves) Tj T*
(may contain other coloring matters that are perceptible only when the) Tj T*
(chlorophyll disappears; and in the sap there is a reddish pigment) Tj T*
(which becomes either a very bright red, or a dark purplish maroon,) Tj T*
(from the effect of chemicals that combine with it in the leaves. With) Tj T*
(these coloring materials at command it is easy to see how the autumn) Tj T*
(woods can assume such splendid hues.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
