(=206. Modification and adaptation.=?Modification is structural) Tj T*
(adjustment, or adaptation, carried so far as to obscure the original) Tj T*
(form of an organ. Its true nature, however, can generally be) Tj T*
(determined by some of the tests mentioned in 100.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Examples of the modification of leaves to do the work of other) Tj T*
(organs have already been noticed, as also their entire disappearance) Tj T*
(in certain cases \(97, 101, 149\) and replacement by other parts; it is) Tj T*
(unnecessary, therefore, to revert to this branch of the subject here.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=207. Protective modifications.=?The most general protective) Tj T*
(modifications that leaves undergo are \(1\) for the conservation) Tj T*
(of moisture, as explained in 202, and \(2\) for protection against) Tj T*
(animals. Many of the adaptations for the former purpose serve) Tj T*
(incidentally for defense against animals also. Spines, hairs, scales,) Tj T*
(sticky exudations, water holders, clasping and perfoliate leaves bar) Tj T*
(the way to crawling insects; horny cuticles, as well as offensive) Tj T*
(odors, bitter secretions, and poisonous juices warn leaf-eating) Tj T*
(cattle and bugs away. These devices are merely protective, however,) Tj T*
(and adapted to a passive attitude of self-defense.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 256-258.?Protective hairs magnified: 256,) Tj T*
(mullein; 257, cinque-foil 258, Shepherdia.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=208. Insectivorous leaves.=?But sometimes a plant becomes the) Tj T*
(aggressor, and instead of standing on the defensive or suffering) Tj T*
(itself to be quietly devoured, proceeds to capture and devour small) Tj T*
(game on its own account, and in this case, the leaf sometimes becomes) Tj T*
(a deadly weapon of destruction.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=209. Pitcher plants.=?The sarracenia, or trumpet leaf, is a) Tj T*
(familiar example of this class. The lower part of the leaf blade is) Tj T*
(transformed into a hollow vessel for holding water, and the top is) Tj T*
(rounded into a broad flap called the _lamina_. Sometimes the lamina) Tj T*
(stands erect, as in the common yellow trumpets of our coast regions,) Tj T*
(and when this is the case, it is brilliantly colored and attracts) Tj T*
(insects \(Fig. 259\). Sometimes, as in the parrot-beaked and the) Tj T*
(spotted trumpet leaf, it is bent over the top of the water vessel) Tj T*
(like a lid, and the back of the leaf, near the foot of the lamina, is) Tj T*
(dotted with transparent specks that serve to decoy foolish flies away) Tj T*
(from the true opening and tempt them to wear themselves out in futile) Tj T*
(efforts to escape, as we often see them do against a window pane.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 259.?Yellow trumpets \(_Sarracenia flava_\).) Tj T*
(\(_From_ the Mo. Botanical Garden Rep?t.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
