(=210. Flytraps.=?The most remarkable examples of insect-catching) Tj T*
(leaves are the Venus?s-flytrap, found in the seacoast region of) Tj T*
(North Carolina, and the sundew \(_Drosera rotundifolia_\), common) Tj T*
(on the margins of sandy bogs and ponds. The latter is a delicate,) Tj T*
(innocent-looking little plant, and owes its poetic name to the) Tj T*
(dewlike appearance of a shining, sticky fluid exuded from glands on) Tj T*
(its leaves, which glitter in the sun like dewdrops. It is, however,) Tj T*
(a most voracious carnivorous plant, the sticky leaves acting as so) Tj T*
(many bits of fly paper by means of which it catches its prey. When a) Tj T*
(fly has been trapped, the tentacles close upon it, the edges of the) Tj T*
(leaf curve inward, making a sort of stomach, from the glands of which) Tj T*
(an acid juice exudes and digests the meal. After a number of days,) Tj T*
(varying according to the digestibility of the diet, the blades slowly) Tj T*
(unfold again and are ready for another capture.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 264.?Bladderwort, showing finely dissected) Tj T*
(submerged leaves bearing bladders for capturing animalculæ.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The bladderwort, common in pools and still waters nearly everywhere,) Tj T*
(has its petioles transformed into floats, while the finely dissected,) Tj T*
(rootlike blades bear little bladders which, when examined under the) Tj T*
(microscope, are found to contain the decomposed remains of captured) Tj T*
(animalculæ.) Tj T*
