(  lobing and branching of leaves in letting the sunlight through.) Tj T*
(  Notice any general differences that may appear as to shape, margin,) Tj T*
(  and texture in the leaves of sun plants, shade plants, and water) Tj T*
(  plants, and account for them. Study the arrangement of leaves on) Tj T*
(  stems of various kinds, with reference to the size and shapes of) Tj T*
(  leaves and their light relations. Consider the value of the various) Tj T*
(  kinds of foliage for shade; for ornament; as producers of moisture;) Tj T*
(  as food; as insect destroyers, etc.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Make a special study of the twelve principal deciduous trees of) Tj T*
(  your neighborhood. Compare the leaves, bark, and branches of the) Tj T*
(  same trees so that you will be able to recognize them by any one of) Tj T*
(  these means alone.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  \(2\) In connection with Sections III and V, consider the effects) Tj T*
(  upon soil moisture of transpiration from the leaves of forest) Tj T*
(  trees and from those of shallow-rooted herbs and weeds that draw) Tj T*
(  their water supply from the surface. Consider the value of forests) Tj T*
(  in protecting crops from excessive evaporation by acting as wind) Tj T*
(  breaks. Study the effect of the fall of leaves upon the formation) Tj T*
(  of soil. In any undisturbed forest tract turn up a few inches of) Tj T*
(  soil with a garden trowel and see what it is composed of. Notice) Tj T*
(  what kind of plants grow in it. Note the absence of weeds and) Tj T*
(  account for it. Compare the appearance of trees scattered along) Tj T*
(  windy hillsides, where the fallen leaves are constantly blown away,) Tj T*
(  or in any position where the soil is unrenewed, with those in an) Tj T*
(  undisturbed forest, and then give an opinion as to the wisdom of) Tj T*
(  hauling away the leaves every year from a timber lot.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  \(3\) In Section VII, observe, in different kinds of leaf mosaics,) Tj T*
(  the means by which the adjustment has been brought about and the) Tj T*
(  purpose it subserves. Make a list of plants illustrating the two) Tj T*
(  habits. Notice the form and position of petioles of different) Tj T*
(  leaves, and their effect upon light exposure, drainage, etc., and) Tj T*
(  the behavior of the different kinds in the wind. Look for compass) Tj T*
(  plants in your neighborhood, and for other examples of adjustment) Tj T*
(  to heat and light. Study the position of leaves at different times) Tj T*
(  of day and in different kinds of weather and note what changes) Tj T*
(  occur and to what they are due.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Make a list of ten plants that seem to you to have best worked out) Tj T*
(  the problem of leaf adjustment, giving the reasons for your opinion.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
