(is impossible for any of the pollen to reach the stigmatic surface.) Tj T*
(It remains in this position till the anthers have shed their pollen,) Tj T*
(then, as may be seen by examining an older flower, the style begins) Tj T*
(to elongate, pushing up the pollen that has fallen on the hairy) Tj T*
(outside of the closed stigma, and forcing it out of the corolla tube,) Tj T*
(where it can be scattered by insects among the other flowers of the) Tj T*
(cluster. When the pollen of its own floret has been thus disposed of,) Tj T*
(the stigma lobes open and curl outward, ready to receive the pollen) Tj T*
(from other flowers. This arrangement is practically universal among) Tj T*
(plants of the composite family; can you divine its object? It will be) Tj T*
(shown later, that much larger and stronger seeds are produced when) Tj T*
(the pistil is pollinated from a different flower, or, better still,) Tj T*
(from a different plant of the same species; hence, you see what a) Tj T*
(useful adaptation this is.) Tj T*
