(          IV. SPECIALIZED FLOWERS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?For spring and early summer: sweet pea, black locust,) Tj T*
(  wistaria, lupine, or any of the characteristic butterfly-shaped) Tj T*
(  flowers of the pea family. For autumn or late summer: tropæolum,) Tj T*
(  monkshood, or a bilabiate flower?snapdragon, digitalis, dead) Tj T*
(  nettle, salvia, catalpa, etc.?of the mint or figwort family.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=237. Irregularity and specialization.=?Irregularity and bilateral) Tj T*
(regularity are, as a rule, indicative of specialization, or) Tj T*
(adaptation to a particular purpose, such as the ready distribution) Tj T*
(of pollen, or its protection against injury. These adaptations) Tj T*
(are more noticeable in the corolla than in other parts, and hence) Tj T*
(flowers of this kind are usually classed according to the shape of) Tj T*
(their corollas. The most highly specialized flowers in this respect) Tj T*
(are the orchids, but they are too rare and difficult of access) Tj T*
(to be available objects for study. The most familiar and widely) Tj T*
(distributed kinds of specialized corollas are the _bilabiate_, or) Tj T*
(two-lipped, and the _papilionaceous_, or butterfly, forms. The) Tj T*
(first is characteristic of the mint and figwort families, of which) Tj T*
(the toadflax, sage, and catalpa are familiar examples. The second) Tj T*
(comprises the well-known papilionaceous flowers of the pea family,) Tj T*
(named from the Latin word _papilio_, a butterfly, on account of their) Tj T*
(general resemblance to that insect.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=238. Dissection of a papilionaceous flower.=?Sketch a blossom of any) Tj T*
(kind of pea or vetch as it appears on the outside. Are the sepals) Tj T*
(all of the same length and shape? If not, which are the shorter, the) Tj T*
(upper or the lower ones?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Turn the flower over and examine its inner face. Notice the large,) Tj T*
(round, and usually upright petal at the back, the two smaller ones) Tj T*
(on each side, and the boat-shaped body between them, formed of) Tj T*
(two small petals more or less united at the apex. Press the side) Tj T*
(petals gently down with the thumb and forefinger and notice how) Tj T*
(the essential organs are forced out from the little boat in which) Tj T*
(they are concealed. Observe how the end of the style is bent over) Tj T*
(so as to bring the stigma uppermost when the petals are depressed.) Tj T*
