(simulating petals: _pet_, petals; _st_, staminodia.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 328.?Flower of a cactus \(_cereus greggii_\),) Tj T*
(showing transition from scales to petals.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=244. Morphology of the flower.=?We have seen that the venation of) Tj T*
(petals and sepals corresponds in a general way with that of foliage) Tj T*
(leaves of the class to which they belong, and that their arrangement) Tj T*
(around their axis is analogous to the arrangement of foliage leaves) Tj T*
(on the branch. In our study of inflorescence, it was observed that) Tj T*
(flowers and flower buds occur in the same positions where leaf buds) Tj T*
(occur, and that they are subject to the same laws of arrangement) Tj T*
(and growth. We learned, also, in our study of leaves, something) Tj T*
