(=246. Office of the flower.=?The one object of the flower is the) Tj T*
(production of fruit and seed, and all its wonderful specializations) Tj T*
(and variations of form and color tend either directly or indirectly) Tj T*
(to this end.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=247. Pollination and fertilization.=?It was stated in 215 that only) Tj T*
(in very exceptional cases can seed be developed unless some of the) Tj T*
(pollen reaches the stigma. This act, called _pollination_, is an) Tj T*
(essential step in seed production, but is not sufficient to secure) Tj T*
(that end unless it leads to the process known as _fertilization_.) Tj T*
(Successful pollination is a necessary preliminary to fertilization,) Tj T*
(and the one begins where the other ends.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=248. The next step toward fertilization.=?Examine with a lens the) Tj T*
(pollinated pistil of a mallow, lily, or other large flower, and) Tj T*
(notice the flabby, withered appearance of grains that have stood for) Tj T*
(some time on the stigma, as compared with those of a newly opened) Tj T*
(anther. Can you account for the difference? Touch the tip of your) Tj T*
(tongue to the stigma, or apply the proper chemical test, and it will) Tj T*
(be seen that the sticky fluid which it exudes, contains sugar. Refer) Tj T*
(to Exp. 77 and say what effect this substance has on the pollen.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 329.?A pollen grain emitting a tube \(magnified\).]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=249. The pollen tube.=?The same thing happens when a pollen grain) Tj T*
(falls on the moist surface of the stigma. It begins to germinate by) Tj T*
(sending a little tube down into the substance of the pistil, and the) Tj T*
(withered appearance of the grains on the stigma results from the) Tj T*
(nourishment in them having been exhausted, just as the endosperm of) Tj T*
(the seed is exhausted when the embryo begins to germinate. Here,) Tj T*
(however, the analogy ends, for the pollen tube is not adapted, like) Tj T*
(the radicle of the seedling, to absorb and convey nourishment up to) Tj T*
(the other parts, but to feed and carry down to the ovary two small) Tj T*
(bodies called _generative cells_, which it discharges there, and) Tj T*
(then its work is done and it disappears. So it must be borne in mind) Tj T*
(that when we speak of the germination of the pollen grains, we mean) Tj T*
(something really very different from the germination of a seed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=250. The course of the pollen tube.=?Cut the thinnest possible) Tj T*
(section through a freshly pollinated pistil and place under the) Tj T*
(microscope. Watch the pollen tubes from the grains on the stigma as) Tj T*
(they descend through the style toward the ovary. A pollinated strand) Tj T*
(of corn silk?which is only a very much elongated style?is excellent) Tj T*
(for this purpose. It is so thin and transparent that no section need) Tj T*
(be made, and the tube can be traced as it works its way down through) Tj T*
(the entire length of the threadlike style to the young grain, or) Tj T*
