() Tj T*
(=254. Self-fertilization= takes place when a stigma is pollinated) Tj T*
(from the same flower. Horticulturists have long known that continued) Tj T*
(self-fertilization, or ?in-breeding? as it is called by nurserymen,) Tj T*
(tends to deteriorate a stock; but Charles Darwin was the first to) Tj T*
(explain, by a series of pains-taking experiments, the meaning of) Tj T*
(those careful adjustments which the more highly organized plants, as) Tj T*
(a rule, have developed to guard against it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 336.?Showing the effect of in-breeding on corn in) Tj T*
(one generation. The two left-hand rows are from self-fertilized seed.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=255. Cross-fertilization= is effected by the pollination of a) Tj T*
(stigma from another flower of the same variety or species. As) Tj T*
(used by practical horticulturists, the expression means that the) Tj T*
(two factors, pollen and ovule, belong to different plants. Since) Tj T*
(pollination is the necessary antecedent to fertilization, and the) Tj T*
(only means by which we can control it, the breeder?s part in crossing) Tj T*
(is concerned with this act only and nature does the rest. Darwin?s) Tj T*
(experiments?and they are confirmed by the experience of plant growers) Tj T*
(everywhere?prove that the offspring from crossing different plants) Tj T*
(of the same kind is usually stronger and more productive than that) Tj T*
(from self-fertilized ones; and if the parent stocks are grown in) Tj T*
(different places and under different conditions, the offspring) Tj T*
(is more vigorous than that from the same kind of plants grown) Tj T*
(under like conditions. For instance, plants from crossed seeds of) Tj T*
(morning-glory vines growing near each other exceeded in height those) Tj T*
(from self-fertilized seeds as 100:76; while the offspring of plants) Tj T*
(growing under different conditions exceeded those of the other cross,) Tj T*
(in height, as 100:78; in number of pods, as 100:57, and in weight) Tj T*
