(=271. ?Nature abhors self-fertilization.?=?These are the) Tj T*
(three principal methods by which nature provides against) Tj T*
(self-fertilization. Other cases occur in which the relative position) Tj T*
(of the two organs is such that self-pollination is difficult, or) Tj T*
(impossible, as in the iris and bear?s grass; or the pollen may be) Tj T*
(incapable of acting on the stigma of the flower that produced it.) Tj T*
(This aversion to self-fertilization is so great that many flowers,) Tj T*
(even when capable of it, will give preference to the pollen of) Tj T*
(another plant of the same kind, if dusted with both. From his) Tj T*
(observations on the behavior of plants in reference to this function,) Tj T*
(Charles Darwin drew the conclusion that ?Nature abhors perpetual) Tj T*
(self-fertilization.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=272. Cleistogamic flowers.=?Apparent exceptions to this rule are) Tj T*
(the hidden flowers found on certain plants which seem to have been) Tj T*
(constructed with a special view to self-fertilization. They are) Tj T*
(called _cleistogamic_, or closed, because they never open, but are) Tj T*
(fertilized in the bud; and those of the fringed polygala do not) Tj T*
(even rise above ground at all. Flowers of this kind can be found on) Tj T*
(several species of violet, concealed under the leaves, close to the) Tj T*
(ground; and the flowers of the peanut, found in the same situation,) Tj T*
(while they open slightly, are close-fertilized and practically) Tj T*
(cleistogamic. They are much more prolific than ordinary flowers,) Tj T*
(but are not common, and seem to be a provision against accident, for) Tj T*
(the plants producing them are generally provided with other flowers) Tj T*
(of the usual kind,?some, as the violet, having elaborate special) Tj T*
(adaptations for cross fertilization.) Tj T*
