(  EXPERIMENT 80. TO TEST THE EFFECT OF SHUTTING OUT EXTERNAL) Tj T*
(  AGENCIES.?Tie paper bags over flower buds of different kinds when) Tj T*
(  nearly ready to open and leave until the flowers have withered.) Tj T*
(  On removing the bags, mark with colored threads the flowers that) Tj T*
(  had been covered, and watch until seed time. Do you notice any) Tj T*
(  difference in the number, size, or weight of the seed produced by) Tj T*
(  them and by those of the same kind left exposed? How do you account) Tj T*
(  for the difference, if there is any? By what agencies could foreign) Tj T*
(  pollen have been carried to the stigmas of the exposed flowers? If) Tj T*
(  any of the covered specimens wither and drop their seed vessels) Tj T*
(  without any attempt to fruit, examine a fresh flower, and see if it) Tj T*
(  is capable of self-pollination.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  As already explained, experiments of this kind, to be conclusive,) Tj T*
(  should be tried on as many specimens as possible. The greater the) Tj T*
(  number of species and individuals included, the better. Where it is) Tj T*
(  not practicable to carry on experiments by the class, pupils who) Tj T*
(  are interested can make them at home.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=273. The problem of pollination.=?When a plant has provided against) Tj T*
(self-pollination, its problem is only half solved, as it must now) Tj T*
(depend upon the conveyance of pollen to the stigma by extraneous) Tj T*
(means.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 351.?Feathery stigmas of a grass adapted to wind) Tj T*
(pollination.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=274. Adaptations to wind pollination.=?A very large number of) Tj T*
(plants, among which are included nearly all our principal forest) Tj T*
(trees, grains, and grasses of every kind, depend exclusively upon) Tj T*
(the wind for the distribution of their pollen. This being the case,) Tj T*
(it is, of course, an advantage to them to get rid of all unnecessary) Tj T*
(appendages that might hinder a free play of the wind among their) Tj T*
(flowers, and so they consist, as a rule, of essential organs only) Tj T*
(\(Figs. 341, 342\). Such flowers are often distinguished, however,) Tj T*
(especially among grasses and low herbs, by large, feathery stigmas) Tj T*
(that are well adapted to catch and hold any stray pollen grains which) Tj T*
(may be floating in the air. Place a stigma of oat or other grass) Tj T*
(under the microscope and you will probably see a number of pollen) Tj T*
(grains clinging to its branches.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=275. The disadvantages of wind pollination.=?This is a very clumsy) Tj T*
(and wasteful method, however, for so much pollen is lost by the) Tj T*
(haphazard mode of distribution that the plant is forced to spend its) Tj T*
