(such as is sometimes seen in the sides of wormy plums and pears. This) Tj T*
(is caused by the larvæ of the moth, which feed upon the unripe seeds.) Tj T*
(A glance under the nodding perianth of a yucca blossom \(Fig. 354\)) Tj T*
(will show that the short stamens are curved back from the pistil in) Tj T*
(such a manner that, under ordinary circumstances, the pollen cannot) Tj T*
(reach the stigma except by the rarest accident. But the yucca moth,) Tj T*
(as soon as she has deposited her eggs in the seed vessel, takes care) Tj T*
(to provide a crop of food for her offspring by gathering a ball of) Tj T*
(pollen in her antennæ and deliberately plastering it over the stigma) Tj T*
(\(Fig. 353\). In this way fertilization of the ovules and maturing of) Tj T*
(the fruit is secured. The larvæ feed on the unripe seeds for a time,) Tj T*
(but so few are destroyed in proportion to the number matured that the) Tj T*
(plant can well afford to pay the small toll charged in return for the) Tj T*
(service rendered.) Tj T*
