(=280. Means of protection.=?Where plants have adapted themselves to) Tj T*
(insect pollination, it is, of course, important to shut out intruders) Tj T*
(that would not make good carriers. In general, small, creeping) Tj T*
(things, like ants and plant lice, are not such efficient pollen) Tj T*
(bearers as winged insects, and hence the various devices, such as) Tj T*
(hairs, scales, and constrictions, at the throat of the corolla, by) Tj T*
(means of which their access to the pollen is prohibited. To this) Tj T*
(class of adaptations belong the hairy filaments of the spiderwort,) Tj T*
(the sticky ring about the peduncles of the catchfly, the swollen) Tj T*
(lips of the snapdragon, the scales or hairs in the throat of the) Tj T*
(hound?s-tongue, the velvet petals of the partridge berry, and the) Tj T*
(recurved edges of corollas like those of the morning-glory and) Tj T*
(tobacco, over which small crawling insects cannot easily climb.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Of flowers that are pollinated by night moths, some close during) Tj T*
(the day, as the four-o?clock and the evening primrose; and _vice) Tj T*
