(  EXPERIMENT 87. WHERE DO THE FOOD SUBSTANCES CONTAINED IN FRUITS) Tj T*
(  COME FROM??Apply your food tests to the pulp of a young apple,) Tj T*
(  squash, bean pod, chestnut, buckeye, or a ?green? ear of corn or) Tj T*
(  wheat, and see what it contains. Test the stem and roots of a plant) Tj T*
(  of the same kind in the same way. Do you find the same foods in) Tj T*
(  them? Where is the food stored?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 88. THROUGH WHAT PARTS OF THE STEM AND FRUIT DO WATER) Tj T*
(  AND NOURISHMENT TRAVEL TO THE SEED??Cut a young squash or cucumber) Tj T*
(  from the vine, leaving stem enough to insert by its cut end in a) Tj T*
(  glass of eosin solution. Leave for two or three days, then make a) Tj T*
(  vertical section through the stem and fruit. What course has the) Tj T*
(  liquid followed? Can you trace some of it into each seed? Do you) Tj T*
(  see now a use for the seed stalk and the rhaphe?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 89. DOES THE SURFACE OF FRUITS GIVE OFF WATER BY) Tj T*
(  TRANSPIRATION??Try Exp. 59, using in place of leaves a young) Tj T*
(  squash, eggplant, or a bunch of grapes, and after a day or two) Tj T*
(  notice whether any moisture has been given off. If the fruit skin) Tj T*
(  gives off moisture, it is natural to expect that it would be) Tj T*
(  provided with stomata, like other transpiring organs. To find out) Tj T*
(  whether this is so, place a thin piece of the outer epidermis of) Tj T*
(  a grape, tomato, plum, or apple under the microscope. Do you find) Tj T*
(  stomata on any of them? Do you see anything else? Try the skin of) Tj T*
(  an apple, and compare the corky dots you find there with those on) Tj T*
(  the bark of a young dicotyl stem \(118\) and decide what they are.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 90. WILL FRUITS RIPEN WELL IN THE ABSENCE OF LIGHT AND) Tj T*
(  AIR??Envelop a number of immature fruits in bags of dark cloth or) Tj T*
(  paper so that no light can reach them. Keep a number of others well) Tj T*
(  coated with oil or vaseline, and watch. Do the fruits so treated) Tj T*
(  mature as quickly or develop as fully as those of the same kind) Tj T*
(  left untreated?) Tj T*
