(=282. What is a fruit?=?Horticulturally and commercially the) Tj T*
(distinction between a fruit and a vegetable depends very much upon) Tj T*
(the use we make of it?whether as food, or as a mere gratification of) Tj T*
(the palate. Broadly speaking, those fruits that are lacking in sugar,) Tj T*
(as the tomato and cucumber, are classed as vegetables. Botanically, a) Tj T*
(fruit is any ripened seed vessel, or ovary, with such connected parts) Tj T*
(as may have become incorporated with it; and hence, to the botanist,) Tj T*
(a boll of cotton, a tickseed, or a cocklebur is just as much a fruit) Tj T*
(as a peach or a watermelon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=283. Classification of fruits.=?For convenience of description,) Tj T*
(fruits are classed as:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_a_\) Dry or fleshy, according as they have a more or less hard and) Tj T*
(bony, or soft and fleshy, texture.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_b_\) Dehiscent, or indehiscent, according as they open at maturity) Tj T*
(in a regular way to discharge their seed, or remain closed until the) Tj T*
(covering wears away or is burst by the germinating embryo.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Fleshy fruits are very seldom dehiscent, though some few, as the) Tj T*
(balsam apple and the chayote, or one-seeded squash, discharge their) Tj T*
(seed when mature. The banana and some other fleshy fruits, when) Tj T*
(peeled, separate along regular lines, and in this respect behave very) Tj T*
(much as if they were fleshy pods.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=284. When is a fruit ripe?=?A fruit is ripe horticulturally, when) Tj T*
(it is good to eat; it is ripe botanically, when it has set its seed.) Tj T*
(Many of our choicest table fruits, such as the pineapple, banana, and) Tj T*
(most varieties of fig, seldom are botanically ripe, since they rarely) Tj T*
(produce perfect seeds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is the constant effort of the horticulturist to develop those) Tj T*
(parts of a plant that are useful to man, while in a state of nature) Tj T*
(the plant seeks to develop such parts as best serve its own purpose) Tj T*
(in the struggle for existence. The plants most useful to man have,) Tj T*
(as a general thing, been subjected to a long course of artificial) Tj T*
(breeding and selection. They are forced developments, often) Tj T*
(monstrosities, from the plant?s point of view, if we could conceive) Tj T*
(of it as capable of having an opinion. Nature is continually striving) Tj T*
(to reclaim them; and if left to themselves, they must either obey) Tj T*
(?the call of the wild,? or die out.) Tj T*
ET
