([Illustration: FIG. 366.?A seedless citrange, hybrid between the) Tj T*
(orange and the lemon.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=285. Seedless fruits and vegetables.=?As the seed is the most) Tj T*
(important thing to the plant, the edible parts in wild fruits are, as) Tj T*
(a rule, subsidiary to its development. In a state of nature, fruits) Tj T*
(will generally wither and drop from the stem, if for any reason they) Tj T*
(have become incapable of perfecting their seed. It is only in a few) Tj T*
(kinds, limited to those which can successfully propagate themselves) Tj T*
(by other means, that the production of seed does not take place.) Tj T*
(Among cultivated species, however, where propagation is carefully) Tj T*
(provided for by man, the seed is of less importance, and sterile) Tj T*
(varieties that might soon die out under natural conditions, continue) Tj T*
(their existence indefinitely under his fostering hand. The seeds) Tj T*
(of edible fruits are, as a general thing, both indigestible and) Tj T*
(unpalatable \(21\), and hence the efforts of the horticulturist are) Tj T*
(directed largely to getting rid of them, or to very greatly reducing) Tj T*
(their size and number in proportion to the edible parts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=286. How seedless fruits arise.=?The perfecting of seed requires a) Tj T*
(great consumption of food and energy on the part of the plant, and) Tj T*
(when it is led, for any reason, to expend an unusual amount of force) Tj T*
(in some other function,?as for instance, in producing tubers or in) Tj T*
(growing bulbs,?it is apt to bear few seeds and to depend more or less) Tj T*
(completely upon other methods of reproduction.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among cultivated plants, selection on the part of man, whether) Tj T*
(conscious or unconscious, has perhaps contributed more than any) Tj T*
(other cause to bring about the same result. To this agency is) Tj T*
(probably due the development of our common domestic fig, of which) Tj T*
(over four hundred varieties that mature fruits without fertilization) Tj T*
(are cultivated in the United States alone. The fig was one of the) Tj T*
(earliest fruits known to cultivation; and the early navigators,) Tj T*
(ignorant of the processes of fertilization, would naturally, in) Tj T*
(choosing specimens to carry home with them, select only fruit-bearing) Tj T*
(trees. Such of these as matured fruits would be preserved and) Tj T*
(propagated, until, by repeated selection, hundreds of edible) Tj T*
(varieties have been developed that ripen fruits without caprification) Tj T*
(\(279\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=287. The use of the fruit to the plant.=?The object of the fruit is) Tj T*
(to furnish protection to the seeds during their period of development) Tj T*
(and inactivity, and to aid in various ways the work of dispersal.) Tj T*
(It probably takes part also in digesting and diffusing nourishment) Tj T*
(for the use of the developing seeds. It has been shown in previous) Tj T*
(chapters that plants, almost without exception, are in the habit of) Tj T*
(storing up food in various ways as a provision for fruiting. That a) Tj T*
(large portion of the stored nourishment is used up in the performance) Tj T*
(of this function is proved by its disappearance from those parts?for) Tj T*
(example, from fleshy roots, such as beets and turnips, after they) Tj T*
(have ?gone to seed.?) Tj T*
ET
