(two-celled fruit, like the potato berry \(Fig. 374\), but it has been) Tj T*
(so modified by cultivation that the original plan is not always easy) Tj T*
(to distinguish. See if you can make it out. Do the seeds in your) Tj T*
(specimen appear to be healthy and well developed, or are some of them) Tj T*
(small and aborted? How do you account for this? \(285, 286.\) What) Tj T*
(difference do you notice in color between the ripe and unripe fruit?) Tj T*
(Write a definition of the berry from the study you have made of it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Berries are the commonest of all fleshy fruits, and the most variable) Tj T*
(and difficult to define. In general, any soft, pulpy, or juicy mass,) Tj T*
(like the grape and tomato, whether one or many seeded, inclosed in) Tj T*
(a containing envelope, whether skin or rind, is a berry. Its typical) Tj T*
(forms are such fruits as the grape, mistletoe, pokeberry, etc.,) Tj T*
(though such diverse forms as the eggplant, persimmon, red pepper,) Tj T*
(orange, banana, and pomegranate have been classed as berries; and, in) Tj T*
(fact, the melon and the pumpkin are only greatly modified kinds of) Tj T*
(the same fruit. In popular language, any small, round, edible fruit) Tj T*
(is called a berry. This is a good commercial classification, though) Tj T*
