(          III. DRY FRUITS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?Some easily attainable specimens of dry fruits are \(1\)) Tj T*
(  nuts: acorn, hickory nut, walnut, chestnut, pecan, filbert; \(2\)) Tj T*
(  pods: pea and bean pods, capsules of larkspur, milkweed, jimson) Tj T*
(  weed, cotton; \(3\) grains: corn, wheat, oats, rice; \(4\) akene:) Tj T*
(  sunflower, thistle, dandelion, buckwheat, clematis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=293. Importance of dry fruits.=?Dry fruits are not in general so) Tj T*
(conspicuous or so attractive as fleshy ones, but on account of their) Tj T*
(great number and variety they offer a wide field for study. They are) Tj T*
(also of great interest from an economic point of view: \(1\) because) Tj T*
(they include the cereal grains that furnish so large a portion of our) Tj T*
(food, and \(2\) because the greater part of the troublesome weeds that) Tj T*
(infest our crops are scattered by fruits of this class.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=294. Indehiscent fruits.=?These kinds are so simple that it will not) Tj T*
(be necessary to give much time to them. Compare an acorn, a chestnut,) Tj T*
(or a filbert with a ripe bean pod or with a capsule of morning-glory.) Tj T*
(Try to open each with your fingers; which _dehisces_, or opens, the) Tj T*
(more readily? Which is indehiscent, having no regular way of opening?) Tj T*
(How many seeds or kernels do you find in the dehiscent pod? How many) Tj T*
(in the indehiscent one? Would it be of any advantage for a one-seeded) Tj T*
(pod to open? Remove the kernel from the indehiscent fruit; has it any) Tj T*
(covering besides the shell? Which is the pericarp, and which the seed) Tj T*
(coat?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 376, 377.?Nut of the pecan tree: 376, exterior;) Tj T*
(377, cross section.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIGS. 378, 379.?Nutlike seeds: 378, horse-chestnut;) Tj T*
(379, seed of the fetid sterculia.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=295. The nut= is easily recognized by its hard, bony covering,) Tj T*
(containing usually, when mature, a single large seed that fills) Tj T*
(the interior. Care should be taken not to confound with true nuts,) Tj T*
(large bony seeds, like those of the buckeye, horse-chestnut, date,) Tj T*
(and the Brazil nut sold in the markets. In the true nut, the hard) Tj T*
(covering is the seed vessel, or pericarp, and not a part of the seed) Tj T*
