(=298. Dehiscent fruits.=?_Pod_, or _capsule_, is the general name) Tj T*
(applied to all dehiscent fruits. The simplest possible kind of pod) Tj T*
(is the _follicle_, composed of a single carpel, like those of the) Tj T*
(larkspur, milkweed, and marsh marigold, and may be regarded as a) Tj T*
(modified leaf. Examine one of these pods and you will find that) Tj T*
(it splits down one side, which corresponds to the edges of the) Tj T*
(leaf brought together and turned inward to form a placenta for the) Tj T*
(attachment of the seed. This line of union is called a ?suture,? from) Tj T*
(a Latin word meaning a ?seam.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=299. The legume.=?Get a pod of any kind of bean or pea, and observe) Tj T*
(that it differs from the follicle in having two sutures or lines) Tj T*
(of dehiscence. One of these runs along the back of the carpel and) Tj T*
(corresponds to the midrib of the leaf; the other, corresponding to) Tj T*
(the united edges of the carpellary leaf, always turns inward, toward) Tj T*
(the axis of the flower, and forms the placenta.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
