(=300. Compound or syncarpous pods.=?The carpellary leaves may unite) Tj T*
(either by their open edges, as if a whorl like that represented in) Tj T*
(Fig. 188 were to grow together by the margins \(Fig. 395\); or each) Tj T*
(may first roll itself into a simple follicle like the larkspur) Tj T*
(and columbine \(Fig. 396\), and then a number of these may unite by) Tj T*
(their ventral sutures into a single syncarpous capsule, with as) Tj T*
(many locules as there are carpels \(Fig. 398\). The seed-bearing) Tj T*
(sutures being all brought together in the center, the placenta) Tj T*
(becomes _central_ and _axial_. In the first case \(Fig. 395\) the open) Tj T*
(carpels form a one-chambered capsule, though the placentas sometimes) Tj T*
(project, as in the cotton, so far as to produce the effect of true) Tj T*
(partitions with a central axial placenta. In capsules with only one) Tj T*
(compartment, the number of carpels can generally be determined by the) Tj T*
(number of sutures or of placentas.) Tj T*
