(          IV. ACCESSORY, AGGREGATE, AND MULTIPLE FRUITS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?For autumn and winter, examples of accessory fruits are:) Tj T*
(  pineapple, common apple, pear, rose hip; aggregate: magnolia,) Tj T*
(  tulip tree, wild cucumber, sweet flag \(_Calamus_\); multiple: osage) Tj T*
(  orange, sweet gum balls, pine cones, figs, fresh or dried.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  For spring and summer, examples of accessory fruits are: raspberry,) Tj T*
(  strawberry, squash, cucumber; aggregate: strawberry, blackberry,) Tj T*
(  Jack-in-the-pulpit; multiple: fig, mulberry. Most of those named) Tj T*
(  will be found to belong to more than one class; the strawberry, for) Tj T*
(  instance, is both accessory and aggregate; the fig and pineapple,) Tj T*
(  accessory and multiple.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=301.= Besides the varieties already named, all fruits, whether) Tj T*
(fleshy or dry, may be simple, accessory, aggregate, or collective.) Tj T*
(Fruits of the first kind need no explanation; they consist merely) Tj T*
(of a single ripened ovary, whether of one or more carpels, as the) Tj T*
(peach, cherry, bean, and lemon.) Tj T*
