(CHAPTER IX. THE RESPONSE OF THE PLANT TO ITS SURROUNDINGS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(          I. ECOLOGICAL FACTORS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?A number of small flowerpots filled with soils of as many) Tj T*
(  different kinds as can be found in the neighborhood.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=308. Definition.=?By _ecology_ is meant the relation of plants to) Tj T*
(their surroundings, which may be considered under three general) Tj T*
(heads: their relations to inanimate nature, to other plants, and) Tj T*
(to animals. The subject has been touched upon repeatedly in the) Tj T*
(foregoing pages, and, in fact, it is impossible to treat of any) Tj T*
(branch of botany without some reference to it. All that was said) Tj T*
(about the adjustment of leaves for light and moisture, and their) Tj T*
(adaptations for protection and food storage, about the devices for) Tj T*
(pollination, and for fruit and seed dispersal, really belong to) Tj T*
(ecology.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=309. Symbiosis.=?The relations of plants to animate nature are) Tj T*
(_biological factors_, and may act in two ways: \(1\) through the) Tj T*
(destruction of vegetation by hungry animals and by parasitic and) Tj T*
(disease-producing organisms; and \(2\) by associations for mutual) Tj T*
(benefit, such as are described in section viii of chapter VII.) Tj T*
(Associations of this kind are included under the general term) Tj T*
(_symbiosis_, a word which means ?living together.? In its broadest) Tj T*
(sense symbiosis refers to any sort of dependence or intimate organic) Tj T*
(relation between different kinds of individuals, and so may include) Tj T*
(the climbing and parasitic habits; but it is usually restricted to) Tj T*
(cases where the relation is one of mutual benefit. It may exist) Tj T*
(either between plants of one kind with those of another, between) Tj T*
(animals with animals, or between plants and animals, as in the case) Tj T*
(of the clover and bumblebee, and the yucca and pronuba.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: PLATE 13.?Showing the quick response of vegetation to) Tj T*
(surroundings. The upper cut shows the appearance of an irrigation) Tj T*
(canal in the arid plains region, when first completed; the lower cut,) Tj T*
(ten years after completion.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The occurrence of root tubercles on certain of the leguminosæ \(63\) is) Tj T*
(a clear case of symbiosis, the microscopic organisms in the tubercles) Tj T*
(getting their food from the plant and at the same time enabling it to) Tj T*
(get food for itself from the air in a way that it could not otherwise) Tj T*
(do.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
