() Tj T*
(=310. Relations with inanimate nature.=?But it is to the relations of) Tj T*
(plants with inanimate nature, and their grouping into societies under) Tj T*
(the influence of such conditions, that the term ?ecology? is more) Tj T*
(strictly applied. The external conditions that lead to the grouping) Tj T*
(are called _ecological factors_. The most important of these are) Tj T*
(temperature, moisture, soil, light, and air, including the direction) Tj T*
(and character of the prevailing winds. Each of these factors is) Tj T*
(complicated with the others and with conditions of its own in a way) Tj T*
(that often makes it difficult to determine just what effect any one) Tj T*
(of them may have in the formation of a given plant society.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=311. Temperature= may be even and steady, like that of most oceanic) Tj T*
(regions, or it may be subject to sudden caprices and variations,) Tj T*
(like the ?heated terms? and ?cold snaps? that afflict our Eastern) Tj T*
(coast region every few years. It is not the average temperature of) Tj T*
(a climate, but its extremes, especially of cold, that limit the) Tj T*
(character of vegetation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Temperature probably has more influence than any other factor upon) Tj T*
(the distribution of plants over the globe; but it can have little) Tj T*
(or no effect in evolving local differences in vegetation, because) Tj T*
(the temperature of any given locality, except on the sides of high) Tj T*
(mountains, will ordinarily be the same within a circuit of many miles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=312. Moisture=, again, may be of all degrees, from the) Tj T*
(superabundance of lakes and rivers and standing swamps, to the arid) Tj T*
(dryness of the desert, and the water may be still and sluggish,) Tj T*
(or in rapid motion. It may exist more or less permanently in the) Tj T*
(atmosphere, as in moist climates like those of England and Ireland,) Tj T*
(where vegetation is characterized by great verdure; or it may come) Tj T*
(irregularly in the form of sudden floods, or at fixed intervals,) Tj T*
(causing an alternation of wet and dry seasons. Moreover, the moisture) Tj T*
(of the soil or the atmosphere may be impregnated with minerals or) Tj T*
(gases, which may affect the vegetation independently of the actual) Tj T*
(amount of water absorbed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 410.?The effect of cold?a Mt. Katahdin bog.) Tj T*
(\(_From_ Mo. Botanical Garden Rep?t.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Snow is a form of water which may act in two entirely opposite ways:) Tj T*
(\(1\) by keeping the atmospheric precipitation locked up in a solid) Tj T*
(state and thus bringing about a condition analogous to drought?for) Tj T*
(example, in arctic deserts and Alpine snow fields; \(2\) by causing) Tj T*
(annual floods and overflows when it melts in the spring, as in the) Tj T*
(Nile and Mississippi valleys.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In cold temperate regions it also influences vegetation to a) Tj T*
(considerable extent by covering the warm earth like a blanket during) Tj T*
(winter, and thus protecting tender seeds and shoots that otherwise) Tj T*
(would not be able to survive.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
