(=313. Light= may be of all degrees of intensity, from the blazing) Tj T*
(sun of the treeless plain to the darkness of caves and cellars where) Tj T*
(no green thing can exist. Between these extremes are numberless) Tj T*
(intermediate stages: the dark ravines on the northern side of) Tj T*
(mountains, the dense shade of beech and hemlock forests, and the) Tj T*
(light, lacy shadows of the pines,?each characterized by its peculiar) Tj T*
(form of vegetation. Absence of light, too, is usually accompanied by) Tj T*
(a lowering of temperature and a reduction of transpiration, factors) Tj T*
(which tend to accentuate the difference between sun plants and shade) Tj T*
(plants, giving to the latter some of the characteristics of aquatic) Tj T*
(vegetation. Generally, the tissues of these are thin and delicate,) Tj T*
(and having no need to guard against excessive transpiration, they) Tj T*
(wither rapidly when cut or exposed to too great intensity of heat and) Tj T*
(light.) Tj T*
