(=314. Winds= affect vegetation, not only as to the manner of) Tj T*
(seed distribution and the conveyance of pollen, but directly by) Tj T*
(increasing transpiration, and necessitating the development of strong) Tj T*
(holdfasts in plants growing upon mountain sides and in other exposed) Tj T*
(situations. The nature of the region from which they blow?whether) Tj T*
(moist, dry, hot, cold, etc.?is also an important factor. In a) Tj T*
(district open to sea breezes, live oaks, which require a salt) Tj T*
(atmosphere, may sometimes be found as far as a hundred miles from the) Tj T*
(coast.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=315. Soil.=?While water is the most important, soil is perhaps the) Tj T*
(most interesting of these factors to the farmer, because it is the) Tj T*
(one that he has it most largely in his power to modify. It is to be) Tj T*
(viewed under two aspects: first, as to its mechanical properties,) Tj T*
(whether soft, hard, compact, porous, light, heavy, etc.; secondly, as) Tj T*
(to its chemical composition and the amount of plant food-materials) Tj T*
(contained in it. The first can be regulated by tillage and drainage,) Tj T*
(the second by a proper use of fertilizers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
