(=317. Principles of subdivision.=?The mixed associations described in) Tj T*
(the last paragraph are quite independent of botanical relationships,) Tj T*
(and any of the factors named in 310, or others of a different kind,) Tj T*
(could be made the basis of their classification. They might be) Tj T*
(grouped, for instance, according to their economic uses, or according) Tj T*
(to origin, whether native or introduced, as best suited the purpose) Tj T*
(of the classification in each case. The moisture factor, however, has) Tj T*
(been generally agreed upon as the one most convenient for ordinary) Tj T*
(purposes. Upon this principle plants are divided into three great) Tj T*
(groups:?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Hydrophytes=, or water plants, those that require abundant moisture.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Xerophytes=, or drought plants, those that have adapted themselves) Tj T*
(to desert or arid conditions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 415.?A water plant \(_Limnophila_\), with water) Tj T*
(leaves and air leaves and transitional forms.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Mesophytes=, plants that live in conditions intermediate between) Tj T*
(excessive drought and excessive moisture. To this class belong) Tj T*
(most of our ordinary cultivated plants and the greater part of the) Tj T*
(vegetation of the globe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Halophytes=, ?salt plants,? is a term used to designate a fourth) Tj T*
(class, based not directly upon the water factor, but upon the) Tj T*
(presence of a particular mineral in the water or the soil which) Tj T*
(they can tolerate. They seem to bear a sort of double relation to) Tj T*
(hydrophytes on the one hand and to zerophytes on the other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
