(medium in which they live. That they may absorb readily, the tissues) Tj T*
(are apt to be soft and succulent and the walls of the cells composing) Tj T*
(them very thin. In some of the pipeworts \(_Eriocaulon_\), the ells) Tj T*
(are so large as to be easily seen with the unaided eye. If you can) Tj T*
(obtain one of these, examine it with a lens and notice how very thin) Tj T*
(the walls are. Water plants also contain numerous air cavities, and) Tj T*
(often develop bladders and floats, as in the common bladderwort and) Tj T*
(many seaweeds. The leaves of submerged plants are usually either) Tj T*
(greatly reduced in size or very much cut and divided, while the ones) Tj T*
(that rise above water, like those of the water lily, are apt to be) Tj T*
(large and entire, to facilitate floating, and have stomata on their) Tj T*
(upper surface. Floating plants sometimes form such large colonies as) Tj T*
(to be a serious menace to navigation. Well-known instances of this) Tj T*
(are the water hyacinths in the St. John?s River, Florida, and the) Tj T*
(vast formations of swimming gulfweed from which the Sargasso Sea) Tj T*
