(The sedges and cattails are the pioneers of these societies, which) Tj T*
(tend constantly to encroach upon the water and so prepare the way) Tj T*
(for the advance of other colonists. Drawing their nourishment from) Tj T*
(the loose soil in which they are anchored, and lacking the support) Tj T*
(of a liquid medium, they develop roots and vascular stems. The roots) Tj T*
(of plants growing in swamps have difficulty in obtaining proper) Tj T*
(aëration on account of the water, which shuts off the air from them;) Tj T*
(hence they are furnished with large air cavities, and the bases) Tj T*
(of the stems are often greatly enlarged, as in the Ogeechee lime) Tj T*
(\(_Nyssa capitata_\) and cypress, to give room for the formation of air) Tj T*
(passages. The peculiar hollow projections known as ?cypress knees?) Tj T*
(are arrangements for aërating the roots of these trees.) Tj T*
