(=325. The origin of vegetable zones.=?The terms ?zone? and ?zonation?) Tj T*
(are used to express a general tendency of plant societies and) Tj T*
(formations to distribute themselves in more or less regular belts) Tj T*
(or strata, relatively to the varying intensity of the prevalent) Tj T*
(ecological factor of their habitat. In almost every locality there) Tj T*
(exists some special feature?a pond, a brook, a small ravine, an) Tj T*
(isolated hilltop, a deserted quarry, a gravel pit, or a railroad) Tj T*
(cut,?sufficiently distinct from the general surroundings to exercise) Tj T*
(a perceptible control over the vegetation in its immediate vicinity,) Tj T*
(and thus to become the starting point of a series of plant zones) Tj T*
(that mark the decreasing influence of the factor concerned, by) Tj T*
(their change of character as they recede from its point of greatest) Tj T*
(intensity. Starting from a barren, exposed hilltop, for example,) Tj T*
(with a covering of dry broom sedge \(_Andropogon_\) and fleabane, we) Tj T*
(encounter next an almost desert zone of washed and gullied slopes) Tj T*
(in whose hard, sunbaked soil nothing but a few scrub pines and) Tj T*
(brambles can gain a foothold. This will, perhaps, be succeeded, by a) Tj T*
(straggling belt of sassafras, sumac, and buckthorn, mixed with cat) Tj T*
(brier and blackberry canes, beyond which, at the foot of the hill,) Tj T*
(begins a stretch of meadow, or a bit of woodland crossed by a brook,) Tj T*
(or hollowed into a boggy depression. From this new factor originates) Tj T*
(a second series of zonations, passing through all the stages of bog,) Tj T*
(swamp, shade, and sun plants, back to the prevailing type of the) Tj T*
(region. Moisture is really the controlling factor in both cases, its) Tj T*
(influence in the first being negative,?that is, inversely,?and in the) Tj T*
(other, positive, or directly proportioned to the quantity present.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=326. Direction of zonation.=?When the direction in which the) Tj T*
(controlling factor changes is horizontal, as with soil and water,) Tj T*
(the zonation will be _horizontal_; when, as in the case of light,) Tj T*
(it is vertical, the zonation or stratification will be _vertical_.) Tj T*
(Examples of this can be observed in the growth of almost any forest) Tj T*
(area, the natural order of succession being: \(1\) a ground layer of) Tj T*
(mosses and fungi; \(2\) low, creeping vines,?partridge berry, trailing) Tj T*
(arbutus, twinflower \(_Linnæa_\); \(3\) small ferns and low flowering) Tj T*
(herbs?pyrola, clintonia, trillium; \(4\) a zone of tall herbs and low) Tj T*
(bushes?royal fern, cohosh \(_Actæa_\), blueberries; \(5\) tall herbs and) Tj T*
(shrubs, small trees, and climbing vines?kalmia, dogwood, farkleberry,) Tj T*
(smilax, Virginia creeper; \(6\) tall treetops towering up into full) Tj T*
(sunlight.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
