(          I. THEIR PLACE IN NATURE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=331. Order of development.=?All the forms that have hitherto claimed) Tj T*
(our attention belong to the great division of Spermatophytes, or) Tj T*
(seed-bearing plants, designated also as _Phanerogams_, or flowering) Tj T*
(plants. They comprise the higher forms of vegetable life, and because) Tj T*
(they are more conspicuous and better known than the other groups,) Tj T*
(they have been taken up first, since it is more convenient, for) Tj T*
(ordinary purposes, to work our way backward from the familiar to the) Tj T*
(less known, rather than in the reverse order.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But it must be understood that this is not the order of nature. The) Tj T*
(geological record shows that the simplest forms of life were the) Tj T*
(first to appear, and from these all the higher forms were gradually) Tj T*
(evolved. There is no sharp line of division between any of the orders) Tj T*
(and groups of plants, but the line of development can be traced) Tj T*
(through a succession of almost imperceptible changes from the lowest) Tj T*
(forms to the highest, and it is only by a study of the former that) Tj T*
(botanists have come to understand the true nature and structure of) Tj T*
(the latter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=332. Basis of distinction.=?_Cryptogams_, or seedless plants as) Tj T*
(a whole, are distinguished from the phanerogams by their simpler) Tj T*
(structure and by their mode of propagation, which in the former) Tj T*
(is by means of spores, while in the phanerogams it is by seeds. A) Tj T*
(spore is a simple organic body, consisting usually of a single cell) Tj T*
(which separates from the parent plant at maturity and gives rise to) Tj T*
(a new individual. A seed is a complicated, many-celled structure,) Tj T*
(containing within itself the rudimentary structure of a new plant) Tj T*
(already organized.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Beginning with the simplest forms, cryptogams are grouped in three) Tj T*
(great orders:?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=333. I. Thallophytes=, or thallus plants.?This group takes its name) Tj T*
(from the _thallus_ structure that characterizes its vegetation. In) Tj T*
(its typical form, a thallus is a more or less flat, expanded body, of) Tj T*
(which the lichens and liverworts offer familiar examples among land) Tj T*
(plants, and the kelps and laminarias among seaweeds. It may be of any) Tj T*
(size and shape, however, and sometimes consists of a mere filament,) Tj T*
(as in the common brook silk, or even of a single cell \(Fig. 429\).) Tj T*
(The term is applied in general to the simplest kinds of vegetable) Tj T*
(structure, in which there is no differentiation of tissues, and no) Tj T*
(true distinction of root, stem, and leaves. While it is not peculiar) Tj T*
(to the thallophytes, it has attained its most typical development) Tj T*
(among them, and the name is therefore retained as distinctive of) Tj T*
(that group. It embraces two great divisions, the Algæ and Fungi. The) Tj T*
(first includes seaweeds and the common freshwater brook silks and) Tj T*
(pond scums, besides numerous microscopic forms whose presence escapes) Tj T*
(the eye altogether, or is made known only by the discolorations and) Tj T*
(other changes caused by them in the water. To the fungi belong the) Tj T*
(mushrooms and puffballs, the molds, rusts, mildews, and the vast) Tj T*
