(=334. II. Bryophytes=, or moss plants.?This group likewise contains) Tj T*
(two main divisions, Mosses and Liverworts. Familiar examples of the) Tj T*
(latter are the flat, spreading green plants, bearing somewhat the) Tj T*
(aspect of lichens, met with everywhere on wet rocks and banks around) Tj T*
(shady watercourses. The name is a reminiscence of their former use in) Tj T*
(medicine as a specific for diseases of the liver, and not, as in the) Tj T*
(case of the liver leaf, of a fancied resemblance to that organ.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Mosses are one of the best defined of botanical orders, and are) Tj T*
(easily recognized by their slender, leafy fruiting stalks, growing) Tj T*
(usually in dense, spreading mats, and presenting every appearance of) Tj T*
(a highly organized structure, well differentiated into root, stem,) Tj T*
(and leaves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The liverworts represent the more primitive division of the group,) Tj T*
(and in some of their forms approach so near the thallophytes that it) Tj T*
(is not difficult to recognize them as connecting links in the same) Tj T*
(chain of life. Their relationship to the next higher group is not) Tj T*
(clear, but while they represent a more primitive stage of evolution) Tj T*
(than the mosses, the development of the latter has followed a course) Tj T*
(divergent from the main line of evolutionary progress.) Tj T*
