(adhering together in groups of two, four, or more, with the sides) Tj T*
(that are in contact slightly flattened. Each of these bodies is an) Tj T*
(individual plant consisting of a single cell, whence they are said) Tj T*
(to be _unicellular_. Draw one of the single cells and one of the) Tj T*
(groups, or colonies, as they appear under the microscope. Try to) Tj T*
(make out the cell wall and the nucleus, and label all the parts \(see) Tj T*
(7\). If you have any difficulty in distinguishing the cell wall, drop) Tj T*
(a little glycerine or salt water on the slide. This will cause the) Tj T*
(cell contents to shrink by osmosis \(56, 59\). Can you make out the) Tj T*
(structure of the cell colonies? They have resulted from the peculiar) Tj T*
(mode of multiplication that prevails among this class of plants. A) Tj T*
(cell elongates, contracts in the middle, and divides into two parts,) Tj T*
