(may be considered for the present as standing apart in a class by) Tj T*
(themselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=344. Numbers and variety.=?Fungi exceed every other class of) Tj T*
(living organisms both in the number of species and of individuals) Tj T*
(composing them. They include such diverse forms as bacteria, molds,) Tj T*
(rusts, mildews, mushrooms, and the like, ranging in size all the way) Tj T*
(from the giant puffball, a foot or more in diameter, to the almost) Tj T*
(inconceivably minute influenza bacillus, of which nearly two thousand) Tj T*
(million can inhabit a single drop of water without inconvenient) Tj T*
(crowding!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 433.?Cephalothecium, a fungus parasitic on) Tj T*
(rosehips?greatly magnified. \(_From_ Mo. Botanical Garden Rep?t.) Tj T*
(Photographed by Hedgcock.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=345. The parasitic habit.=?But while their life history is obscure) Tj T*
(and hard to trace, the fungi are, as a class, well differentiated by) Tj T*
(their parasitic habit. They contain no chlorophyll, can manufacture) Tj T*
(no food, and consequently have to obtain it ready-made from the) Tj T*
(tissues of living or dead animals and plants. On this account they) Tj T*
(are active agents in the production of disease and decay, especially) Tj T*
(certain of those manifold forms that have been grouped together) Tj T*
(under the general head of bacteria. While not responsible for all) Tj T*
(the disease known to be caused by living organisms,?some very) Tj T*
(serious ones, such as malaria and cattle fever, being due to animal) Tj T*
(parasites,?the majority of those that have been most carefully) Tj T*
(investigated are traced to the bacteria, or other fungi. After any) Tj T*
(of these parasites have found a lodgment in the body of an organism) Tj T*
(whose tissues furnish them a congenial habitat, they multiply with) Tj T*
(enormous rapidity, and through the action of certain poisons called) Tj T*
(_toxins_, which they excrete, give rise to the most destructive) Tj T*
(diseases in both animals and plants; and no rational sanitary science) Tj T*
(is possible without a knowledge of their habits and life history. Add) Tj T*
(to the vast amount of human suffering that is to be laid at their) Tj T*
(door the economic damage done by rust and smut fungi, by molds and) Tj T*
(blights and mildews, and we shall be tempted to conclude that the) Tj T*
(?battle of life? is largely a struggle against these invisible foes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
