(          A. BACTERIA) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  MATERIAL.?A vessel of water in which hay has been left to soak for) Tj T*
(  several hours; a freshly boiled potato.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  APPLIANCES.?A double boiler for sterilizing; a number of clean) Tj T*
(  glass jars and bottles; cotton wool for stoppers; a compound) Tj T*
(  microscope.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  CULTURE MEDIUMS.?A freshly boiled potato answers very well for) Tj T*
(  ordinary purposes. ?Bread mash? can be made by drying some bread) Tj T*
(  crumbs in an oven, then mashing and mixing them to a paste with) Tj T*
(  boiling water; sterilize by three successive heatings in a double) Tj T*
(  boiler. A sterilized preparation of gelatine solution is the medium) Tj T*
(  most commonly used.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=347. How to obtain specimens for observation.=?While bacteria are) Tj T*
(plentiful almost everywhere, it is not always easy to capture them) Tj T*
(just when and where you want them. For this purpose, put some hay) Tj T*
(in water and leave in a warm place away from the light until the) Tj T*
(liquid becomes cloudy or a film forms on the surface. This will show) Tj T*
(that bacteria are present. If it is desired to study any particular) Tj T*
(kind of bacterium, inoculate one of the culture mediums described) Tj T*
(under ?material,? or a few drops of sterilized extract of beef, with) Tj T*
(a small quantity of the substance to be examined, or with dust or) Tj T*
(scrapings from the locality under consideration.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  EXPERIMENT 93. BY WHAT MEANS ARE BACTERIA COMMONLY DISTRIBUTED??Put) Tj T*
(  a slice of freshly boiled potato into each of three glass tumblers) Tj T*
