(=351. Reproduction and multiplication.=?The ordinary mode of) Tj T*
(reproduction in bacteria, as in other unicellular organisms, is by) Tj T*
(fission \(337, 338\). As each individual forms but a single spore, no) Tj T*
(increase in numbers could take place by this means alone. Hence,) Tj T*
(while the spores are an important factor in the preservation of) Tj T*
(the species by continuing its existence under conditions which the) Tj T*
(active organisms could not survive, their successful propagation) Tj T*
(depends on their power of rapid multiplication by division. If) Tj T*
(this process were to go on unchecked, every hour, in an unbroken) Tj T*
(geometrical progression, the progeny of a single bacterium would,) Tj T*
(in 24 hours, number nearly 17 million; in 25 hours, 34 million; in) Tj T*
(26 hours, 68 million, and in five days they would cover the entire) Tj T*
(surface of the globe, land and sea, to a depth of 3 feet! In ordinary) Tj T*
(standard milk sold by dairymen, and containing, when examined, less) Tj T*
(than 10,000 microbes to the cubic centimeter,?about 20 drops,?the) Tj T*
(number was found to have increased after 24 hours to 600 million.) Tj T*
(It is comforting to know, however, that the majority of these are) Tj T*
(of the harmless kinds which are the active agents in the making of) Tj T*
(buttermilk and cheese.) Tj T*
