(=359. Red rust.=?Uredo stage. Examine a leaf of ?red rusted? wheat) Tj T*
(under the lens, and notice the little oblong brown dots that cover) Tj T*
(it. These are clusters of spore cases, and are the only part that) Tj T*
(appears above the surface. Viewed under the microscope, the red rust) Tj T*
(will be seen to consist of a mycelium \(see Fig. 452\), which ramifies) Tj T*
(through the tissues of the leaf and bears clusters of single-celled) Tj T*
(reddish spores that break through the epidermis and form the reddish) Tj T*
(brown spots and streaks from which the disease takes its name. These) Tj T*
(spores, falling upon other leaves, germinate in a few hours and form) Tj T*
(new mycelia, from which, in six to ten days, fresh spores arise.) Tj T*
(Formerly this was thought to complete the life history of the fungus,) Tj T*
(to which the name of _Uredo_ was given. It is now known, however,) Tj T*
(that the red rust is merely a stage in the life cycle of the plant,) Tj T*
(and to this stage the old name uredo is applied, the spores being) Tj T*
(called _uredospores_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=360. Black rust.=?Teleuto stage. Next examine with a lens a part) Tj T*
(of the plant attacked by black rust. Do you observe any difference) Tj T*
(except in the color? Do the two kinds of rust attack all parts) Tj T*
(of the plant equally? If not, what part does each seem to affect) Tj T*
(more particularly? At what season does the black rust appear most) Tj T*
(abundantly? Place a section of the diseased part under the microscope) Tj T*
(and notice that the difference in color is due to a preponderance) Tj T*
(of long, two-celled bodies with very thick, black walls \(Fig. 453\).) Tj T*
(These are called _teleutospores_, a word meaning ?final spores,?) Tj T*
(because they are formed only toward the end of the season. They are) Tj T*
(developed from the same mycelium with the uredospores, and are not) Tj T*
