(=375. The hymenium.=?Cut a tangential section through one side of) Tj T*
(the pileus and sketch the section of the gills as they appear under) Tj T*
(a lens, or a low power of the microscope. Notice that the blade) Tj T*
(consists of a central portion called the _trama_ \(_tr_, Fig. 462\)) Tj T*
(and a somewhat thickened portion, _h_, constituting the _hymenium_,) Tj T*
(or spore-bearing surface. Now examine, under a high power, a small) Tj T*
(section from the edge of a gill, including a bit of the trama.) Tj T*
(Notice that this last consists of a tissue of mycelial cells \(Fig.) Tj T*
(463\) covered by the hymenium, or spore-bearing membrane, which is) Tj T*
(thickly clothed with a layer of elongated, club-shaped cells \(_b_,) Tj T*
(_b_ and _p_, _p_, Fig. 463\) set upon it at right angles to the) Tj T*
(surface. Some of these put out from two to four, or in some species) Tj T*
(as many as eight, little prongs, each bearing a spore \(_s_, _s_,) Tj T*
(Fig. 463\), while others remain sterile. The spore-bearing cells) Tj T*
(are called _basidia_; the sterile ones, _paraphyses_; and the) Tj T*
(whole spore-bearing surface together, the _hymenium_, from a Greek) Tj T*
(word meaning a membrane. It is from the presence of this expanded) Tj T*
(fruiting membrane that the class of mushrooms we are considering gets) Tj T*
(its botanical name, _Hymenomycetes_, membrane fungi. The hymenium) Tj T*
(is not always borne on gills, but is arranged in various ways which) Tj T*
(serve as a convenient basis for distinguishing the different orders.) Tj T*
(In the tube fungi, to which the edible boletus belongs \(Figs. 464,) Tj T*
(465\), the basidia are placed along the inside of little tubes that) Tj T*
(line the under side of the pileus, giving it the appearance of a) Tj T*
(honeycomb. In another order, the porcupine fungi, they are arranged) Tj T*
(on the outside of projecting spines or teeth, while in the morelles) Tj T*
(they are held in little cups or basins.) Tj T*
