(=379. Examination of a typical specimen.=?The commonest kind of) Tj T*
(lichens, and generally the most easily obtained, are those that) Tj T*
(grow on rocks and tree trunks in flat, spreading patches. Their) Tj T*
(margins are much dented and curled, giving them a somewhat leaflike) Tj T*
(appearance, whence they are called ?foliaceous? lichens. This broad,) Tj T*
(expanded body is the thallus, or vegetative part, as distinguished) Tj T*
(from its reproductive part. Examine carefully the thallus of your) Tj T*
(specimen. Note the size and shape of the indentations. Is there any) Tj T*
(order or regularity about them, such as was observed in the lobing of) Tj T*
(leaves? Is there any difference in color between the upper and under) Tj T*
(sides? What other differences do you notice? Do you see anything) Tj T*
(like hairs, or rootlets, on the under side? Mount one of them in) Tj T*
(water and place under the microscope. What does it look like? Compare) Tj T*
(with one of the hairs from a leaf of mullein, gromwell, blueweed, or) Tj T*
(other hairy plant, with the hypha of a fungus mycelium, and with your) Tj T*
(study of the root hair in 67 \(_a_\). Is it a hair or a root? These) Tj T*
(rootlike hairs are called _rhizoids_, and serve to anchor the lichen) Tj T*
(to its substratum. Look on the upper side for little cup-shaped or) Tj T*
(saucer-shaped receptacles. On what part of the thallus are they) Tj T*
(situated? Examine with a lens and see if you can make out what they) Tj T*
(contain. These cups are the spore cases. The lichen fungus belongs to) Tj T*
(the division of sac fungi, which produce their spores in closed sacs,) Tj T*
(or cups.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 470.?Portion of the thallus of a lichen,) Tj T*
(magnified, showing imprisoned algæ.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=380. Structure of the thallus.=?Make a thin section through a) Tj T*
(thallus and place under the microscope. Notice the small green bodies) Tj T*
(enveloped in the hyphæ of the fungus. Are they most abundant near the) Tj T*
(upper or the lower epidermis? Has their green color anything to do) Tj T*
(with this, and with the difference in color between the two surfaces) Tj T*
(of the thallus? \(184.\) Do they look like chlorophyll granules? Can) Tj T*
(you tell what they are? Compare with your study of the unicellular) Tj T*
(algæ \(337\) and with Fig. 429. Does this throw any light on their real) Tj T*
(nature?) Tj T*
