(=381. The lichen thallus a composite body.=?You will probably have no) Tj T*
(difficulty in making out that these small round bodies are green algæ) Tj T*
(of some kind, but of what species will depend upon the kind of lichen) Tj T*
(with which it is associated. In Cladonia and the bearded lichen) Tj T*
(\(Fig. 473\), it is a protococcus; in other forms, a pleurococcus or a) Tj T*
(nostoc?and so on, each species of lichen fungus being specialized to) Tj T*
(a certain form of alga. The great botanist, De Bary, showed that it) Tj T*
(is even possible to produce a lichen thallus artificially by sowing) Tj T*
(the spores of a fungus among the cells of the particular alga with) Tj T*
(which it is able to unite. The spores will germinate without the) Tj T*
(alga, but soon perish unless they come in contact with the right) Tj T*
(one. It is thus made clear that the lichen plant as a whole is a) Tj T*
(combination of elements belonging to two distinct orders, the algæ) Tj T*
(and fungi, but so closely associated as to constitute practically a) Tj T*
(single individual.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=382. Slavery, or partnership?=?Now, what can be the object of this) Tj T*
(peculiar association? Is it a symbiosis, or a case of enslavement?) Tj T*
(The fungi, as we know, are all parasites, unable to manufacture) Tj T*
(their own food or to exist at all except at the expense of other) Tj T*
(organisms, living or dead. But the lichens have refined upon the) Tj T*
(gross rapacity of their order, and instead of indiscriminately) Tj T*
(destroying the hosts that furnish their nourishment, have used their) Tj T*
(victims to better purpose by converting them into contented, well-fed) Tj T*
(slaves! The imprisoned algæ perform for them the same service that) Tj T*
(the chlorophyll bodies do for the higher plants, and so the lichen) Tj T*
(fungi have the advantage of other parasites in getting their food) Tj T*
(manufactured at home, so to speak. And while the algæ have to do) Tj T*
(double work in order to feed both themselves and their masters,) Tj T*
(the fungus, in return, shelters them against cold and drought, and) Tj T*
(prolongs their growing period by giving them a more continuous supply) Tj T*
(of moisture and food materials, which it draws from the substratum by) Tj T*
(means of its rhizoids. In this way both plants are enabled to live in) Tj T*
(situations that neither could occupy without the other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 472.?A crustaceous lichen \(_Graphis elegans_\)) Tj T*
(growing on holly: _A_, natural size; _B_, slightly magnified.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=383. Reproduction.=?The multiplication of the lichen algæ is) Tj T*
(exclusively vegetative. The fungus, on the other hand, reproduces) Tj T*
(normally by spores, and the fruiting bodies found on the thallus) Tj T*
(originate from the fungus mycelium.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=384. Classification.=?To be strictly accurate, the two kinds of) Tj T*
(vegetable bodies that make up the lichen thallus would probably have) Tj T*
(to be classified separately, as algæ or fungi, respectively, but as) Tj T*
(fructification is the generally accepted basis of classification,) Tj T*
(and the plant body is too intimately permeated with both kinds of) Tj T*
(tissue to be divided, each lichen body as a whole is classed with) Tj T*
(its particular kind of fungus. The entire group, on account of the) Tj T*
