(plants? \(250, 251.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=393. The spore case.=?After fertilization the egg becomes an) Tj T*
(_oöspore_, capable of producing a new plant. Instead, however, of) Tj T*
(separating from the mother plant and giving rise to an independent) Tj T*
(growth, it germinates within the archegonium and produces there a) Tj T*
(small, stalked body, called a _sporogonium_, or _sporophyte_, which) Tj T*
(at length ripens into a spore case, as shown at _f_, Fig. 479. At) Tj T*
(maturity this capsule-like sporophyte ruptures at the apex, and) Tj T*
(discharges a mass of spores, mingled with elongated filaments) Tj T*
(called _elators_, which, by their elastic movements, assist in) Tj T*
(disseminating the spores. These latter, on germinating, produce,) Tj T*
(not a simple sporophyte like that which bore them, but the thallus) Tj T*
(of the liverwort with all its complicated arrangement of antheridia) Tj T*
(and archegonia and vegetative organs that seem to foreshadow, by the) Tj T*
(analogies they suggest, the coming of the higher plants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=394. Sexual and asexual reproduction.=?We find here a very marked) Tj T*
(change from the simple reproductive processes observed in the algæ) Tj T*
(and fungi. In the forms thus far considered, this function was) Tj T*
(carried on mainly by simple vegetative fission or budding, with a) Tj T*
(more or less irregular intervention of resting spores. If only one) Tj T*
(kind of spore is concerned, reproduction is said to be _asexual_.) Tj T*
