(=396. The protonema or thallus stage.=?In mosses the sexual, or) Tj T*
(gametophyte generation differs from that of liverworts in undergoing) Tj T*
(two phases. The germinating cells of the sporophyte do not develop) Tj T*
(immediately into the leafy stem, which is the typical gametophyte) Tj T*
(of true mosses, but produce first a filamentous, creeping structure) Tj T*
(called the _protonema_ \(Fig. 483\), that spreads over the ground) Tj T*
(and forms the tangled green felt usually observed where mosses are) Tj T*
(growing. Place a few of these filaments on a slide in water, and) Tj T*
(examine under the microscope. Do they remind you of any of the) Tj T*
(forms of algæ? Look near the base of the branches for knots or) Tj T*
(enlargements, like those seen at _kn_, Fig. 483. These are buds) Tj T*
(from which the leafy moss stems will develop. Do they correspond) Tj T*
(to anything observed among the thallophytes? Notice the rootlike) Tj T*
(filaments that extend under ground; how do they differ from the ones) Tj T*
(above ground? Why are they colorless? How do you know that they are) Tj T*
(not true roots? [67 \(_a_\), 379.] Sketch one of each kind of filament) Tj T*
(sufficiently enlarged to show the cells composing it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A protonema that arises directly from the spore is said to be) Tj T*
(_primary_, while those which sometimes spring from rhizoids above) Tj T*
(ground, or from stems or leaves, are _secondary_. The fact that a) Tj T*
(protonema can bud from parts of the fruiting stems shows that the two) Tj T*
(do not belong to different generations, but are merely successive) Tj T*
(stages of a single generation, and both together compose the) Tj T*
(gametophyte.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=397. The leafy stage.=?In their fully developed state the true) Tj T*
(mosses show a marked advance in organization over the liverworts.) Tj T*
(There is a distinct differentiation of the growing axis into stem) Tj T*
(and leaves, though no true roots are formed. The leaves are arranged) Tj T*
