(=416. Significance of the sporophyte.=?The gametophyte is obviously a) Tj T*
(more ancient and primitive structure than the sporophyte, which first) Tj T*
(becomes prominent in the ferns and their allies. The sudden and) Tj T*
(violent break in the succession of vegetable life that accompanies) Tj T*
(the appearance of the pteridophytes \(412\) is probably to be explained) Tj T*
(by the development of a land flora and the necessity of adaptation to) Tj T*
(life in a new medium. The fact that no living cell, whether vegetable) Tj T*
(or animal, can absorb nourishment except in a liquid form, seems to) Tj T*
(point to an aquatic origin more or less remote for all life. This) Tj T*
(inference is further strengthened, in the case of plants, by the) Tj T*
(fact that even in so highly organized a group as the pteridophytes,) Tj T*
(fertilization cannot take place except in water. Such a requirement) Tj T*
(would manifestly be a great disadvantage to land plants, and one of) Tj T*
(the first steps in response to the demands of a new habitat would be) Tj T*
(to get rid, as far as possible, of the primitive gametophyte with) Tj T*
(its outgrown adaptations to a liquid medium, and to transfer the) Tj T*
(greater part of the work of reproduction to the asexual generation,) Tj T*
(in which the problem of fertilization did not have to be directly) Tj T*
(met, the asexual spores germinating without it. The greater the) Tj T*
(number of these produced, the better the chance that at least some of) Tj T*
(the gametes developed from them would meet the difficult conditions) Tj T*
(of fertilization, and the survival of the species be assured. At the) Tj T*
(same time, in order to meet the requirements of terrestrial life) Tj T*
(successfully, and to provide for continuing the sexual generation,) Tj T*
(correlative changes would have to take place in the gametophyte by) Tj T*
(which the increasing uncertainty of fertilization due to structural) Tj T*
(changes in the sporophyte, and the absence of a liquid medium for) Tj T*
(the conveyance of free swimming antherozoids would be avoided. This) Tj T*
(necessity has been met by the development of the pollen tube, which) Tj T*
(bores its way to the egg cell, carrying with it the generative cells,) Tj T*
(which in seed plants have taken the place of the more primitive) Tj T*
(antherozoids. With the concomitant reduction of the gametophyte and) Tj T*
(development of the seed habit, the adaptation to land conditions has) Tj T*
(been made complete.) Tj T*
