(aquatic dicotyl, and represent, not an ancient and primitive stock,) Tj T*
(but a case of retrogressive evolution from a higher type. Strong) Tj T*
(presumptions in favor of this view are: \(1\) that various species of) Tj T*
(dicotyls show an unequal development of the seed leaves, amounting,) Tj T*
(in the bryony, to complete abortion of one of them, while some) Tj T*
(monocotyl seeds show morphological characters that can best be) Tj T*
(explained as survivals, or inheritances, from a dicotyl ancestor;) Tj T*
(\(2\) the structural resemblances between gymnosperms and dicotyls are) Tj T*
(closer than between gymnosperms and monocotyls, which could hardly) Tj T*
(be the case if the latter were the more ancient; \(3\) the geological) Tj T*
(record does not show them to have appeared before dicotyls; \(4\) the) Tj T*
(number of cotyledons furnishes no criterion as to the relative age) Tj T*
(of any plant group, since all three types are represented among the) Tj T*
(pteridophytes, where plants are found bearing one, two, or more) Tj T*
(cotyledons.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The theory of their comparatively recent origin from an aquatic) Tj T*
(ancestor is further borne out by the many points of similarity) Tj T*
(between their internal structure and that of hydrophytes \(318\), and) Tj T*
(also by the great proportion of aquatic plants among them, amounting) Tj T*
(to thirty-three per cent, while in dicotyls the proportion is only) Tj T*
(four per cent. Can you give any reasons, from your examination of) Tj T*
(their internal structure \(113, 114\), for believing that the line of) Tj T*
(development which they have followed is a less effective one for) Tj T*
